00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.330
connection with nature, connection to other people,

268
00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:28.400
and the like.

269
00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.440
But let's kind of approach this

270
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.570
from a neuroscience perspective.

271
00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.160
Like how are you thinking about this

272
00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.460
from a mechanistic point of view?

273
00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:38.402
Yeah.

274
00:10:38.402 --> 00:10:39.620
So a lot of people are struggling with focus

275
00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.373
and I'll just point out

276
00:10:41.373 --> 00:10:43.330
that there is a fair amount

277
00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:46.800
of clinically diagnosed ADHD out there.

278
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.040
I think the current numbers are somewhere between 10 and 11%

279
00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.950
of young folks are now diagnosed with ADHD.

280
00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.290
And I think that's probably accurate.

281
00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:57.290
I don't know whether or not

282
00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.330
the numbers are going up with social media.

283
00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:01.520
There's a lot of speculation about whether or not things

284
00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.310
like ADHD and eating disorders

285
00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:04.810
are increasing with socia media,

286
00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:06.130
or whether or not they've been constant

287
00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.950
in our ability to detect them is just getting better.

288
00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:10.160
So that's an ongoing debate,

289
00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.250
but the numbers are about 10 to 11%.

290
00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.440
First of all, a couple,

291
00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:19.500
I mean sort of pseudo myths to eliminate.

292
00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.180
The first thing is that we are very good

293
00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:23.720
at splitting our attention.

294
00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:25.580
Humans can multitask.

295
00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.870
All old world primates have what's called covert attention.

296
00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:30.740
I can look at you and have a conversation with you

297
00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.160
and I can attend to the glass,

298
00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.280
that's just to the right of my right hand.

299
00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.990
So I can split my attention into two cones.

300
00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.830
I can also bring one of those cones of attention internally,

301
00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.490
so I can pay attention to maybe

302
00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:44.190
how fast my heart is beating right now

303
00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:45.240
while I'm talking to you.

304
00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.700
So it's a complete myth that we can't multitask.

305
00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.340
We absolutely can multitask.

306
00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:50.940
It's much harder to split our attention

307
00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.530
into three cones of attention or four cones of attention,

308
00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:55.200
but one thing that's very clear,

309
00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.797
it's very well grounded in neuroscience

310
00:11:56.797 --> 00:11:59.590
and this concept of covert detention

311
00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:01.030
is that it has utility, right?

312
00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:04.037
Covert detention has been thought to be useful

313
00:12:04.037 --> 00:12:07.150
in the context of monitoring social circumstances.

314
00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.860
So the typical neuroscience lecture form of this

315
00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.180
is that monkeys in a big troop

316
00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.330
would need to pay attention to who the dominant

317
00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.630
and subordinate males and females are,

318
00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:19.540
and govern their behavior accordingly.

319
00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.310
In other words, pay attention to what's going around you,

320
00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:23.150
going on around you,

321
00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.030
not just to what you want to do and how the person or monkey

322
00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:27.490
in front of you is behaving.

323
00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:28.628
And humans do this too.

324
00:12:28.628 --> 00:12:31.210
The common example that's used in neuroscience lectures

325
00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:35.300
is a couple on a date out to dinner

326
00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.526
and someone's attending to somebody else in the corner

327
00:12:37.526 --> 00:12:38.470
that might have been a better date

328
00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.890
or something or paying attention to that conversation.

329
00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:41.860
Yeah, it's totally possible.

330
00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.250
So we can split our attention.

331
00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.710
But there's gotta be a distinction

332
00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:50.480
between what your primary focus is lasered in on

333
00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:54.694
versus whatever that kind of background multitask is, right?

334
00:12:54.694 --> 00:12:58.324
Like you couldn't like write two essays

335
00:12:58.324 --> 00:12:59.835
with each hand at the same time.

336
00:12:59.835 --> 00:13:00.668
That's right.

337
00:13:00.668 --> 00:13:02.280
And so that highlights a really important point,

338
00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.130
which is that you can wait these cones of attention

339
00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.340
and you can bring them together.

340
00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.940
So the best way to think about 'em is two spotlights,

341
00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:10.180
and you can bring both spotlights

342
00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:13.700
to one common area of focus and get a deeper,

343
00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:14.960
depth of focus if you will,

344
00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.810
deeper depth sort of redundant there,

345
00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:20.575
but you can get a more heightened state of attention

346
00:13:20.575 --> 00:13:23.780
or focus by bringing those together.

347
00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:26.730
Likewise, if I close my eyes and focus in entirely

348
00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:27.760
on my internal state,

349
00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:29.620
something we called interoception

350
00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:31.380
to distinguish it from exteroception,

351
00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.100
which is the view of the outside world.

352
00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.040
You can pay attention to your heart beat,

353
00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:36.180
to your breathing,

354
00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:38.390
this is common to many meditative practices.

355
00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:41.240
So we can split this thing that we call focus or attention

356
00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:45.550
and we can wait this thing that we call focus and attention.

357
00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:50.550
Now in ADHD, one of the less appreciated aspects

358
00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:52.790
of the clinical phenotype,

359
00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.190
meaning that the sorts of symptoms that show up,

360
00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:00.150
it's commonly thought that people with ADHD cannot focus,

361
00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.740
but that's simply not true.

362
00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.440
If they are engaged in something

363
00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.350
they really care about or like,

364
00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.200
they can focus very intensely.

365
00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.100
And that is because much of focus

366
00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:11.700
relates to the dopamine system,

367
00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.070
this neuromodulator/neurotransmitter that...

368
00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:17.672
I know you had Anna Lamche, Mike gone here,

369
00:14:17.672 --> 00:14:21.370
the neuromodulator dopamine plays a very active role

370
00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.730
in motivation, craving and pursuit.

371
00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.120
It's commonly thought that it's only associated with reward

372
00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.200
and feel good type of behaviors or phenomenon,

373
00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:32.640
but it's actually associated with pursuit of things

374
00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.258
that at least we think are gonna make us feel good

375
00:14:34.258 --> 00:14:35.870
in the short term.

376
00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:40.180
So to kind of a generic currency for pursuit and motivation.

377
00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:43.950
When we focus and when we are excited about something,

378
00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.550
there is a lot of activity

379
00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.940
in these so-called dopaminegic circuits.

380
00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:49.840
And so it's not a surprise

381
00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:53.740
in that many of the prescription medications for ADHD

382
00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:57.190
are medications that amplify the amount of dopamine

383
00:14:57.190 --> 00:14:58.220
that's available on the brain

384
00:14:58.220 --> 00:14:59.580
through one mechanism or another.

385
00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.440
So these would be your Ritalins, your Adderalls.

386
00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.290
And then nowadays there's also a lot of prescribing

387
00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:06.160
of things like Modafinil,

388
00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.770
which is yet another category of drugs

389
00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:10.970
used to treat ADHD slightly different,

390
00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.460
and then in the kind of supplementation world,

391
00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:15.230
people delve into things like L-Tyrosine,

392
00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:19.130
which is the precursor to dopamine.

393
00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:20.750
So there are a variety of ways

394
00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:22.720
to access the dopamine system,

395
00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.560
but the way that the dopamine system

396
00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.186
was designed to enhance focus

397
00:15:26.186 --> 00:15:28.900
is that when you are excited about something

398
00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.780
or you really want to attend to something,

399
00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.920
it literally brings about a narrowing of the aperture

400
00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.890
of your visual window and your auditory window,

401
00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.640
so that you attend to this particular location in space.

402
00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.800
And then events within that location in space

403
00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:43.370
for what you see, and what you hear,

404
00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.310
movements of whatever you're attending to

405
00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:48.140
become extremely important and extremely relevant.

406
00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:50.810
And so it just highlights this larger theme

407
00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.130
of how the brain and attends and unattend,

408
00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:56.630
which is that we have an aperture or a window on our focus.

409
00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:58.790
And dopamine seems to narrow that,

410
00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:01.590
literally any drug, like any stimulant

411
00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:03.470
like cocaine or amphetamine at the extreme,

412
00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:07.260
but even caffeine will dilate the pupils causing that,

413
00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.220
somewhat paradoxically

414
00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:11.600
causes a narrowing of the visual window

415
00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.610
and your ability to focus is enhanced.

416
00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:16.710
And that brings about the perhaps the most important point,

417
00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:17.870
which is that for most people

418
00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.420
provided that they are sighted,

419
00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:24.683
provided they can see, mental focus follows visual focus.

420
00:16:24.683 --> 00:16:26.000
And for that reason,

421
00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.250
there's a lot of exciting research being done in classrooms

422
00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.840
and in clinical settings, training children,

423
00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:32.900
into some extent adults,

424
00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.980
but training children to anchor their visual focus

425
00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:39.140
to maybe a crosshatch or a fixation point,

426
00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.740
and just learn how to keep their visual attention

427
00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.510
on a narrow aperture.

428
00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:45.560
It's essentially training these...

429
00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.900
It turns out before brain circuit,

430
00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.110
so the front of the brain right behind the forehead

431
00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.040
and some of these dopamine circuits deeper in the brain.

432
00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.160
It's training those to engage

433
00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.500
whenever the person deliberately wants to engage them.

434
00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.507
So for most people, they hear this and they go,

435
00:16:59.507 --> 00:17:02.230
"Oh, of course, mental focus follows visual focus,"

436
00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:03.063
and yet,

437
00:17:03.063 --> 00:17:05.520
if you think about the way our world is arranged now,

438
00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:08.154
with your phone calling you over to this location

439
00:17:08.154 --> 00:17:10.980
and your computer calling you to that location,

440
00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.160
and whatever's happening in your immediate environment

441
00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:14.170
calling to that location,

442
00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:15.860
and things that are happening within your body,

443
00:17:15.860 --> 00:17:16.693
it makes perfect sense

444
00:17:16.693 --> 00:17:18.450
if you only have two cones of attention

445
00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.810
and they're moving around like those lights that advertise

446
00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.970
or used to be used to advertise some event in a city,

447
00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.810
shining up in the sky, moving all over the place,

448
00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.467
you realize that,

449
00:17:29.467 --> 00:17:32.760
"Oh, it makes perfect sense why focus is hard."

450
00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.610
We are now realizing that we need to try train up focus,

451
00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.040
and whereas in the past,

452
00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.650
when we weren't so inundated with devices and distraction,

453
00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:41.640
that was probably easier to do.

454
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.510
We didn't need such rigid blinders,

455
00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:45.906
but in fact, we do need blinders.

456
00:17:45.906 --> 00:17:48.170
And many people actually benefit

457
00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.820
from things like putting on a hoodie or wearing a hat,

458
00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.240
actually limiting their visual window

459
00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:54.930
and restricting their visual window

460
00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:57.360
can be very helpful for people to learn

461
00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.704
how to use visual focus to anchor cognitive focus.

462
00:17:59.704 --> 00:18:01.740
Yeah. That makes a lot of sense.

463
00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.290
I mean, I'm just thinking about,

464
00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:05.810
let me throw this at you.

465
00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:08.463
If I'm sitting down and I'm trying to write,

466
00:18:09.856 --> 00:18:11.410
I can't focus on two things.

467
00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.144
I have to focus on that one specific thing,

468
00:18:13.144 --> 00:18:17.580
my vision is laser focused on that one thing

469
00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.320
and I become very intolerant

470
00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:22.260
to any kind of external stimuli.

471
00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:23.560
I feel very fragile.

472
00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.940
A notification can throw me off

473
00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:28.100
and there's the switch cost effect of that,

474
00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.600
versus, let's say I'm out on my bike on a training ride

475
00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:35.000
and I'm listening to an audio book or a podcast,

476
00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.740
I can do both of those things simultaneously,

477
00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:38.950
and I would contend,

478
00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:41.030
and I'm interested in your perspective on this.

479
00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.610
I would contend that my memory retention

480
00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:45.260
of what I'm listening to

481
00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:48.210
is actually better when I'm out doing that

482
00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:52.470
even though I'm focused on the exertion

483
00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:54.550
of running on a trail, riding my bike,

484
00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.480
something about the elevated heart rate

485
00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:00.070
and the kind of controlled labored breath.

486
00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:03.060
For some reason, that's a better combination

487
00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:04.820
than if I was just driving around in my car,

488
00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:06.090
trying to listen to it.

489
00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:07.840
Yeah. It's exactly right.

490
00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.470
I mean, what we're really getting to here

491
00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.540
is this notion of brain states.

492
00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:14.350
And I imagine this is something we might get into

493
00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:16.040
repeated times throughout our conversation,

494
00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:17.120
which is that just;

495
00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.090
as just in sleep we have states of mind

496
00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:20.860
that relate to dreaming or slow wave sleep,

497
00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.760
or rapid eye movement sleep,

498
00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.003
in our waking states,

499
00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:26.110
our waking period, I should say,

500
00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:28.290
we transition through various states.

501
00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:30.300
And for some of us,

502
00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:35.300
there are conditions in which we have so much activation

503
00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.740
in our body, what we call autonomic arousal,

504
00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.370
but so much energy, basically,

505
00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:42.620
that just sitting down to write at the computer,

506
00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:44.090
you would think, "Oh, well, this is very hard,

507
00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:45.920
it should anchor all of my attention and energy

508
00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.730
into this narrow cone of attention."

509
00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:51.560
But the fact that you also have to sit still,

510
00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:54.350
the fact that there's actually not just what's right

511
00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:55.183
in front of you,

512
00:19:55.183 --> 00:19:56.710
but there's also the internal dialogue

513
00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:57.607
that you're carrying about,

514
00:19:57.607 --> 00:19:59.070
"What's gonna happen with this passage?

515
00:19:59.070 --> 00:19:59.930
Is this any good?

516
00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:01.270
What someone gonna think?

517
00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:03.130
Is a notification gonna go off in a moment?"

518
00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:04.560
There are multiple scripts running.

519
00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.010
And that's why we distinguish between this exteroception,

520
00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:08.880
which is the perception of things in the outside world

521
00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.120
and interoception,

522
00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:11.770
which is the perception of things

523
00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.770
within the confines of our skin.

524
00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:14.850
And when we sit down to write,

525
00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.140
we might think that it's just one thing in front of us,

526
00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:19.620
but actually we're paying attention to internal events

527
00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:20.610
and dialogues as well.

528
00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:23.030
So we're splitting our attention in multiple ways.

529
00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:24.750
When I can only speculate,

530
00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.920
but I can imagine that when you're out for a ride,

531
00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:29.910
a good amount of that excess autonomic arousal

532
00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:32.530
is consumed by the pedaling and the breathing

533
00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:34.770
that puts your brain into a kind of a rhythmic state.

534
00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.790
And we know that breathing,

535
00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.190
we know based on a lot of good data

536
00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:41.770
that breathing sets a condition in the brain

537
00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:43.710
for certain brain states to arise.

538
00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.200
And this is very easily experienced

539
00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:47.960
where if you slow down your breathing

540
00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:51.570
and commit to doing maybe two or three breaths per minute,

541
00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.890
you'll notice that your brain and your entire body

542
00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:56.270
will shift into probably a more relaxed state

543
00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.700
for most people.

544
00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.160
And if you were to commit

545
00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.860
to doing 15 or 30 breaths per minute,

546
00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:05.210
your brain and body would shift into a more aroused state.

547
00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:06.270
So over time,

548
00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.750
I think almost everybody figures out

549
00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:09.850
the things they need to do,

550
00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:11.930
how much coffee, how much movement,

551
00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.190
whether or not you work before or after your ride,

552
00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:15.760
what sorts of information you consume

553
00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:17.350
and what types of work one does

554
00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:19.600
in order to try and optimize this process.

555
00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.380
But a lot of what my laboratory is doing at Stanford

556
00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:23.830
and what other laboratories are doing elsewhere

557
00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:26.120
is to try and figure out, what are these brain states?

558
00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:27.330
How do we define them?

559
00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.330
What really is a state of focus

560
00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.020
and how do we arrive at a state of focus if we're tired?

561
00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:34.480
How do we arrive at a state of focus

562
00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.594
if we are kind of over-agitated?

563
00:21:36.594 --> 00:21:39.720
And so beneath all brain states,

564
00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.240
including our ability to focus is this,

565
00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:43.073
I like to visualize

566
00:21:43.073 --> 00:21:45.950
as sort of a seesaw of autonomic arousal.

567
00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:48.260
We can either be relaxed and alert,

568
00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:49.430
which is kind of the optimal state

569
00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:51.130
for most things besides sleep.

570
00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.690
We can be very alert,

571
00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:55.500
we can be panicked, or we can be very, very calm,

572
00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:56.333
or we can be asleep.

573
00:21:56.333 --> 00:22:00.780
So on one end you have calming type experiences or states,

574
00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.150
and on the other end you have kind of amplified states.

575
00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:07.370
If you are feeling really upset about something,

576
00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:09.370
it's gonna be hard for you to focus

577
00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:10.450
whether or not it's at the computer,

578
00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:11.730
or whether or not it's out on your ride

579
00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.420
while listening to this podcast

580
00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.100
or whatever it happens to be.

581
00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:16.600
So I think that in,

582
00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:17.670
again, I can only speculate,

583
00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:20.400
but I have to imagine that your ability to comprehend things

584
00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:22.110
and remember things when you're out on these rides

585
00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:23.730
is based on a number of things:

586
00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:25.270
It's the familiarity with the process.

587
00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:27.480
You're obviously very familiar with the bike.

588
00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:30.810
It's also probably a subconscious familiarity

589
00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:32.570
with a certain pace of riding

590
00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:34.550
that allows your brain to access the state

591
00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:38.600
in which you can digest information through audio.

592
00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.432
I should say one kind of interesting set of studies

593
00:22:40.432 --> 00:22:41.737
that's emerging now,

594
00:22:41.737 --> 00:22:43.660
and I can't really report back on the data yet,

595
00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:45.060
simply because we don't know,

596
00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:47.310
is that some people who have trouble with attention,

597
00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.250
especially while reading are finding it very beneficial

598
00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:51.740
to listen to audio books while actually reading the book.

599
00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.980
And I swear this is not an attempt

600
00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:55.420
to get people to both purchase the audio book

601
00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:56.950
and the written book.

602
00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:59.890
I don't even have a book to promote at this point.

603
00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:01.440
So that's not the purpose in this,

604
00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.240
but we definitely are a species

605
00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:06.860
that likes to combine our audio and visual world.

606
00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:09.010
In fact, the maps of our visual world

607
00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:10.890
and the maps of our audio world,

608
00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.270
or our audition really,

609
00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:16.980
and the maps of our motor world are in perfect register,

610
00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:18.710
such that if we hear sound off to our right,

611
00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:20.480
we turn to the right, not to the left.

612
00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:22.410
If we hear something right in front of us,

613
00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.160
meaning arrives at the two ears at the exact same time,

614
00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.450
and what we call the Interaural time difference is zero,

615
00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:28.990
well, then we look straight ahead.

616
00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:32.767
And so it makes sense that when we can bring the audio world

617
00:23:32.767 --> 00:23:35.000
and the visual world into alignment,

618
00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.000
our focus is always going to be greatest

619
00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:38.520
under those conditions.

620
00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:42.500
Some people learn better through their visual field

621
00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:44.540
than their auditory field, right?

622
00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:46.670
Like I know if I'm reading a book

623
00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.970
I'm in a better position to retain that information.

624
00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:52.100
When I recall something that I've read in a book,

625
00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:53.780
I see it on the page,

626
00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.830
and when I hear it, it's much more elusive.

627
00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.830
And I would assume that some people

628
00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.400
are the opposite of that.

629
00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.233
Yeah.

630
00:24:01.233 --> 00:24:03.120
Some people are much more auditory in nature.

631
00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:05.130
Most people are primarily visual.

632
00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:06.050
There's an interesting study

633
00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:08.890
that was published recently, just came out.

634
00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:13.890
And that shows that actually memory and recall for material

635
00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:16.570
that's consumed on a phone

636
00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.300
is much lower than on the written page.

637
00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:24.300
And there's an interesting reason that they give for this.

638
00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:25.670
There could be a lot of reasons.

639
00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:26.503
I just wanna point that out,

640
00:24:26.503 --> 00:24:27.980
but they monitored breathing,

641
00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:29.110
and they monitored heart rate,

642
00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:31.120
and they monitored some other physiological signals

643
00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:32.930
while people were either reading on a smartphone

644
00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:34.460
or reading paper.

645
00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.240
And what they found was that breathing

646
00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:39.140
was essentially normal or equivalent in both conditions.

647
00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:41.110
Meaning people breathe the same way,

648
00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:42.650
whether or not they're reading on their smartphone

649
00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:45.290
or reading from a written page.

650
00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:48.280
But there was a particular category of breath

651
00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:51.180
that was abolished by reading on the smartphone.

652
00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:54.200
And this is what is referred to as physiological sighs.

653
00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:57.050
I talk a lot about physiological sighs on the podcast

654
00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:58.210
and my lab works on them.

655
00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:01.030
This is a spontaneous innate pattern of breathing

656
00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:02.960
that's governed by a little subset of neurons

657
00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:03.960
in the brain stem,

658
00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.070
for those you who wanna know,

659
00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:07.620
this is a brain area called the parafacial nucleus,

660
00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:09.560
discovered by Jack Feldman at UCLA,

661
00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:12.100
who's the world expert in the neural mechanisms

662
00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:13.000
of respiration.

663
00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.820
And this low collection of neurons,

664
00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:17.820
probably only a hundred neurons or so,

665
00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.510
make sure that every three to five minutes

666
00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:22.500
you do what's called the physiological side,

667
00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:23.900
which is a very deep breath

668
00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:26.160
sometimes followed by another short inhale.

669
00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:26.993
So,

670
00:25:26.993 --> 00:25:29.140
(Huberman breaths deeply)

671
00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:31.410
and then a long exhale and the double deep breath

672
00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:33.300
with a very deep breath reinflates

673
00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:34.540
the little sacs in the lungs.

674
00:25:34.540 --> 00:25:35.990
Your lungs aren't just two big bags of air,

675
00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:38.440
they actually have hundreds of millions of little sacs

676
00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:40.330
that we call the aveoli of the lungs.

677
00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:43.680
And when you don't do these physiological sighs,

678
00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:45.020
so in the second or third minute

679
00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.260
after not having done a physiological sigh,

680
00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:48.980
these little sacs collapse,

681
00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:50.590
and they're kind of moist on the inside.

682
00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:52.560
So you can imagine trying to blow open a balloon

683
00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:53.530
that's moist on the inside.

684
00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:55.600
You need a little bit of added air pressure.

685
00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:57.590
And so the size reinflate those,

686
00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.670
and then on the exhale allow you to offload carbon dioxide.

687
00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:02.550
Now this happens even during sleep,

688
00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:04.080
every three to five minutes,

689
00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:06.130
and I've talked a lot about physiological sighs

690
00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:09.050
as what I think is really a terrific realtime tool

691
00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:12.250
for reducing anxiety and calming oneself down.

692
00:26:12.250 --> 00:26:13.280
Indeed, it works very well.

693
00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:15.350
We have a study that's out for review now,

694
00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:17.998
is a collaborative work with David Spiegel and psychiatry,

695
00:26:17.998 --> 00:26:20.390
but it points out that these physiological sighs

696
00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:21.850
can be used double inhale through the nose,

697
00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:24.020
long exhale through the mouth to calm down.

698
00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:26.720
And it works very well because you offload carbon dioxide.

699
00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:28.760
Now in this study about smartphone reading

700
00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:29.900
versus reading on paper

701
00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:31.970
what they found is that people stop engaging

702
00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:33.300
in physiological sighs

703
00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:36.880
anytime they're reading on the smartphone.

704
00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:38.860
Now it wasn't a perfect study

705
00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:41.240
because they probably also should had a tablet

706
00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:42.950
and a laptop condition.

707
00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:45.170
So it's not clear whether or not it's due only

708
00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:47.220
to the reduced aperture of the visual window

709
00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:48.490
that you're narrowing your attention,

710
00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:50.920
and there's this sort of almost like an apnea,

711
00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:53.410
a kind of starving of yourself or oxygen when you do this.

712
00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:54.700
But it's very clear,

713
00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:57.210
I mean, it's abundantly clear that two things,

714
00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:58.500
one is nasal breathing

715
00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:01.600
and that the other is getting sufficient oxygen to the brain

716
00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:03.240
is important for comprehension.

717
00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:05.520
So the takeaway from this is if ever possible,

718
00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.841
try and read from a written page

719
00:27:07.841 --> 00:27:10.190
or from a book or a piece of paper,

720
00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:11.630
and if you can't do that,

721
00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:13.760
then try and access whatever you're learning

722
00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:16.280
from as large a screen as you can manage

723
00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:17.537
in that particular environment.

724
00:27:17.537 --> 00:27:19.390
And I do realize when people are commuting

725
00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:21.150
and sometimes I'm guilty of looking at my phone,

726
00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:23.620
and trying to read papers and passages on my phone.

727
00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:25.800
But it's interesting because this study highlights

728
00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:30.440
the linkage between this visual attention, our respiration,

729
00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:32.540
and the fact that our body and our brain

730
00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:33.710
have these innate mechanisms

731
00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:35.890
for making sure that we're getting sufficient oxygen,

732
00:27:35.890 --> 00:27:37.960
we're getting sufficient blood flow to the brain areas

733
00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:39.340
that are required for learning.

734
00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:43.500
And so again, I don't want to deemphasize the fact

735
00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:46.930
that there is a lot of legitimate clinically

736
00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:48.450
diagnosed ADHD out there,

737
00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.870
but for a lot of people that are struggling with attention

738
00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.870
or they feel like they just can't focus,

739
00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:54.747
I think we all owe it to ourselves to ask,

740
00:27:54.747 --> 00:27:58.000
"Well, what kind of environment am I creating for myself

741
00:27:58.000 --> 00:27:59.717
that is depriving me of this focus?

742
00:27:59.717 --> 00:28:01.330
And what simple things can I do

743
00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:03.010
to try and enhance my ability of focus?"

744
00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:06.780
Because I'm personally of the belief that if possible

745
00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:08.830
and wherever it's not hazardous

746
00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:10.740
to start with behavioral tools, right?

747
00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:12.520
Before ever moving to supplementation

748
00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:15.660
or prescription drugs, unless there's a real dire need,

749
00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.330
I would think that behavioral tools

750
00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:18.440
should be the most valuable

751
00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:19.273
for the simple reason

752
00:28:19.273 --> 00:28:21.110
that when you engage in a behavioral over and over again,

753
00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:22.160
you get neuroplasticity,

754
00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:23.860
your brain circuits change and get better.

755
00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:26.570
Simply taking a drug or a supplement of any kind,

756
00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:27.950
although they can have their value,

757
00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.110
isn't going to rewire your brain in the ways that you want.

758
00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:32.267
You're essentially dependent on that chemistry

759
00:28:32.267 --> 00:28:34.200
in order to access the state that you want.

760
00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.033
Right.

761
00:28:35.033 --> 00:28:38.210
And the behavioral tools are gonna range from breath,

762
00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:43.210
light exposure, nutrition, sleep, mindfulness, temperature,

763
00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:47.563
all of these things that are becoming more and more

764
00:28:47.563 --> 00:28:52.090
part and parcel of the neuroscience toolbox, right?

765
00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:55.460
Like this field, which originated

766
00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:57.701
from this perspective of let's understand the brain

767
00:28:57.701 --> 00:29:02.701
as by necessity broadened to this mind body study

768
00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.450
and trying to understand human biology more holistically.

769
00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:07.283
Yeah, absolutely.

770
00:29:07.283 --> 00:29:10.050
And I'm delighted that that's happening.

771
00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.940
And I wanna point out that oftentimes

772
00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:15.580
in the journey of teaching some of these tools

773
00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:17.830
and sharing some of the science around breathwork,

774
00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:20.763
or which is really respiration science

775
00:29:20.763 --> 00:29:22.720
in the academic terms,

776
00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:25.210
or about the role of temperature, or light,

777
00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:27.557
people will quite understandably respond,

778
00:29:27.557 --> 00:29:29.029
"Well, wait! This has been known about

779
00:29:29.029 --> 00:29:30.870
for thousands of years."

780
00:29:30.870 --> 00:29:32.620
Getting light in your eyes in the morning is useful,

781
00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:34.000
these sorts of things, breathing in this way.

782
00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.980
And I completely acknowledge that.

783
00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:41.330
And my stance is that the communities

784
00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:43.410
that have talked about these things for a number of years,

785
00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.090
as well as the scientific community

786
00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:46.680
are both guilty of the same thing,

787
00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:48.344
which is hiding and disguising things

788
00:29:48.344 --> 00:29:50.360
in very complicated language.

789
00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:51.777
And so my goal is not to say,

790
00:29:51.777 --> 00:29:55.023
"Oh, science is discovering these things,"

791
00:29:55.023 --> 00:29:57.450
many of these things were discovered before,

792
00:29:57.450 --> 00:29:59.020
but rather to put a language to them

793
00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:01.570
that is neither cloaked in a mysticism,

794
00:30:01.570 --> 00:30:03.940
nor is cloaked in scientific jargon.

795
00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:05.436
That's really a key goal.

796
00:30:05.436 --> 00:30:08.600
Because I think that we should all acknowledge

797
00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:10.570
that many of these practices are quite useful

798
00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:12.240
and we weren't really taught them in school,

799
00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:16.070
and also mysticism is often a barrier

800
00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:18.240
to a lot of these things becoming accessible.

801
00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.050
