So we weren't reliant on just the sleep scores within whoop,

1061
00:39:59.890 --> 00:40:02.570
we could get everything we wanted out of those data.

1062
00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:06.010
And so for us it represented a really powerful dataset,

1063
00:40:06.010 --> 00:40:09.310
and we're also delighted because NCCIH,

1064
00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:11.340
which is this complimentary health division of NIH.

1065
00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:12.540
They did not fund this study,

1066
00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:13.840
but they paid careful attention

1067
00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.040
to the fact that we and others started to do this.

1068
00:40:16.040 --> 00:40:19.560
And now NIH is starting to have specific calls

1069
00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:21.580
to action for funding science

1070
00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:23.510
that's done in the real world

1071
00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:26.270
and analyzed in labs at universities.

1072
00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:27.450
And of course there are limitations

1073
00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:29.330
to doing things this way,

1074
00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:32.590
but I found this to be incredibly interesting,

1075
00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:33.850
fun and informative.

1076
00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:36.880
And it really says to me that if you were going

1077
00:40:36.880 --> 00:40:38.210
to do a breathwork practice,

1078
00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:41.880
first of all, you don't need more than five minutes per day.

1079
00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:45.070
If you are going to select one breathwork practice,

1080
00:40:45.070 --> 00:40:47.530
cyclic sighing, this double inhales followed by exhales

1081
00:40:47.530 --> 00:40:48.450
for five minutes a day,

1082
00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:50.950
seems to have the greatest positive effect

1083
00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:53.380
on the greatest number of parameters.

1084
00:40:53.380 --> 00:40:55.370
And of course, there are other box breathing

1085
00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:57.310
is still useful, meditation is still useful;

1086
00:40:57.310 --> 00:41:01.010
we certainly found positive effects of those practices,

1087
00:41:01.010 --> 00:41:02.910
but it really points out the fact that

1088
00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:04.920
some of these innate patterns of breathing,

1089
00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:05.753
like cyclic sighing

1090
00:41:05.753 --> 00:41:08.210
that we do naturally every five minutes or so

1091
00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:11.380
can be sort of hijacked if you will,

1092
00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:13.630
and brought into a more condensed form

1093
00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:14.770
for five minutes a day.

1094
00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:15.850
And I think five minutes a day

1095
00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:17.420
is a pretty reasonable amount of time

1096
00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:18.253
to ask of oneself to do breathwork.

1097
00:41:18.253 --> 00:41:19.860
And I should say it didn't matter

1098
00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:22.100
if people did it early in the day or later in the day,

1099
00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:24.090
but the consistency was important.

1100
00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:26.332
And how significant was the state change

1101
00:41:26.332 --> 00:41:29.292
of that type of breathwork versus baseline

1102
00:41:29.292 --> 00:41:32.850
and also in comparison to the other varieties?

1103
00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:33.683
Yeah. Great question.

1104
00:41:33.683 --> 00:41:36.160
So during the actual breathwork session,

1105
00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:38.450
every group and including the meditation group

1106
00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:41.450
experienced a dramatic shift in their state.

1107
00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:43.430
For the sick like hyperventilation group,

1108
00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:46.210
they actually were ramped up more autonomic arousal.

1109
00:41:46.210 --> 00:41:47.860
You could think of that as more stress,

1110
00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:49.690
but as David Spiegel says,

1111
00:41:49.690 --> 00:41:51.150
it's not just the state you're in,

1112
00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:52.060
it's how you got there

1113
00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:53.670
and whether or not you had anything to do with it.

1114
00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:55.480
So when you are self-directing

1115
00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:56.821
an increase in autonomic arousal,

1116
00:41:56.821 --> 00:41:59.990
like getting into an ice bath, or charging up a hill,

1117
00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:01.270
or breathing really fast,

1118
00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:03.690
when you are doing that deliberately and you want to,

1119
00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:06.390
you generally associate that with a positive shift

1120
00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:08.780
in increased autonomic arousal.

1121
00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:10.270
When somebody else triggers that in you

1122
00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:12.490
or when event in the world triggers that in you,

1123
00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:13.447
then you generally say,

1124
00:42:13.447 --> 00:42:14.820
"Well, that was really stressful."

1125
00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:17.350
Which really speaks to the power of the prefrontal cortex,

1126
00:42:17.350 --> 00:42:19.500
our mind in interpreting these bodily states

1127
00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:21.050
that are exactly the same.

1128
00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:22.840
But to answer your question,

1129
00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:24.770
we always have this cutoff

1130
00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:28.440
of a P less than 0.05 statistically significant differences.

1131
00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:30.930
So they could not be attributed to chance.

1132
00:42:30.930 --> 00:42:33.330
The cyclic sighing condition did lead to,

1133
00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:36.510
I would say a near doubling of the positive effects

1134
00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:37.610
compared to the others,

1135
00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:41.390
but all four groups displayed positive shifts

1136
00:42:41.390 --> 00:42:45.410
compared to what was at baseline essentially.

1137
00:42:45.410 --> 00:42:47.214
Now it's very hard to have a pure control condition

1138
00:42:47.214 --> 00:42:48.550
in something like this.

1139
00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:51.130
'Cause if you tell people just sit quietly for five minutes,

1140
00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:54.190
it is its own sort of form of mindfulness and stillness.

1141
00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:56.180
So control conditions in these sorts of studies

1142
00:42:56.180 --> 00:42:57.510
are a little bit complicated.

1143
00:42:57.510 --> 00:42:59.460
Our next step is to get people into brain scanners

1144
00:42:59.460 --> 00:43:02.740
to really understand what is changing at the neural level

1145
00:43:02.740 --> 00:43:04.220
in the short term and the long term.

1146
00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:06.210
And that's actually where Spiegel's group

1147
00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:08.150
has done this incredible work on hypnosis,

1148
00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:11.490
showing that when people go into states of self-hypnosis

1149
00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:13.610
there are signature shifts

1150
00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:16.480
in what they call the default mode network.

1151
00:43:16.480 --> 00:43:18.740
How the brain is idling the different brain areas

1152
00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:22.490
that are active and the ability to control context,

1153
00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:24.160
which has a lot to do with focus.

1154
00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:25.690
And we could talk about hypnosis if you like,

1155
00:43:25.690 --> 00:43:27.210
although he'd be better suited to tell...

1156
00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:29.070
Yeah, no, that was my next thing.

1157
00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:32.620
I wanna better understand the Spiegel version of hypnosis

1158
00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:34.272
versus the spinning pinwheel version

1159
00:43:34.272 --> 00:43:36.700
that we all conjure in our minds,

1160
00:43:36.700 --> 00:43:38.408
like explain what that is

1161
00:43:38.408 --> 00:43:43.408
and how that's becoming this tool for scientific study.

1162
00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:45.793
Yeah.

1163
00:43:45.793 --> 00:43:47.633
Well, so this is David Spiegel's work

1164
00:43:47.633 --> 00:43:49.300
and he has an interesting story.

1165
00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:53.310
His father was actually a psychiatrist and hypnotist.

1166
00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:56.940
And his father learned about hinosis and incorporated it

1167
00:43:56.940 --> 00:44:00.270
into his psychiatric practice based on a mentor that he had.

1168
00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.880
So this is often how things are handed down in medicine.

1169
00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:05.570
And so David learned it from his father.

1170
00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:08.050
And I should say that the effects of clinical hypnosis

1171
00:44:08.050 --> 00:44:09.393
are very robust.

1172
00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:13.960
Incredible success with things like smoking cessation,

1173
00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:16.040
incredible success with pain reduction,

1174
00:44:16.040 --> 00:44:19.410
50 to 80% reductions in chronic pain,

1175
00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:21.034
outcomes in cancer even probably

1176
00:44:21.034 --> 00:44:23.570
due to the negative effects of chronic stress

1177
00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:27.210
on cancer outcomes by reducing stress, trauma and so forth.

1178
00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:31.950
So basically what Spiegel and his father developed

1179
00:44:31.950 --> 00:44:35.580
was a assay, sort of what we call a curbside assay

1180
00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:37.440
for determining whether or not someone

1181
00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.860
has a high, medium, or low degree of hypnotizability.

1182
00:44:40.860 --> 00:44:43.890
And this brings us back to the visual system.

1183
00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:48.763
The neurons in the brain stem that control eye movements,

1184
00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:50.900
they have a relationship

1185
00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:53.520
either to the aspect of the nervous system

1186
00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:55.670
that's associated with alertness or with calmness.

1187
00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:56.560
And not surprisingly,

1188
00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:59.340
when people look up when the eyes are directed upward,

1189
00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:01.410
that's actually in the neural pathways

1190
00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:02.910
associated with alertness.

1191
00:45:02.910 --> 00:45:04.760
When they look downward or close their eyelids,

1192
00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:06.210
those are associated with the neural pathways

1193
00:45:06.210 --> 00:45:08.010
associated with calmness and sleep.

1194
00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:09.330
Which is kind of a dough when you hear it.

1195
00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:10.163
So like when you get tired,

1196
00:45:10.163 --> 00:45:12.330
you kind of put your chin down, when you close your eyes.

1197
00:45:12.330 --> 00:45:13.163
When you're wide awake,

1198
00:45:13.163 --> 00:45:15.240
you tend to be eyes up and eyes really wide.

1199
00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:17.093
Recalling of course,

1200
00:45:17.093 --> 00:45:18.980
that your eyes are the two pieces of your brain

1201
00:45:18.980 --> 00:45:20.790
that happen to be outside your cranial vault

1202
00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:21.910
and the only two pieces of your brain

1203
00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:22.830
outside your cranial vault.

1204
00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:25.000
So when you see eyes, those are two pieces of brain,

1205
00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:26.120
which is just to underscore

1206
00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:28.019
why they're so powerfully reflective

1207
00:45:28.019 --> 00:45:30.030
of what's going on deeper in the brain.

1208
00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:31.810
So when people can look this up online,

1209
00:45:31.810 --> 00:45:32.960
it sounds a little wacky,

1210
00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:36.150
but there's something called the Spiegel eye roll test,

1211
00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:37.970
which is not the teen eye roll

1212
00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:40.764
that's usually associated with a different kind of sigh.

1213
00:45:40.764 --> 00:45:41.838
I'm familiar with that one.

1214
00:45:41.838 --> 00:45:42.960
(both laughing)

1215
00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:46.120
We've all done it or experienced it.

1216
00:45:46.120 --> 00:45:50.550
So basically you can assess how hypnotizable somebody is

1217
00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:51.810
by having them look up.

1218
00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:53.330
I can actually do with you right now.

1219
00:45:53.330 --> 00:45:56.610
And again, I'm hijacking David's tool,

1220
00:45:56.610 --> 00:45:58.530
so you can look up towards the ceiling

1221
00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:01.180
and then while maintaining upward gaze,

1222
00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:03.660
slowly close your eyelids.

1223
00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:04.493
Okay.

1224
00:46:04.493 --> 00:46:05.770
So you were able to just shut your eyelids down,

1225
00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:06.850
and you can open your eyes now.

1226
00:46:06.850 --> 00:46:08.140
And so what I observed was,

1227
00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:10.150
as soon as you brought your eyelids down,

1228
00:46:10.150 --> 00:46:12.240
your eyes also move towards me and your eyelids shut.

1229
00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:14.300
Now I'm very hypnotizable,

1230
00:46:14.300 --> 00:46:15.970
and there's a little bit of a bias demonstration

1231
00:46:15.970 --> 00:46:17.770
because I'm trying to illustrate the differences.

1232
00:46:17.770 --> 00:46:20.240
But I fall at the very far end of the scale

1233
00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:22.270
on high-hypnotizability,

1234
00:46:22.270 --> 00:46:24.120
where when I look up and then...

1235
00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:27.240
I don't wanna go into hypnosis, then I close my eyes.

1236
00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:29.220
The whites of my eyes should still be exposed

1237
00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:30.970
for a second before...

1238
00:46:30.970 --> 00:46:32.020
In other words,

1239
00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:35.980
you're able to maintain your pupils focused upward

1240
00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:37.130
as you're closing your eyes.

1241
00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:37.963
Right.

1242
00:46:37.963 --> 00:46:40.230
And the reason this test was developed to be clear,

1243
00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:41.750
'cause I realized this is probably all sounding

1244
00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:43.750
a little bit wacko to people.

1245
00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:45.190
The reason that this was developed

1246
00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:46.740
is that there's a known role

1247
00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:49.140
of particular cranial nerves, okay?

1248
00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:51.140
Three, four, and seven,

1249
00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:52.860
as well as some other cranial nerves,

1250
00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:56.140
one set of cranial nerves directs the eyes upward

1251
00:46:56.140 --> 00:46:57.490
and is associated with alertness.

1252
00:46:57.490 --> 00:47:00.070
The other set of cranial nerves directs the eyes downwards

1253
00:47:00.070 --> 00:47:01.700
associated with closing of the eyelid.

1254
00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:03.580
So these are two competing circuits.

1255
00:47:03.580 --> 00:47:07.360
People who are very capably of being hypnotized,

1256
00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:09.320
tend to be able to maintain activation

1257
00:47:09.320 --> 00:47:11.100
of both circuits simultaneously,

1258
00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:13.320
which will make sense for what I'm gonna say next,

1259
00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:17.370
which is that hypnosis is really a state of deep calm,

1260
00:47:17.370 --> 00:47:20.590
yet high degree of focused and limited context.

1261
00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:22.270
So we remember that autonomic seesaw

1262
00:47:22.270 --> 00:47:27.270
that we're either asleep or panicked, drowsy or alert,

1263
00:47:27.640 --> 00:47:30.300
and in waking state like you are in now

1264
00:47:30.300 --> 00:47:32.410
probably more towards alert

1265
00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:34.290
but calm kind of the seesaw is even.

1266
00:47:34.290 --> 00:47:38.270
So this is almost a an unusual bending of the seesaw

1267
00:47:38.270 --> 00:47:41.230
in a way that you're both very relaxed and very alert

1268
00:47:41.230 --> 00:47:44.450
and context is limited by whatever the hypnotist says.

1269
00:47:44.450 --> 00:47:46.780
Now in clinical hypnosis,

1270
00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:48.060
the goal is to get the person

1271
00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:51.270
to self-direct their own mental changes.

1272
00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:54.540
Whereas in stage hypnosis, the goal is exactly the same;

1273
00:47:54.540 --> 00:47:57.680
bring people into a state of very calm,

1274
00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:00.260
but narrow context and increased focus,

1275
00:48:00.260 --> 00:48:01.570
but the hypnotist is interested

1276
00:48:01.570 --> 00:48:04.020
in directing the person's actions and states.

1277
00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:05.380
So that's the key distinction.

1278
00:48:05.380 --> 00:48:08.280
And I think that clinical hypnotists like Spiegel and others

1279
00:48:08.280 --> 00:48:10.660
who are also world certified physicians and psychiatrists,

1280
00:48:10.660 --> 00:48:12.750
but I think all clinical hypnotists,

1281
00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:15.920
they look at stage hypnosis as kind of the...

1282
00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:17.230
They don't like it so much

1283
00:48:17.230 --> 00:48:19.133
because it detracts from the power of this thing

1284
00:48:19.133 --> 00:48:21.470
that we call hypnosis and people get distracted

1285
00:48:21.470 --> 00:48:23.840
by the fact that people can be inspired to do things

1286
00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:25.330
that they wouldn't otherwise.

1287
00:48:25.330 --> 00:48:27.640
So in a clinical hypnosis session,

1288
00:48:27.640 --> 00:48:29.750
Spiegel or someone else would bring you in,

1289
00:48:29.750 --> 00:48:31.360
you do this test,

1290
00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:33.150
you would probably fall into the category

1291
00:48:33.150 --> 00:48:36.350
of low to moderate hypnotizability,

1292
00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:37.520
that might rule you out,

1293
00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:39.730
but for most people they're going to be moderate

1294
00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:41.390
to high levels of hypnotizability.

1295
00:48:41.390 --> 00:48:43.110
And then there would be some discussion

1296
00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:46.130
while you were in this state of hypnosis

1297
00:48:46.130 --> 00:48:50.010
where the clinician would encourage you

1298
00:48:50.010 --> 00:48:52.860
to think about certain aspects of whatever it is

1299
00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:53.693
that you're dealing with.

1300
00:48:53.693 --> 00:48:55.940
So maybe it was problems with focus

1301
00:48:55.940 --> 00:48:58.900
and they would have you visualize to spotlights

1302
00:48:58.900 --> 00:48:59.790
and bringing them together.

1303
00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:01.150
Maybe it would be two focus on something

1304
00:49:01.150 --> 00:49:03.330
that really bothers you quite a lot

1305
00:49:03.330 --> 00:49:04.520
and the bodily sensations,

1306
00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:06.800
and then to dissociate those bodily sensations.

1307
00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:11.150
And the degree of positive clinical outcomes using hypnosis,

1308
00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:12.950
again, are just remarkable.

1309
00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:15.440
If people wanna try this there is an app,

1310
00:49:15.440 --> 00:49:16.750
I can mention with that Spiegel

1311
00:49:16.750 --> 00:49:19.450
has developed on the basis of the clinical data

1312
00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:21.280
and a lot of scientific studies

1313
00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:22.950
exploring the brain areas that are activated.

1314
00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:25.730
It's called Reveri, R-E-V-E-R-I.

1315
00:49:25.730 --> 00:49:26.563
I should mention it,

1316
00:49:26.563 --> 00:49:28.030
it does now sit behind a paywall,

1317
00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:30.250
but I think you get a seven day free trial.

1318
00:49:30.250 --> 00:49:33.130
And it's right now only available for Apple,

1319
00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:36.930
but I'm told that they're gonna have it for Android as well.

1320
00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:39.220
And there are other clinical tools for hypnosis.

1321
00:49:39.220 --> 00:49:41.640
I've personally found hypnosis to be very valuable

1322
00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:43.880
for enhancing my ability to get into sleep.

1323
00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:46.840
And for any time that I'm dealing with a problem

1324
00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:50.357
that I can't seem to solve simply by talking, running

1325
00:49:50.357 --> 00:49:51.907
and a couple good night's sleep

1326
00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:55.940
or any kind of cathartic behavior that I'm might attempt.

1327
00:49:55.940 --> 00:49:57.790
So there's a lot there

1328
00:49:57.790 --> 00:50:00.220
and there's a lot more to be said about that,

1329
00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:02.940
and maybe Spiegel should step in at some point

1330
00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:04.110
and join the conversation.

1331
00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:08.930
But I think that hypnosis is really a wonderful example

1332
00:50:08.930 --> 00:50:11.210
of how vision can allow one

1333
00:50:11.210 --> 00:50:12.700
to assess whether or the brain

1334
00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.440
can shift state easily or not.

1335
00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:17.100
It certainly involves narrowing of visual fields

1336
00:50:17.100 --> 00:50:18.900
in order to anchor focus,

1337
00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:21.710
and there's also a respiration component.

1338
00:50:21.710 --> 00:50:23.330
Almost always in hypnosis

1339
00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:25.493
the hypnotist will encourage the person

1340
00:50:25.493 --> 00:50:28.830
to take a deep breath as they close their eyes

1341
00:50:28.830 --> 00:50:31.700
and to then imagine floating and being in a state of calm.

1342
00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:33.430
And the bigger theme here,

1343
00:50:33.430 --> 00:50:36.490
perhaps the most important theme is that neuroplasticity,

1344
00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:38.480
the brain and nervous system's ability to change

1345
00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:41.550
in response to experience really is a two part process.

1346
00:50:41.550 --> 00:50:43.010
The first part of that process

1347
00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:45.640
always involves focus and attention.

1348
00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:46.610
Especially as an adult,

1349
00:50:46.610 --> 00:50:49.350
you simply cannot learn unless you are focused

1350
00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:50.700
on what you want to learn.

1351
00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:51.533
We know this.

1352
00:50:51.533 --> 00:50:54.110
As a kid, there's a bit more passive learning.

1353
00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:56.630
But as an adult, unless it's a negative event,

1354
00:50:56.630 --> 00:50:59.160
which tends to automatically recruit your focus, right?

1355
00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:01.880
The hot stove, the horrible experience, the car crash,

1356
00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:03.200
the trauma of any kind,

1357
00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:06.290
which immediately grabs all your attention,

1358
00:51:06.290 --> 00:51:08.510
you have to direct your attention.

1359
00:51:08.510 --> 00:51:10.650
And then the second part of neuroplasticity

1360
00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:15.520
'cause it is indeed a process is periods of deep rest.

1361
00:51:15.520 --> 00:51:17.190
It is during periods of sleep

1362
00:51:17.190 --> 00:51:19.550
and what we call non-sleep deep rest

1363
00:51:19.550 --> 00:51:23.280
that the neural circuits themselves change and rewire.

1364
00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:26.740
Hypnosis seems to incorporate both the focus

1365
00:51:26.740 --> 00:51:28.010
and the relaxation

1366
00:51:28.010 --> 00:51:30.660
in a way that accelerates neuroplasticity.

1367
00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:32.670
And so while it might seem kind of mystical,

1368
00:51:32.670 --> 00:51:34.990
or wacky, or crazy, it makes perfect sense

1369
00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:35.823
as to why this would be.

1370
00:51:35.823 --> 00:51:38.290
It grabs both states of autonomic arousal,

1371
00:51:38.290 --> 00:51:40.210
high degree of focus and arousal

1372
00:51:40.210 --> 00:51:42.120
and high degree of relaxation

1373
00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:44.880
and it compacts them into a single routine.

1374
00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:49.350
But in the context of a clinical hypnosis session,

1375
00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:54.290
the kind of direction that's being given the patient

1376
00:51:54.290 --> 00:51:58.320
is to disassociate with the neural groove or whatever,

1377
00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:00.150
like let's say, they're trying to quit smoking.

1378
00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:05.150
Like what is the process of untangling that knot

1379
00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:08.700
so that you can create this new behavior pattern.

1380
00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:09.533
Okay. Yeah.

1381
00:52:09.533 --> 00:52:12.280
Very astute of you because it highlights a conundrum

1382
00:52:12.280 --> 00:52:13.177
that the field of neuroscience,

1383
00:52:13.177 --> 00:52:15.590
and psychiatry, and psychology has.

1384
00:52:15.590 --> 00:52:18.260
And indeed that all of wellness and high performance has,

1385
00:52:18.260 --> 00:52:22.130
which is on the one hand you could imagine

1386
00:52:22.130 --> 00:52:24.470
that the way to shift one's brain and body

1387
00:52:24.470 --> 00:52:26.930
around a traumatic event or some challenge

1388
00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:29.710
would be to really fully embody

1389
00:52:29.710 --> 00:52:33.000
all the emotions and bodily sensations of that thing,

1390
00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:35.980
and then over time, desensitize yourself.

1391
00:52:35.980 --> 00:52:38.210
So that's one form of gradual dissociation

1392
00:52:38.210 --> 00:52:40.790
from at least the emotional component of something.

1393
00:52:40.790 --> 00:52:43.920
You could also imagine that the goal is to split those off

1394
00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:44.753
at the outset.

1395
00:52:44.753 --> 00:52:45.950
And I'll just mention,

1396
00:52:45.950 --> 00:52:48.410
we hear nowadays a lot about a FDA approved therapy

1397
00:52:48.410 --> 00:52:50.010
which is ketamine therapy.

1398
00:52:50.010 --> 00:52:52.350
Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic.

1399
00:52:52.350 --> 00:52:54.550
It's being used to treat depression and trauma.

1400
00:52:54.550 --> 00:52:55.960
This is kept in emergency rooms,

1401
00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:58.390
now this is in widespread use.

1402
00:52:58.390 --> 00:53:00.590
Work from my colleague called (indistinct) lab at Stanford

1403
00:53:00.590 --> 00:53:03.070
has shown the precise neural networks in the brain

1404
00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:06.100
that are activated by ketamine in an animal model,

1405
00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:07.250
but also in humans.

1406
00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:10.242
And it's very clear that it causes this dissociative state.

1407
00:53:10.242 --> 00:53:13.460
It actually uncouples brain areas

1408
00:53:13.460 --> 00:53:14.780
that normally would be coupled.

1409
00:53:14.780 --> 00:53:15.613
And so you think,

1410
00:53:15.613 --> 00:53:16.720
"Well, that's weird,

1411
00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.760
I thought that in order to heal trauma,

1412
00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:20.410
you're supposed to go into the trauma

1413
00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:22.180
and then reduce the amount of emotion."

1414
00:53:22.180 --> 00:53:24.110
But in these ketamine induced states,

1415
00:53:24.110 --> 00:53:26.107
people actively report things like,

1416
00:53:26.107 --> 00:53:27.650
"I was watching myself,"

1417
00:53:27.650 --> 00:53:30.130
third person in myself, "Go through the experience,"

1418
00:53:30.130 --> 00:53:31.660
which is exactly what you hear about people

1419
00:53:31.660 --> 00:53:32.960
who went through a trauma,

1420
00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:36.510
things like sexual trauma, like rapes, people say,

1421
00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:37.730
I was floating above my body

1422
00:53:37.730 --> 00:53:39.930
and could see it happening as somebody else.

1423
00:53:41.660 --> 00:53:43.440
Many of the therapies designed to treat trauma

1424
00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:46.160
are exactly the sort of dissociative process

1425
00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:47.830
that is occurring during the trauma.

1426
00:53:47.830 --> 00:53:51.730
So I don't have an answer as to why those treatments

1427
00:53:51.730 --> 00:53:55.330
can work despite embodying the same kind of approach

1428
00:53:55.330 --> 00:53:56.890
that happened during the trauma.

1429
00:53:56.890 --> 00:53:58.340
What does seem to be the case

1430
00:53:58.340 --> 00:54:02.500
is that accessing the state of mind that was occurring

1431
00:54:02.500 --> 00:54:06.860
during the trauma, or during anxiety, or insomnia, or pain,

1432
00:54:06.860 --> 00:54:09.950
and then third personing that experience

1433
00:54:09.950 --> 00:54:12.290
and being able to imagine a different bodily

1434
00:54:12.290 --> 00:54:16.130
or mental response seems to be the common theme

1435
00:54:16.130 --> 00:54:19.001
through all treatments for trauma, fear, anxiety, et cetera.

1436
00:54:19.001 --> 00:54:21.230
And the additional requirement,

1437
00:54:21.230 --> 00:54:23.329
however, is that it's not sufficient

1438
00:54:23.329 --> 00:54:25.500
to just cognitively rehearse it.

1439
00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:27.770
There needs to be a shift in bodily state

1440
00:54:27.770 --> 00:54:30.755
and almost always what threads through all these therapies

1441
00:54:30.755 --> 00:54:33.260
like ketamine therapy or any kind of,

1442
00:54:33.260 --> 00:54:36.000
even cathartic therapy is that at some point

1443
00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:38.139
the patient or the person needs to access a state

1444
00:54:38.139 --> 00:54:42.010
of self-directed deep calm.

1445
00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:43.917
And so there are versions of hypnosis,

1446
00:54:43.917 --> 00:54:46.560
for instance, if you're dealing with a particular problem,

1447
00:54:46.560 --> 00:54:48.500
you imagine the problem on our left screen,

1448
00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:50.010
this is under hypnosis,

1449
00:54:50.010 --> 00:54:53.120
and a left panel and then you write out in your mind

1450
00:54:53.120 --> 00:54:55.890
the possible alternate responses on the right panel,

1451
00:54:55.890 --> 00:54:58.980
you grow the size of that and reduce the size of the other,

1452
00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:00.690
might sound like, why would that work?

1453
00:55:00.690 --> 00:55:03.790
But under conditions where the total context of the mind

1454
00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:05.230
is set to that process,

1455
00:55:05.230 --> 00:55:07.210
and you're not even aware of anything else

1456
00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.650
going on in the room,

1457
00:55:08.650 --> 00:55:11.360
that seems to accelerate the neuroplasticity

1458
00:55:11.360 --> 00:55:13.560
and allows people to actually do that in real life

1459
00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:15.150
out of hypnosis.

1460
00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:16.420
It's super fascinating.

1461
00:55:16.420 --> 00:55:18.840
I mean, it's crazy to me

1462
00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:22.030
to hear about the therapeutic applications of ketamine,

1463
00:55:22.030 --> 00:55:25.900
because my association with that is being in treatment

1464
00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:27.300
with people that use that.

1465
00:55:27.300 --> 00:55:29.300
Like I knew one guy who jumped off his roof.

1466
00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:30.133
Oh, yeah.

1467
00:55:30.133 --> 00:55:32.459
Ketamine, that drug is no joke.

1468
00:55:32.459 --> 00:55:34.299
It's cool that they're finding these ways

1469
00:55:34.299 --> 00:55:38.347
of applying it in in a context like this,

1470
00:55:38.347 --> 00:55:42.310
but yeah, like buyer beware, I suppose too,

1471
00:55:42.310 --> 00:55:44.420
these are controlled environments obviously.

1472
00:55:44.420 --> 00:55:45.253
Absolutely.

1473
00:55:45.253 --> 00:55:47.140
And even though they are controversial

1474
00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:50.500
one can't help but notice the work of,

1475
00:55:50.500 --> 00:55:51.930
for instance, Matthew Johnson,

1476
00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:53.890
who's at Johns Hopkins School of Medicine,

1477
00:55:53.890 --> 00:55:56.330
and he's looking at macro dose psilocybin

1478
00:55:56.330 --> 00:55:58.210
for the treatment of trauma and depression.

1479
00:55:58.210 --> 00:55:59.975
And I've talked to Matt directly and I said,

1480
00:55:59.975 --> 00:56:04.330
what is the key element of a successful therapeutic session?

1481
00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:05.460
Because he was telling me,

1482
00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:06.830
one person is having one experience,

1483
00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:08.130
another person's having another experience.

1484
00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:09.630
So there's nothing to anchor this.

1485
00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:11.577
And he said, it's the quote unquote,

1486
00:56:11.577 --> 00:56:14.350
"Letting go and allowing their autonomic arousal

1487
00:56:14.350 --> 00:56:16.500
to be taken over by something else."

1488
00:56:16.500 --> 00:56:18.970
And I thought, well, the data are pretty impressive,

1489
00:56:18.970 --> 00:56:20.894
but that's the exact opposite of what we're talking about

1490
00:56:20.894 --> 00:56:23.290
in terms of ketamine type therapies.

1491
00:56:23.290 --> 00:56:25.570
I think hypnosis kind of resides in the middle

1492
00:56:25.570 --> 00:56:27.980
in that it involves going into the state

1493
00:56:27.980 --> 00:56:30.560
that creates the anxiety, trauma or pain,

1494
00:56:30.560 --> 00:56:33.520
and then actively dissociating from that state

1495
00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:36.810
in a way that you actively replace it with another state.

1496
00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:39.650
Psychedelic therapies are still very poorly understood.

1497
00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:42.530
One of the things that I think is important to emphasize is,

1498
00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:45.166
we always hear such and such opens plasticity,

1499
00:56:45.166 --> 00:56:46.570
such and such...

1500
00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:49.820
Plasticity is a process like digestion

1501
00:56:49.820 --> 00:56:50.653
or something like that.

1502
00:56:50.653 --> 00:56:52.870
You don't necessarily want to open plasticity

1503
00:56:52.870 --> 00:56:54.670
because all sorts of things can happen in there.

1504
00:56:54.670 --> 00:56:57.210
And I'm aware of people that have benefited tremendously

1505
00:56:57.210 --> 00:56:58.380
from psychedelic therapies,

1506
00:56:58.380 --> 00:57:01.370
I'm also aware of people that have suffered tremendously

1507
00:57:01.370 --> 00:57:02.620
from psychedelic therapies.

1508
00:57:02.620 --> 00:57:04.970
And I have a colleague at Stanford also in psychiatry,

1509
00:57:04.970 --> 00:57:06.220
whose name is Nolan Williams.

1510
00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:08.530
He does transcranial magnetic stimulation.

1511
00:57:08.530 --> 00:57:10.900
He's an expert and a researcher and clinician

1512
00:57:10.900 --> 00:57:13.580
in understanding depression and tools to explore that.

1513
00:57:13.580 --> 00:57:15.850
And he has studies that he's performing now

1514
00:57:15.850 --> 00:57:18.140
looking at the brains of people

1515
00:57:18.140 --> 00:57:20.580
who have undergone different forms of psychedelic therapies

1516
00:57:20.580 --> 00:57:21.780
with no bias whatsoever

1517
00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:23.270
as to whether or not they're beneficial or not,

1518
00:57:23.270 --> 00:57:25.460
but really to highlight the individual differences

1519
00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:27.700
before, not during,

1520
00:57:27.700 --> 00:57:30.180
but then after the psychedelic therapies.

1521
00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:32.340
And so I think there's still a lot to be learned.

1522
00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:36.760
I mean, as much as we understand mechanisms and brain areas,

1523
00:57:36.760 --> 00:57:38.940
and there's some successes out there

1524
00:57:38.940 --> 00:57:41.490
and some failures out there in psychiatry and neuroscience,

1525
00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:43.560
I mean, we're still groping around in the dark

1526
00:57:43.560 --> 00:57:44.990
more or less in terms of figuring out

1527
00:57:44.990 --> 00:57:46.860
like what these compounds do.

1528
00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:48.490
And a big effort in large part

1529
00:57:48.490 --> 00:57:50.133
led by a group up at UC Davis.

1530
00:57:51.010 --> 00:57:53.120
Some people are gonna be dismayed by this,

1531
00:57:53.120 --> 00:57:58.020
but a big effort is being made to take these drug compounds,

1532
00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:01.040
remove the hallucinogenic components from them

1533
00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:02.960
and ask whether or not there are other aspects

1534
00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:05.100
to their biology that have nothing to do

1535
00:58:05.100 --> 00:58:07.420
with hallucinating or the letting go,

1536
00:58:07.420 --> 00:58:10.300
or the other aspects of psychedelic journeys

1537
00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:11.680
in order to figure out whether or not

1538
00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:13.310
something else in those compounds

1539
00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:15.220
is allowing the brain to readjust itself.

1540
00:58:15.220 --> 00:58:16.053
Yeah, interesting.
Right?

1541
00:58:16.053 --> 00:58:17.980
Because there are a lot of variables

1542
00:58:17.980 --> 00:58:20.400
in a psychedelic journey.

1543
00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:23.760
It's not necessarily the case that the hallucination

1544
00:58:23.760 --> 00:58:26.010
or the disruption of time and space

1545
00:58:26.010 --> 00:58:28.360
is the thing that's rewiring the brain.

1546
00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:29.560
So there's a lot to be learned.

1547
00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:30.660
Take the fun out of it.

1548
00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:32.400
Yeah. For some people.

1549
00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:34.340
Although I do think it will make it more accessible

1550
00:58:34.340 --> 00:58:38.590
for people who are averse to the idea of losing control.

1551
00:58:38.590 --> 00:58:40.736
And so I'm excited by all these things.

1552
00:58:40.736 --> 00:58:42.490
I certainly don't wanna encourage

1553
00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:45.250
on a maverick use of these things,

1554
00:58:45.250 --> 00:58:46.770
many of the them are still scheduled drugs

1555
00:58:46.770 --> 00:58:48.902
so they can land you in jail, right?

1556
00:58:48.902 --> 00:58:52.360
I think hypnosis at least for me

1557
00:58:52.360 --> 00:58:55.050
lands in a category of interesting and intriguing

1558
00:58:55.050 --> 00:58:56.130
for people to explore.

1559
00:58:56.130 --> 00:58:57.840
It's certainly non-invasive.

1560
00:58:57.840 --> 00:58:58.790
You would definitely wanna work

1561
00:58:58.790 --> 00:59:00.320
with somebody very, very good

1562
00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:03.495
who also has some clinical training in dealing with trauma,

1563
00:59:03.495 --> 00:59:04.997
who also has some clinical training

1564
00:59:04.997 --> 00:59:07.639
in dealing with whatever it is that people are dealing with

1565
00:59:07.639 --> 00:59:09.620
because there's been success with eating disorders,

1566
00:59:09.620 --> 00:59:12.750
which are very, very challenging in the abs,

1567
00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:15.860
even with medication, those can be very, very challenging.

1568
00:59:15.860 --> 00:59:18.900
ADHD and some of the other clinical syndromes.

1569
00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:22.020
Well, the broadening of neuroscience

1570
00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:25.780
to kind of include psychiatry and psychology,

1571
00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:26.900
I think is super interesting.

1572
00:59:26.900 --> 00:59:31.900
And this idea of how we're approaching

1573
00:59:32.390 --> 00:59:35.160
the processing of things like trauma

1574
00:59:35.160 --> 00:59:38.610
through dissociative practices is super interesting

1575
00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:42.660
because we've always thought of dealing with our past

1576
00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:46.040
through a kind of talk therapy modality,

1577
00:59:46.040 --> 00:59:48.650
which by definition requires

1578
00:59:48.650 --> 00:59:51.030
that we confront some aspect of our past

1579
00:59:51.030 --> 00:59:53.300
or something that we've repressed.

1580
00:59:53.300 --> 00:59:54.133
And of course,

1581
00:59:54.133 --> 00:59:57.280
that's gonna bring up all types of powerful feelings

1582
00:59:57.280 --> 00:59:59.250
and you have to feel those feelings,

1583
00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:01.810
but you also have to understand that feelings are out facts

1584
01:00:01.810 --> 01:00:05.090
and the only way to kind of get to the other side,

1585
01:00:05.090 --> 01:00:06.980
and kind of withdraw the charge

1586
01:00:06.980 --> 01:00:10.370
that those past events have on

1587
01:00:10.370 --> 01:00:12.970
how you think about yourself and behave in the world

1588
01:00:12.970 --> 01:00:15.870
is to create that disassociation, right?

1589
01:00:15.870 --> 01:00:17.610
So how does that...

1590
01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:20.300
Let's just kind of focus on trauma right now,

1591
01:00:20.300 --> 01:00:22.219
because I think it's super important.

1592
01:00:22.219 --> 01:00:26.950
everybody has their past trauma and those traumas

1593
01:00:26.950 --> 01:00:30.070
are very powerful as predictors of current

1594
01:00:30.070 --> 01:00:31.470
and future behavior.

1595
01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:32.303
Yeah.

1596
01:00:32.303 --> 01:00:35.480
I mean, if ever there was a phenomenon in life

1597
01:00:35.480 --> 01:00:39.600
that I'm struck by it's trauma

1598
01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:43.630
and it's incredible to me

1599
01:00:43.630 --> 01:00:48.060
how we experience these things

1600
01:00:48.060 --> 01:00:50.060
for some people more than others, right?

1601
01:00:51.130 --> 01:00:52.550
To some extent everyone has trauma,

1602
01:00:52.550 --> 01:00:54.360
to some extent I think we need to be fair

1603
01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:56.137
and say that some people have a tremendous amount of trauma

1604
01:00:56.137 --> 01:00:57.463
and other people less.

1605
01:00:58.570 --> 01:01:00.130
But that there's some consistent themes

1606
01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:02.440
that the psychoanalyst actually had right.

1607
01:01:02.440 --> 01:01:05.290
Which is that for many people, there's this,

1608
01:01:05.290 --> 01:01:07.970
what the analyst would call a repetition compulsion, right?

1609
01:01:07.970 --> 01:01:10.471
Somebody experiences something really terrible as a child

1610
01:01:10.471 --> 01:01:13.650
and then as an adult they find themselves seeking out

1611
01:01:13.650 --> 01:01:14.557
similar types of situations.

1612
01:01:14.557 --> 01:01:17.960
And it's just the most illogical thing one can imagine.

1613
01:01:17.960 --> 01:01:19.600
And the analyst would say,

1614
01:01:19.600 --> 01:01:20.980
well, this is a reparative wish,

1615
01:01:20.980 --> 01:01:24.720
an attempt to throw oneself back subconsciously,

1616
01:01:24.720 --> 01:01:26.780
throw oneself back into these scenarios

1617
01:01:26.780 --> 01:01:28.170
to get a different outcome.

1618
01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:29.003
Right.

1619
01:01:29.003 --> 01:01:31.783
So for example, if you had a deadbeat dad,

1620
01:01:32.720 --> 01:01:34.023
you're a young woman,

1621
01:01:34.898 --> 01:01:36.810
you're gonna seek out a boyfriend

1622
01:01:36.810 --> 01:01:38.967
who has those same behavior patterns.

1623
01:01:38.967 --> 01:01:39.850
(indistinct chatters)

1624
01:01:39.850 --> 01:01:41.400
Defies logic.

1625
01:01:41.400 --> 01:01:43.020
Yeah. In one case, it's the exact opposite.

1626
01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:43.853
In which case we might say,

1627
01:01:43.853 --> 01:01:45.650
well, that's a more functional step,

1628
01:01:45.650 --> 01:01:47.080
in the other case it's,

1629
01:01:47.080 --> 01:01:48.520
yeah, it's this repetition compulsion

1630
01:01:48.520 --> 01:01:51.460
making the same mistakes despite knowing better, right?

1631
01:01:51.460 --> 01:01:54.520
And not necessarily from this standpoint of addiction

1632
01:01:54.520 --> 01:01:57.470
where there are some deeper dopamine circuits driving that.

1633
01:01:58.304 --> 01:02:00.560
So trauma is fascinating in that way.

1634
01:02:00.560 --> 01:02:02.010
I mean, what do we know about trauma?

1635
01:02:02.010 --> 01:02:04.780
And again, I'm borrowing from some of my conversations

1636
01:02:04.780 --> 01:02:06.810
with David Spiegel and with Anna Lamche

1637
01:02:06.810 --> 01:02:08.660
who I know you've had on the podcast.

1638
01:02:09.640 --> 01:02:14.640
It's very clear that relief from trauma in some way

1639
01:02:14.910 --> 01:02:18.580
or another almost always involves going

1640
01:02:18.580 --> 01:02:21.540
deliberately bringing oneself back into the state of mind

1641
01:02:21.540 --> 01:02:24.410
and body that occurred during the trauma,

1642
01:02:24.410 --> 01:02:26.200
as horrible as that might seem

1643
01:02:26.200 --> 01:02:28.530
avoiding that seems to be an issue.

1644
01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:31.210
And then gradually or hopefully quickly,

1645
01:02:31.210 --> 01:02:34.630
but in some cases, gradually desensitizing oneself

1646
01:02:34.630 --> 01:02:35.890
to that experience

1647
01:02:35.890 --> 01:02:38.960
as not just a overwhelming, horrible experience,

1648
01:02:38.960 --> 01:02:41.960
but a sad but no longer overwhelming experience.

1649
01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:45.180
So that they can gain some sort of ability

1650
01:02:45.180 --> 01:02:47.430
to think inside of the memory,

1651
01:02:47.430 --> 01:02:51.080
and to parse what happened.

1652
01:02:51.080 --> 01:02:53.160
One resounding theme that I've collected

1653
01:02:53.160 --> 01:02:54.540
in talking to trauma therapists

1654
01:02:54.540 --> 01:02:57.450
and exploring a lot of therapies for trauma

1655
01:02:57.450 --> 01:03:01.410
is that oftentimes trauma involves a deep confusion

1656
01:03:01.410 --> 01:03:02.350
for whatever reason,

1657
01:03:02.350 --> 01:03:05.490
a deep confusion about who was responsible.

1658
01:03:05.490 --> 01:03:07.950
And this is something that's somewhat complicated

1659
01:03:07.950 --> 01:03:10.340
and can be troubling to think about,

1660
01:03:10.340 --> 01:03:13.040
but people will experience a trauma;

1661
01:03:13.040 --> 01:03:15.130
a car crash, a sexual assault,

1662
01:03:15.130 --> 01:03:19.000
a devastating financial loss can also be a trauma,

1663
01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:22.760
and then somehow in one's mind,

1664
01:03:22.760 --> 01:03:23.593
it's not clear whether or not

1665
01:03:23.593 --> 01:03:25.210
that was something that happened to them

1666
01:03:25.210 --> 01:03:27.000
or that they created for themselves.

1667
01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:28.270
Now, the typical script of this

1668
01:03:28.270 --> 01:03:29.587
was people talking about,

1669
01:03:29.587 --> 01:03:31.260
"Oh, I shouldn't have been out that late,

1670
01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:33.180
or dressed that way, or acted that way,"

1671
01:03:33.180 --> 01:03:34.750
but it's actually can be much more subtle

1672
01:03:34.750 --> 01:03:36.163
and diabolical than that.

1673
01:03:37.882 --> 01:03:41.100
It can start to route into people's own percept of self.

1674
01:03:41.100 --> 01:03:42.940
Maybe I'm not worthy of being happy,

1675
01:03:42.940 --> 01:03:45.130
and therefore the fact that this happened makes total sense.

1676
01:03:45.130 --> 01:03:47.460
People create these crazy scripts

1677
01:03:47.460 --> 01:03:51.540
and crazy because they don't match any real world facts,

1678
01:03:51.540 --> 01:03:54.860
but they do match a lot of internal structure.

1679
01:03:54.860 --> 01:03:58.630
And so it becomes very complex to unpack all this.

1680
01:03:58.630 --> 01:04:00.610
But what we know for sure is that accessing

1681
01:04:00.610 --> 01:04:01.920
the state of mind and body

1682
01:04:01.920 --> 01:04:05.340
that resembles the state of mind and body during the trauma

1683
01:04:05.340 --> 01:04:09.020
is the first step in moving trauma out of the body,

1684
01:04:09.020 --> 01:04:10.190
so-called trauma release.

1685
01:04:10.190 --> 01:04:12.600
Now, almost always that has to be done in concert

1686
01:04:12.600 --> 01:04:15.550
with a really well trained physician or clinician

1687
01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:17.270
because that can be overwhelming

1688
01:04:17.270 --> 01:04:18.900
certainly the first time.

1689
01:04:18.900 --> 01:04:20.010
There's also some evidence

1690
01:04:20.010 --> 01:04:22.100
based on some decent studies

1691
01:04:22.100 --> 01:04:26.500
that show that deliberately accessing states

1692
01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:28.760
of high autonomic arousal

1693
01:04:28.760 --> 01:04:30.420
that are independent from the trauma.

1694
01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:34.150
So things like ice baths, things like hard exercise,

1695
01:04:34.150 --> 01:04:36.380
things like very, very intense experiences

1696
01:04:36.380 --> 01:04:38.380
separate from the traumatic memory

1697
01:04:38.380 --> 01:04:39.980
can be useful in allowing people

1698
01:04:39.980 --> 01:04:41.920
to attain comfort at high levels

1699
01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:43.063
of autonomic arousal, right?

1700
01:04:43.063 --> 01:04:45.500
I mean, you're trying to essentially say,

1701
01:04:45.500 --> 01:04:48.100
go back to this place and work it through,

1702
01:04:48.100 --> 01:04:50.690
try and get some space or some distance from the emotion

1703
01:04:50.690 --> 01:04:52.031
and yet for some people

1704
01:04:52.031 --> 01:04:55.337
just an elevation in heart rate is overwhelming for them.

1705
01:04:55.337 --> 01:04:57.300
And so they're not even gonna set foot

1706
01:04:57.300 --> 01:04:59.040
on the first step up the mountain

1707
01:04:59.040 --> 01:05:01.880
when in fact that's exactly what they need to do.

1708
01:05:01.880 --> 01:05:04.988
So there's now a kind of movement in psychiatry

1709
01:05:04.988 --> 01:05:07.620
and psychology to bring in more of these,

1710
01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:09.290
I guess you could call them somatic approaches.

1711
01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:11.940
But to my mind, they're really physiological approaches.

1712
01:05:11.940 --> 01:05:14.110
They're really approaches that teach people

1713
01:05:14.110 --> 01:05:18.750
how to be tolerant of high levels of autonomic arousal.

1714
01:05:18.750 --> 01:05:21.600
And so that's something that we're starting to see

1715
01:05:21.600 --> 01:05:22.700
a shift in.

1716
01:05:22.700 --> 01:05:25.063
The other thing I should just acknowledge is that

1717
01:05:25.063 --> 01:05:27.020
until the psychoanalyst,

1718
01:05:27.020 --> 01:05:28.620
the cognitive behavioral therapists,

1719
01:05:28.620 --> 01:05:31.600
the neuroscientists and the psychiatrist join arms,

1720
01:05:31.600 --> 01:05:34.070
we are going to go around and around and around

1721
01:05:34.070 --> 01:05:36.370
this merry go round of not knowing

1722
01:05:36.370 --> 01:05:39.580
how to deal with trauma and feelings, right?

1723
01:05:39.580 --> 01:05:41.170
I mean, we're all told to feel our feelings,

1724
01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:42.320
but not trust them as facts.

1725
01:05:42.320 --> 01:05:43.890
And yet, here I'm telling you

1726
01:05:43.890 --> 01:05:45.810
that most successful trauma therapies

1727
01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:48.330
involve getting right up close to that event,

1728
01:05:48.330 --> 01:05:50.617
really letting it almost overtake oneself,

1729
01:05:50.617 --> 01:05:53.390
and then start to create these gaps.

1730
01:05:53.390 --> 01:05:55.210
And these gaps that I'm referring to

1731
01:05:55.210 --> 01:05:57.870
are real gaps in neural circuitry.

1732
01:05:57.870 --> 01:06:00.800
The work from Spiegel and many others has shown,

1733
01:06:00.800 --> 01:06:02.530
and the work doing brain imaging

1734
01:06:02.530 --> 01:06:03.810
under conditions of ketamine

1735
01:06:03.810 --> 01:06:06.290
and other types of pharmacologic therapies

1736
01:06:06.290 --> 01:06:09.820
has shown that there are active associations

1737
01:06:09.820 --> 01:06:11.800
normally between the prefrontal cortex,

1738
01:06:11.800 --> 01:06:14.000
which is thinking, planning and reasoning,

1739
01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:16.180
the insular, which is an area of the brain

1740
01:06:16.180 --> 01:06:18.350
that monitors how we feel internally,

1741
01:06:18.350 --> 01:06:19.640
and then some of these areas

1742
01:06:19.640 --> 01:06:21.930
like the anterior singlet cortex,

1743
01:06:21.930 --> 01:06:24.110
which are involved in kind of self-monitoring

1744
01:06:24.110 --> 01:06:25.839
and figuring out how much of what I'm experiencing

1745
01:06:25.839 --> 01:06:28.550
is coming from thoughts and things within

1746
01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:30.160
and from things in my environment.

1747
01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:33.060
And under conditions of extreme autonomic arousal,

1748
01:06:33.060 --> 01:06:35.001
and somewhat counterintuitively

1749
01:06:35.001 --> 01:06:37.610
under conditions of deep relaxation,

1750
01:06:37.610 --> 01:06:40.810
those neural circuits are able to rearrange themselves.

1751
01:06:40.810 --> 01:06:43.800
And when one emerges from those treatments,

1752
01:06:43.800 --> 01:06:46.930
the default network then is one of perspective.

1753
01:06:46.930 --> 01:06:47.763
It's one of saying, "

1754
01:06:47.763 --> 01:06:49.070
"Ah, this is something that happened,

1755
01:06:49.070 --> 01:06:51.870
but it was not my fault,"

1756
01:06:51.870 --> 01:06:53.300
or, "This is something that happened

1757
01:06:53.300 --> 01:06:54.760
and indeed it set me up

1758
01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:56.800
for a number of positive opportunities in my life."

1759
01:06:56.800 --> 01:06:59.560
All those sorts of stories that we hear about all the time.

1760
01:06:59.560 --> 01:07:01.065
So if this is sounding a little bit abstract,

1761
01:07:01.065 --> 01:07:03.600
I'm trying my best to put some structure on it,

1762
01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:05.225
but where there's a lack of structure

1763
01:07:05.225 --> 01:07:07.840
I'll kind of wriggle out of that

1764
01:07:07.840 --> 01:07:10.640
by saying that there is no clear answer

1765
01:07:10.640 --> 01:07:13.450
as to where trauma is represented in the nervous system.

1766
01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:15.840
Many people talk about trauma being manifest

1767
01:07:15.840 --> 01:07:17.560
as physical symptoms in their body,

1768
01:07:17.560 --> 01:07:21.090
and that used to be considered kind of pseudoscience,

1769
01:07:21.090 --> 01:07:22.250
makes perfect sense,

1770
01:07:22.250 --> 01:07:23.083
makes perfect sense.

1771
01:07:23.083 --> 01:07:24.550
After all the nervous system is the brain,

1772
01:07:24.550 --> 01:07:26.647
the eyes and the spinal cord for central nervous system,

1773
01:07:26.647 --> 01:07:28.560
and then every organ in your body

1774
01:07:28.560 --> 01:07:31.357
is innovated by nerve cells, neurons,

1775
01:07:31.357 --> 01:07:32.620
and the peripheral nervous system.

1776
01:07:32.620 --> 01:07:36.510
So the manifestation of things in the body and in the brain

1777
01:07:36.510 --> 01:07:37.900
shouldn't surprise anybody.

1778
01:07:37.900 --> 01:07:41.420
And I think one of the great expansions of neuroscience

1779
01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:45.120
in the last few years is that whereas five/10 years ago,

1780
01:07:45.120 --> 01:07:49.840
you would find very few grants funding work in laboratories

1781
01:07:49.840 --> 01:07:52.980
and laboratories working on elements of the neurosystem

1782
01:07:52.980 --> 01:07:53.920
in the body.

1783
01:07:53.920 --> 01:07:55.150
Very, very few.

1784
01:07:55.150 --> 01:07:58.205
Maybe 98% of the focus was on things within the brain,

1785
01:07:58.205 --> 01:08:02.570
memory, consciousness, vision hearing, et cetera.

1786
01:08:02.570 --> 01:08:04.320
Now an enormous number of laboratories

1787
01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:07.050
are moving into this mind body relationship,

1788
01:08:07.050 --> 01:08:08.430
but they don't call it that,

1789
01:08:08.430 --> 01:08:10.380
they call it the peripheral nervous system,

1790
01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:11.830
the autonomic nervous system, right?

1791
01:08:11.830 --> 01:08:14.200
They call it the gut brain axis, right?

1792
01:08:14.200 --> 01:08:17.470
So we have fancy names to disguise the fact

1793
01:08:17.470 --> 01:08:19.190
that we're studying mind and body.

1794
01:08:19.190 --> 01:08:20.023
Right.

1795
01:08:20.023 --> 01:08:21.180
God forbid.
God forbid.

1796
01:08:21.180 --> 01:08:22.543
Anybody find out.

1797
01:08:24.070 --> 01:08:27.850
What's interesting about the trauma discussion is,

1798
01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:28.683
when I think about it,

1799
01:08:28.683 --> 01:08:33.683
I think of trauma being just an extreme example

1800
01:08:33.780 --> 01:08:38.770
of not just something that happened to you,

1801
01:08:38.770 --> 01:08:41.212
but a story we tell about who we are, like what we are,

1802
01:08:41.212 --> 01:08:43.490
what we were talking about this the other day.

1803
01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:47.580
We all walk around looping some narrative in our minds

1804
01:08:47.580 --> 01:08:49.950
about who we are, what we're capable of,

1805
01:08:49.950 --> 01:08:53.033
what happened to us, why we are the way we are.

1806
01:08:53.960 --> 01:08:57.330
And I think we cause ourselves a lot of suffering

1807
01:08:57.330 --> 01:09:00.550
and undue pain as a result of narratives

1808
01:09:00.550 --> 01:09:02.280
that are really detached from reality

1809
01:09:02.280 --> 01:09:03.953
whether positive or negative.

1810
01:09:04.868 --> 01:09:05.902
They take on a life of their own

1811
01:09:05.902 --> 01:09:08.150
and they're very real to us

1812
01:09:08.150 --> 01:09:10.320
and yet if you put them under the microscope

1813
01:09:10.320 --> 01:09:14.030
and deconstruct them, they actually generally don't hold up.

1814
01:09:14.030 --> 01:09:19.030
So from a neurochemistry, neurobiology point of view,

1815
01:09:21.100 --> 01:09:22.117
how do you think about this

1816
01:09:22.117 --> 01:09:26.900
and how can you talk about empowering people

1817
01:09:26.900 --> 01:09:29.330
to deconstruct those narratives

1818
01:09:29.330 --> 01:09:32.960
and hopefully create healthier versions of them?

1819
01:09:32.960 --> 01:09:33.793
Yeah.

1820
01:09:33.793 --> 01:09:38.350
Well, the stability of self is a very mysterious

1821
01:09:38.350 --> 01:09:40.870
but very pervasive theme in neuroscience.

1822
01:09:40.870 --> 01:09:41.930
I mean, you wake up every day

1823
01:09:41.930 --> 01:09:44.110
and depending on how you feel about yourself

1824
01:09:44.110 --> 01:09:45.640
you might feel better or worse,

1825
01:09:45.640 --> 01:09:48.940
but you're you every day, you recognize yourself.

1826
01:09:48.940 --> 01:09:50.440
But what is you?

1827
01:09:50.440 --> 01:09:54.193
And how malleable is your default personality?

1828
01:09:56.500 --> 01:09:57.588
Well, what is you is,

1829
01:09:57.588 --> 01:10:00.760
I mean, that's certainly not gonna be anchored

1830
01:10:00.760 --> 01:10:02.040
in one brain area.

1831
01:10:02.040 --> 01:10:02.873
We know this.

1832
01:10:02.873 --> 01:10:04.570
There's gonna be a network of activity.

1833
01:10:04.570 --> 01:10:07.480
There are cases of course, of multiple personality

1834
01:10:07.480 --> 01:10:08.313
and things of that sort,

1835
01:10:08.313 --> 01:10:10.193
but exceedingly rare.

1836
01:10:11.723 --> 01:10:14.090
Our self percept is often very different

1837
01:10:14.090 --> 01:10:15.880
than other people's perception of us.

1838
01:10:15.880 --> 01:10:17.490
Say, I don't want to out him,

1839
01:10:17.490 --> 01:10:19.460
but we have a member of our podcast team

1840
01:10:19.460 --> 01:10:22.240
who to me is just like the calmest person in the world

1841
01:10:22.240 --> 01:10:24.410
and he manages an incredibly complicated life

1842
01:10:24.410 --> 01:10:25.810
with immense grace.

1843
01:10:25.810 --> 01:10:27.970
He's extremely talented in his work.

1844
01:10:27.970 --> 01:10:30.310
He's just an incredible human being.

1845
01:10:30.310 --> 01:10:34.190
But if you ask him a bit more about his internal world,

1846
01:10:34.190 --> 01:10:36.280
if he's feeling like he wants to share,

1847
01:10:36.280 --> 01:10:38.670
but he'll say, yeah, internally,

1848
01:10:38.670 --> 01:10:41.159
it's just chatter, there's tons going on,

1849
01:10:41.159 --> 01:10:42.810
but externally I experience him

1850
01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:45.270
as the one of the calmest people I know.

1851
01:10:45.270 --> 01:10:48.070
And I think a lot of people see themselves

1852
01:10:48.070 --> 01:10:50.800
or feel themselves as anxious when in reality

1853
01:10:50.800 --> 01:10:52.770
the way they emerge in the real world

1854
01:10:52.770 --> 01:10:56.130
is very, very different from their internal percept.

1855
01:10:56.130 --> 01:10:58.051
So that's a first thing to recognize.

1856
01:10:58.051 --> 01:11:01.290
Conversing a lot of people who think they're incredible

1857
01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:03.450
might not be perceived as incredible.

1858
01:11:03.450 --> 01:11:05.760
And we could have a whole conversation about narcissists

1859
01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:08.060
some other time, because that's a real thing, right?

1860
01:11:08.060 --> 01:11:11.247
I mean, narcissists exploit the reactions of other people

1861
01:11:11.247 --> 01:11:14.280
and the commitments and allegiances

1862
01:11:14.280 --> 01:11:15.540
or lack of allegiances of other people

1863
01:11:15.540 --> 01:11:17.998
in order to feed this very squishy internal ego, right?

1864
01:11:17.998 --> 01:11:19.121
That they have.

1865
01:11:19.121 --> 01:11:21.200
Their self-concept is actually very weak

1866
01:11:21.200 --> 01:11:24.252
but they come across often as very dominant and in control.

1867
01:11:24.252 --> 01:11:27.170
And we're getting better at recognizing

1868
01:11:27.170 --> 01:11:28.020
who those people are

1869
01:11:28.020 --> 01:11:31.310
and the interesting thing is they probably don't realize

1870
01:11:31.310 --> 01:11:32.330
that they are the way they are,

1871
01:11:32.330 --> 01:11:35.700
just by the thousands if not tens of thousands of videos

1872
01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:38.470
about narcissists on YouTube and elsewhere.

1873
01:11:38.470 --> 01:11:40.073
So narcissist aside.

1874
01:11:41.926 --> 01:11:46.810
The reality is that much of how we are gauged

1875
01:11:46.810 --> 01:11:49.850
in terms of who we are is on our behavior

1876
01:11:49.850 --> 01:11:52.900
and how calm people perceive us to be

1877
01:11:52.900 --> 01:11:55.540
or how stressed people perceive us to be

1878
01:11:55.540 --> 01:11:57.300
in a bunch of different contexts.

1879
01:11:57.300 --> 01:11:59.370
And that's actually one of the major ways

1880
01:11:59.370 --> 01:12:00.370
that we just sort of assess

1881
01:12:00.370 --> 01:12:03.030
whether or not somebody would be a useful partner

1882
01:12:03.030 --> 01:12:05.150
or somebody to know in one situation or another

1883
01:12:05.150 --> 01:12:06.350
in many situations.

1884
01:12:06.350 --> 01:12:08.260
We're very good at subconsciously

1885
01:12:08.260 --> 01:12:10.610
perceiving other people's level of autonomic arousal

1886
01:12:10.610 --> 01:12:12.360
through things like pupil dilation.

1887
01:12:13.254 --> 01:12:14.930
I'm talking a lot now,

1888
01:12:14.930 --> 01:12:18.480
so I might be more flush than usual,

1889
01:12:18.480 --> 01:12:20.420
but it's contextual, right?

1890
01:12:20.420 --> 01:12:22.040
It's contextual, we're here and we're talking a lot.

1891
01:12:22.040 --> 01:12:23.840
So you also gain knowledge over time

1892
01:12:23.840 --> 01:12:24.673
and you realize that,

1893
01:12:24.673 --> 01:12:26.800
"Ah, when this person is in this situation,

1894
01:12:26.800 --> 01:12:29.660
they occupy this kind of level of arousal in that situation,

1895
01:12:29.660 --> 01:12:31.130
that level arousal and so on."

1896
01:12:31.130 --> 01:12:33.810
So in terms of our concept of others,

1897
01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:35.310
I think that gaining we're a lot of knowledge

1898
01:12:35.310 --> 01:12:36.160
about the brain areas,

1899
01:12:36.160 --> 01:12:37.610
for instance, incredible work

1900
01:12:37.610 --> 01:12:39.990
from Nancy Kanwisher's lab at MIT,

1901
01:12:39.990 --> 01:12:42.070
we have what's called the fusiform face area,

1902
01:12:42.070 --> 01:12:44.449
literally a little catalog or library

1903
01:12:44.449 --> 01:12:46.930
of neurons in your brain.

1904
01:12:46.930 --> 01:12:49.130
This fusiform gyrus area of your brain

1905
01:12:49.130 --> 01:12:53.480
that recognizes faces with exquisite precision.

1906
01:12:53.480 --> 01:12:56.190
I mean, any number people can walk into the room

1907
01:12:56.190 --> 01:12:58.070
and you'll know who they are, or if you don't know them.

1908
01:12:58.070 --> 01:12:59.560
So facial recognition,

1909
01:12:59.560 --> 01:13:01.250
even when people are in profile

1910
01:13:01.250 --> 01:13:02.690
or you see the back of their head,

1911
01:13:02.690 --> 01:13:04.150
so it's not even a face,

1912
01:13:04.150 --> 01:13:06.850
face recognition in old world primates of which we are

1913
01:13:08.170 --> 01:13:10.500
there's incredible machinery there.

1914
01:13:10.500 --> 01:13:12.770
Very little is understood about self-perception

1915
01:13:12.770 --> 01:13:14.680
and where self-perceptions reside.

1916
01:13:14.680 --> 01:13:16.980
But most people can recognize themself in the mirror

1917
01:13:16.980 --> 01:13:19.030
and that occurs from a very early age.

1918
01:13:19.030 --> 01:13:21.240
So I'd be willing to speculate

1919
01:13:21.240 --> 01:13:24.160
that a lot of what we perceive about ourself

1920
01:13:24.160 --> 01:13:27.010
and the stability of our self-perception

1921
01:13:27.010 --> 01:13:29.660
has a lot to do with our visual images of ourself

1922
01:13:29.660 --> 01:13:31.070
and people's reactions to us.

1923
01:13:31.070 --> 01:13:33.970
So we're cataloging all those data all the time.

1924
01:13:33.970 --> 01:13:36.230
Now, how malleable is it?

1925
01:13:36.230 --> 01:13:39.560
Well, I mean, last time we sat down

1926
01:13:39.560 --> 01:13:40.790
for a recorded discussion,

1927
01:13:40.790 --> 01:13:42.010
we talked about how...

1928
01:13:42.010 --> 01:13:44.340
I was really emphasizing actions first

1929
01:13:44.340 --> 01:13:47.890
as a way to shift perception sensations and emotions.

1930
01:13:47.890 --> 01:13:49.810
And I still stand behind that

1931
01:13:49.810 --> 01:13:53.640
because that's the accessible form of neuroplasticity

1932
01:13:53.640 --> 01:13:54.473
for most of us.

1933
01:13:54.473 --> 01:13:57.360
Actions change your nervous system over time.

1934
01:13:57.360 --> 01:14:01.010
And if ever there was a truth in neurosciences

1935
01:14:01.010 --> 01:14:03.530
that the nervous system can change in response to experience

1936
01:14:03.530 --> 01:14:05.410
the more heightened or critical that experience is,

1937
01:14:05.410 --> 01:14:07.550
the faster those changes are going to occur.

1938
01:14:07.550 --> 01:14:10.430
So that just leads me to a place where I'd say,

1939
01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:13.200
how crucial is it to change one's behavior

1940
01:14:13.200 --> 01:14:16.670
and therefore one's identity is probably going to dictate

1941
01:14:16.670 --> 01:14:18.250
whether or not you're able to do it or not.

1942
01:14:18.250 --> 01:14:20.218
I think about that show, "Madman,"

1943
01:14:20.218 --> 01:14:22.730
where he basically takes on a completely different life

1944
01:14:22.730 --> 01:14:25.490
and persona, and he was able to maintain that

1945
01:14:25.490 --> 01:14:28.187
and this secret that he was actually somebody else

1946
01:14:28.187 --> 01:14:29.640
for a long enough period of time,

1947
01:14:29.640 --> 01:14:32.461
but it's through behaviors that people gauge us

1948
01:14:32.461 --> 01:14:33.750
and then we start to gauge ourself.

1949
01:14:33.750 --> 01:14:34.650
Sure.

1950
01:14:34.650 --> 01:14:37.710
So it's actions if you wanna transcend

1951
01:14:37.710 --> 01:14:38.930
whatever loop you're in,

1952
01:14:38.930 --> 01:14:43.560
you start behaving in contrast or whatever that narrative is

1953
01:14:43.560 --> 01:14:46.900
until your nervous system kind of cottons onto that

1954
01:14:46.900 --> 01:14:49.310
and it becomes more habitual.

1955
01:14:49.310 --> 01:14:50.830
But the ultimate question of course,

1956
01:14:50.830 --> 01:14:53.416
would be if somebody just started behaving

1957
01:14:53.416 --> 01:14:55.940
in complete contraposition

1958
01:14:56.820 --> 01:14:59.310
to their entire personality,

1959
01:14:59.310 --> 01:15:04.100
started talking about the opposite of the ideas

1960
01:15:04.100 --> 01:15:06.250
that they believe in and start behaving on that,

1961
01:15:06.250 --> 01:15:08.850
could you just completely transcend your personality

1962
01:15:08.850 --> 01:15:12.720
and become another individual altogether personality-wise?

1963
01:15:12.720 --> 01:15:14.680
Yeah, I believe that's probably possible.

1964
01:15:14.680 --> 01:15:15.513
It's wild.

1965
01:15:15.513 --> 01:15:16.346
It's really wild.

1966
01:15:16.346 --> 01:15:18.870
And it really speaks to the fact that the nervous system

1967
01:15:18.870 --> 01:15:21.590
is paying attention to its own actions.

1968
01:15:21.590 --> 01:15:23.420
It's kind of a case of cognitive dissonance.

1969
01:15:23.420 --> 01:15:26.490
And it's what your actions do in some sense define you

1970
01:15:26.490 --> 01:15:30.650
both to people outside of you and to oneself.

1971
01:15:30.650 --> 01:15:34.000
There's this concept in psychoanalysis of the interject,

1972
01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:35.350
which I find fascinating.

1973
01:15:35.350 --> 01:15:38.590
The interject is this idea that we can subconsciously embody

1974
01:15:38.590 --> 01:15:40.600
the reactions of somebody else.

1975
01:15:40.600 --> 01:15:42.680
So one thing that fascinates me

1976
01:15:42.680 --> 01:15:44.070
now that I'm a bit more involved

1977
01:15:44.070 --> 01:15:46.990
in sort of online interactions and social medias,

1978
01:15:46.990 --> 01:15:48.150
there's tremendous number

1979
01:15:48.150 --> 01:15:51.340
of really inspiring people out there.

1980
01:15:51.340 --> 01:15:52.450
I put you in this category,

1981
01:15:52.450 --> 01:15:53.590
I'll put you on the spot and embarrass you,

1982
01:15:53.590 --> 01:15:55.210
but I read your book long before we met

1983
01:15:55.210 --> 01:15:56.920
and was really motivated

1984
01:15:56.920 --> 01:15:58.660
to make a number of important changes in my life

1985
01:15:58.660 --> 01:16:00.790
on the basis of reading that book.

1986
01:16:00.790 --> 01:16:01.623
I truly was.

1987
01:16:01.623 --> 01:16:04.283
And so just to embarrass you a bit here,

1988
01:16:04.283 --> 01:16:05.523
but that's the (indistinct)

1989
01:16:05.523 --> 01:16:07.380
No, I'm gonna get flush.

1990
01:16:07.380 --> 01:16:08.920
Yeah, appropriately.

1991
01:16:08.920 --> 01:16:11.460
Yeah, 'cause it's an incredible journey.

1992
01:16:11.460 --> 01:16:14.249
And, or last time we talked about David Goggins,

1993
01:16:14.249 --> 01:16:15.840
and there are many examples, right?

1994
01:16:15.840 --> 01:16:17.680
You can go online now and see examples

1995
01:16:17.680 --> 01:16:20.100
of incredible people doing incredible things.

1996
01:16:20.100 --> 01:16:21.657
Now, one version of that is to think,

1997
01:16:21.657 --> 01:16:22.840
"Oh, that's inspiring."

1998
01:16:22.840 --> 01:16:24.967
It shifts my autonomic arousal

1999
01:16:24.967 --> 01:16:27.070
so I can get up at 4:30 in the morning,

2000
01:16:27.070 --> 01:16:29.460
like Jocko and like get after it and do this stuff.

2001
01:16:29.460 --> 01:16:30.293
Which, and frankly,

2002
01:16:30.293 --> 01:16:32.230
there've been times when I'll see a social media post

2003
01:16:32.230 --> 01:16:33.450
and I'm like, "Yeah, I'm being lazy,

2004
01:16:33.450 --> 01:16:35.620
I should really push a little harder."

2005
01:16:35.620 --> 01:16:37.400
Or you had Chad Wright on here.

2006
01:16:37.400 --> 01:16:38.233
Right.

2007
01:16:38.233 --> 01:16:41.620
He and I are very different in a variety of ways

2008
01:16:41.620 --> 01:16:42.530
and we've never met.

2009
01:16:42.530 --> 01:16:44.860
But I find his content in incredibly inspiring

2010
01:16:44.860 --> 01:16:47.870
because the way that he communicates his conviction,

2011
01:16:47.870 --> 01:16:50.350
even though some of my convictions are different than his.

2012
01:16:50.350 --> 01:16:54.050
And I learned about him through you.

2013
01:16:54.050 --> 01:16:56.540
So we could consider that inspiration,

2014
01:16:56.540 --> 01:17:00.080
but then the idea of the analyst that I think is appropriate

2015
01:17:00.080 --> 01:17:01.860
and keep in mind that while Freud,

2016
01:17:01.860 --> 01:17:05.190
there were many issues with him and Young and analysis,

2017
01:17:05.190 --> 01:17:09.260
they did have a heavy interest in physiology

2018
01:17:09.260 --> 01:17:11.040
as the root of the subconscious.

2019
01:17:11.040 --> 01:17:14.460
And there's this idea of the interject is fascinating to me

2020
01:17:14.460 --> 01:17:15.940
because what it says is that,

2021
01:17:15.940 --> 01:17:18.200
if you consume enough of that content,

2022
01:17:18.200 --> 01:17:20.100
if you read enough books about people

2023
01:17:20.100 --> 01:17:21.890
who have embarked on certain kinds of journeys

2024
01:17:21.890 --> 01:17:23.400
and made certain choices,

2025
01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:25.410
that at some point you might interject

2026
01:17:25.410 --> 01:17:28.078
some of their personality and their responses

2027
01:17:28.078 --> 01:17:31.270
and subconsciously start making decisions,

2028
01:17:31.270 --> 01:17:33.650
hopefully positive decisions on your own behalf.

2029
01:17:33.650 --> 01:17:35.220
Maybe having more appropriate boundaries,

2030
01:17:35.220 --> 01:17:38.040
maybe taking better self care or care of other people,

2031
01:17:38.040 --> 01:17:39.800
and that without even realizing it

2032
01:17:39.800 --> 01:17:42.660
you're starting to make better choices on your own behalf,

2033
01:17:42.660 --> 01:17:45.470
and then at some point you move from the interject

2034
01:17:45.470 --> 01:17:46.327
to a recognition that,

2035
01:17:46.327 --> 01:17:49.300
"Wait, it was me that made that change.

2036
01:17:49.300 --> 01:17:51.630
I'm the one that managed to get up a bit earlier

2037
01:17:51.630 --> 01:17:53.960
and do a little bit more or to be kinder in this context,

2038
01:17:53.960 --> 01:17:55.850
or to listen a little bit better in this context."

2039
01:17:55.850 --> 01:17:59.340
And over time, we start to ascribe those changes to ourself.

2040
01:17:59.340 --> 01:18:01.660
So this is a more gradual shift in personality

2041
01:18:01.660 --> 01:18:04.520
and this story of who one is,

2042
01:18:04.520 --> 01:18:06.350
but I think it's a powerful one

2043
01:18:06.350 --> 01:18:09.010
and I like it because we hear so much today

2044
01:18:09.010 --> 01:18:10.970
about the negativity that's out there

2045
01:18:10.970 --> 01:18:15.970
and how to navigate the just onslaught of negative news

2046
01:18:16.000 --> 01:18:17.970
and negative interactions online.

2047
01:18:17.970 --> 01:18:18.803
And for that reason,

2048
01:18:18.803 --> 01:18:20.670
I really make an effort to really focus

2049
01:18:20.670 --> 01:18:24.060
on kind of the bright shining lights out there.

2050
01:18:24.060 --> 01:18:27.240
Because I do think that the information that we consume

2051
01:18:27.240 --> 01:18:30.320
sets the internal context of our subconscious

2052
01:18:30.320 --> 01:18:31.570
and sets the internal context

2053
01:18:31.570 --> 01:18:33.200
for what we decide to do consciously.

2054
01:18:33.200 --> 01:18:36.350
And in many ways, it's sort of like garbage-in-garbage-out.

2055
01:18:36.350 --> 01:18:39.930
And if it's positive stories and inspiration in

2056
01:18:39.930 --> 01:18:42.620
that's how you're basically gonna react in the world.

2057
01:18:42.620 --> 01:18:44.790
So I think that the nervous system

2058
01:18:44.790 --> 01:18:47.700
has a tremendous capacity to learn consciously

2059
01:18:47.700 --> 01:18:49.310
and subconsciously.

2060
01:18:49.310 --> 01:18:51.980
And after all it is during sleep

2061
01:18:51.980 --> 01:18:53.740
that our nervous system rewires.

2062
01:18:53.740 --> 01:18:55.260
We know that the dreaming that occurs

2063
01:18:55.260 --> 01:18:57.050
during rapid eye movement sleep

2064
01:18:57.050 --> 01:18:58.860
has this very unique signature

2065
01:18:58.860 --> 01:19:01.190
of being very emotionally salient;

2066
01:19:01.190 --> 01:19:03.430
falling, being chased, et cetera,

2067
01:19:03.430 --> 01:19:05.800
but that the body is incapable

2068
01:19:05.800 --> 01:19:08.620
of releasing adrenaline, epinephrine during that time.

2069
01:19:08.620 --> 01:19:11.210
So it's its own sort of form of trauma therapy, right?

2070
01:19:11.210 --> 01:19:12.660
So that's a subconscious learning

2071
01:19:12.660 --> 01:19:13.710
that we go through each night

2072
01:19:13.710 --> 01:19:16.080
provided we're getting into rapid eye movement sleep.

2073
01:19:16.080 --> 01:19:19.700
So the brain is designed to access these states in sleep

2074
01:19:19.700 --> 01:19:21.819
that help us rewire in beneficial ways.

2075
01:19:21.819 --> 01:19:23.640
If you want, I don't recommend it,

2076
01:19:23.640 --> 01:19:24.870
you can run the other experiment,

2077
01:19:24.870 --> 01:19:26.390
sleep deprive yourself for three nights

2078
01:19:26.390 --> 01:19:29.070
and see how your emotionality is doing, right?

2079
01:19:29.070 --> 01:19:30.080
Yeah. That's super interesting.

2080
01:19:30.080 --> 01:19:32.380
I mean, I think ultimately though

2081
01:19:32.380 --> 01:19:34.030
it always goes back to action.

2082
01:19:34.030 --> 01:19:35.640
You can consume a tremendous amount

2083
01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:38.470
of inspirational content and then convince yourself

2084
01:19:38.470 --> 01:19:40.210
that you did something productive,

2085
01:19:40.210 --> 01:19:41.710
or you feel better about yourself

2086
01:19:41.710 --> 01:19:45.160
without having ever actually done anything.

2087
01:19:45.160 --> 01:19:46.914
And I see a lot of that as well,

2088
01:19:46.914 --> 01:19:49.720
but ultimately at some point you would think

2089
01:19:49.720 --> 01:19:50.920
that that's gonna translate

2090
01:19:50.920 --> 01:19:52.860
into some kind of action or behavior.

2091
01:19:52.860 --> 01:19:53.850
One would hope.

2092
01:19:53.850 --> 01:19:56.490
And that brings up kind of these ideas

2093
01:19:56.490 --> 01:19:57.687
around alter ego, right?

2094
01:19:57.687 --> 01:19:59.080
If you feel yourself

2095
01:19:59.080 --> 01:20:02.410
not worthy of being that type of person,

2096
01:20:02.410 --> 01:20:04.940
you see Jocko, he's waking up at 4:30 in the morning

2097
01:20:04.940 --> 01:20:06.747
and doing what he does and you say,

2098
01:20:06.747 --> 01:20:08.080
"Well, what would Jocko do?"

2099
01:20:08.080 --> 01:20:09.500
And then you do that.

2100
01:20:09.500 --> 01:20:13.180
You kind of step into the alter ego of Jocko

2101
01:20:13.180 --> 01:20:16.450
and what he would do and start mimicking that behavior,

2102
01:20:16.450 --> 01:20:18.510
over time then you said,

2103
01:20:18.510 --> 01:20:20.187
you wake up and you realize like,

2104
01:20:20.187 --> 01:20:21.920
"Oh, it's me.

2105
01:20:21.920 --> 01:20:23.213
I can shed that alter ego now,

2106
01:20:23.213 --> 01:20:26.210
and I can kind of own this space for myself."

2107
01:20:26.210 --> 01:20:27.100
Yeah. It exactly.

2108
01:20:27.100 --> 01:20:30.290
And I think that one of the hardest things to do

2109
01:20:30.290 --> 01:20:32.750
is to calibrate one's consumption of social media

2110
01:20:32.750 --> 01:20:36.050
or any kind of high potency information.

2111
01:20:36.050 --> 01:20:37.180
I mean, if you think about it,

2112
01:20:37.180 --> 01:20:38.950
this is the first time in human history

2113
01:20:38.950 --> 01:20:43.330
that you can scroll through 50 movies in one minute.

2114
01:20:43.330 --> 01:20:45.903
I mean if a picture is worth a thousand words, right?

2115
01:20:45.903 --> 01:20:49.490
We know that a movie is worth 10,000 pictures.

2116
01:20:49.490 --> 01:20:53.040
I mean, the visual system loves motion.

2117
01:20:53.040 --> 01:20:56.860
And so I'm sure the algorithms reflect this.

2118
01:20:56.860 --> 01:20:58.680
And so really setting constraints

2119
01:20:58.680 --> 01:21:01.350
on the amount of interaction with stuff is important.

2120
01:21:01.350 --> 01:21:02.950
And I think setting constraints

2121
01:21:02.950 --> 01:21:05.750
on what type of information you're going to consume.

2122
01:21:05.750 --> 01:21:07.670
I mean, there is something about our nervous system

2123
01:21:07.670 --> 01:21:10.690
that draws us to look at the car crash,

2124
01:21:10.690 --> 01:21:14.630
to click behind that muted screen

2125
01:21:14.630 --> 01:21:16.930
that allows you to see the gory thing

2126
01:21:16.930 --> 01:21:18.252
or to get involved

2127
01:21:18.252 --> 01:21:21.400
in some sort of a very narrow context online argument.

2128
01:21:21.400 --> 01:21:22.700
I mean, everyone is prone to that.

2129
01:21:22.700 --> 01:21:24.280
Some people more than others,

2130
01:21:24.280 --> 01:21:28.430
but there's also a lot of incredible content out there.

2131
01:21:28.430 --> 01:21:32.490
And I think that the high potency positive content,

2132
01:21:32.490 --> 01:21:33.730
whatever that means,

2133
01:21:33.730 --> 01:21:35.220
for some people that's listening

2134
01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:36.810
to a piece of classical music,

2135
01:21:36.810 --> 01:21:38.460
these days I'm really interested

2136
01:21:38.460 --> 01:21:41.500
in finding a lot of incredible artwork online

2137
01:21:41.500 --> 01:21:42.333
and just going,

2138
01:21:42.333 --> 01:21:44.946
"Wow, people are creating some amazing stuff."

2139
01:21:44.946 --> 01:21:46.150
That can be inspiring.

2140
01:21:46.150 --> 01:21:47.980
So it comes in a variety of different forms

2141
01:21:47.980 --> 01:21:50.370
for different people, but being very,

2142
01:21:50.370 --> 01:21:52.770
I think that we all need to be more guarded

2143
01:21:52.770 --> 01:21:54.635
of the kinds of information in the context

2144
01:21:54.635 --> 01:21:57.210
that we expose our nervous systems to,

2145
01:21:57.210 --> 01:21:59.750
and the problem with social media to just acknowledge it

2146
01:21:59.750 --> 01:22:02.700
is that it's a free for all in terms of context.

2147
01:22:02.700 --> 01:22:05.050
And you can set limits on it in time,

2148
01:22:05.050 --> 01:22:08.360
but you can't set limits on it in terms of context at all

2149
01:22:08.360 --> 01:22:10.750
unless you're very, very good at self governing

2150
01:22:10.750 --> 01:22:13.810
or governing the use of social media in kids, for instance.

2151
01:22:13.810 --> 01:22:18.750
I mean, the just endless fire hose of stimulation

2152
01:22:18.750 --> 01:22:23.750
that you can expose your neurochemistry to is unprecedented.

2153
01:22:24.020 --> 01:22:25.010
We're not wired for that.

2154
01:22:25.010 --> 01:22:30.010
I can't imagine that being impulsed so vociferously,

2155
01:22:31.280 --> 01:22:33.180
so continually in the way that we are

2156
01:22:33.180 --> 01:22:35.570
is doing anything good for us.

2157
01:22:35.570 --> 01:22:38.130
That seems to be the study, right?

2158
01:22:38.130 --> 01:22:40.140
That needs to be done on,

2159
01:22:40.140 --> 01:22:45.140
what is this crazy mass experiment that we're running

2160
01:22:45.150 --> 01:22:46.441
on the human brain right now?

2161
01:22:46.441 --> 01:22:47.274
Yeah.

2162
01:22:47.274 --> 01:22:48.800
Well, I think it brings us back

2163
01:22:48.800 --> 01:22:50.300
to this issue of brain states.

2164
01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:53.300
We, meaning neuroscience,

2165
01:22:53.300 --> 01:22:56.620
certainly not my work, but Matt Walker and others,

2166
01:22:56.620 --> 01:22:58.050
William Dement at Stanford,

2167
01:22:58.050 --> 01:22:59.790
who defined rapid eye movement sleep,

2168
01:22:59.790 --> 01:23:02.063
although he didn't discover it, he helped define it.

2169
01:23:03.210 --> 01:23:05.491
They successfully defined the major stages

2170
01:23:05.491 --> 01:23:08.070
or states of sleep, slow wave sleep,

2171
01:23:08.070 --> 01:23:10.520
rapid eye movement sleep, alternating in 90 minute cycles,

2172
01:23:10.520 --> 01:23:12.350
more or less, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

2173
01:23:12.350 --> 01:23:14.900
When we dream, when we have emotional dreams, muscle repair,

2174
01:23:14.900 --> 01:23:16.720
growth hormone, all that stuff,

2175
01:23:16.720 --> 01:23:19.660
we have very poor definition of waking states.

2176
01:23:19.660 --> 01:23:21.110
We just don't even know what to call them.

2177
01:23:21.110 --> 01:23:23.340
We call them flow, or focused, or happy, or sad,

2178
01:23:23.340 --> 01:23:25.030
but a lot of those are emotional labels

2179
01:23:25.030 --> 01:23:27.960
and kind of subjective labels

2180
01:23:27.960 --> 01:23:32.617
that have some degree of kind of grading to them like,

2181
01:23:32.617 --> 01:23:34.500
"Oh, that was a good state, or a bad state,

2182
01:23:34.500 --> 01:23:35.830
or a happy state."

2183
01:23:35.830 --> 01:23:37.990
We have not yet defined most waking states.

2184
01:23:37.990 --> 01:23:40.040
We hear about alpha waves, gamma waves, et cetera,

2185
01:23:40.040 --> 01:23:41.390
but that's very crude.

2186
01:23:41.390 --> 01:23:42.410
For people that don't know,

2187
01:23:42.410 --> 01:23:44.980
the brain waves are generally measured by e.g,

2188
01:23:44.980 --> 01:23:46.740
these skull cups that are non-invasive

2189
01:23:46.740 --> 01:23:47.573
with a bunch of electrodes.

2190
01:23:47.573 --> 01:23:49.420
Terrific, because you can do this anywhere

2191
01:23:49.420 --> 01:23:51.140
without drilling through the skull,

2192
01:23:51.140 --> 01:23:52.830
terrible, because they only measure activity

2193
01:23:52.830 --> 01:23:56.270
in the outer centimeter or so of the brain.

2194
01:23:56.270 --> 01:23:57.680
So they're not telling you a whole lot

2195
01:23:57.680 --> 01:23:58.513
about what's happening.

2196
01:23:58.513 --> 01:23:59.500
It's like looking at the ocean,

2197
01:23:59.500 --> 01:24:00.333
the waves on the ocean

2198
01:24:00.333 --> 01:24:02.020
trying to figure out what's happening at depth.

2199
01:24:02.020 --> 01:24:03.930
It's just not gonna work.

2200
01:24:03.930 --> 01:24:06.480
So we need to better define waking brain states.

2201
01:24:06.480 --> 01:24:09.170
However, one thing that's clear from the work on sleep

2202
01:24:09.170 --> 01:24:14.060
that's now working its way into the naming

2203
01:24:14.060 --> 01:24:15.990
and understanding of brain states and wakefulness

2204
01:24:15.990 --> 01:24:18.640
is that one state precedes another.

2205
01:24:18.640 --> 01:24:21.740
Now your bike ride where you're listening to the audio book

2206
01:24:21.740 --> 01:24:23.450
is a way in which you're coordinating

2207
01:24:23.450 --> 01:24:25.830
your respiration physiology or breathing,

2208
01:24:25.830 --> 01:24:27.700
and your motor output, your bike riding

2209
01:24:27.700 --> 01:24:30.410
with the ability to access auditory information

2210
01:24:30.410 --> 01:24:31.243
and remember it well.

2211
01:24:31.243 --> 01:24:33.910
So you figured out something about that state,

2212
01:24:33.910 --> 01:24:35.590
but the neuroscientist in me says,

2213
01:24:35.590 --> 01:24:38.270
okay, but what follows that state when you get back?

2214
01:24:38.270 --> 01:24:39.580
What state are you in then?

2215
01:24:39.580 --> 01:24:40.740
Is it a state of exhaustion

2216
01:24:40.740 --> 01:24:42.890
that allows you to go into deep depress

2217
01:24:42.890 --> 01:24:44.480
to access neuroplasticity

2218
01:24:44.480 --> 01:24:48.380
or is it a state of where you've essentially moved

2219
01:24:48.380 --> 01:24:50.840
all that excess autonomic arousal out of your body

2220
01:24:50.840 --> 01:24:52.680
and now finally you can sit down

2221
01:24:52.680 --> 01:24:55.130
and just focus your cone of attention on your work?

2222
01:24:55.130 --> 01:24:56.497
So one state proceeds another.

2223
01:24:56.497 --> 01:24:58.690
And one thing that I think can be incredibly useful

2224
01:24:58.690 --> 01:25:01.800
to people would be to think about two hallmarks

2225
01:25:01.800 --> 01:25:04.410
of brain states in order to access them more readily.

2226
01:25:04.410 --> 01:25:08.913
One is that the thing that we are all a slave to,

2227
01:25:09.810 --> 01:25:12.920
all of us is the 24 hour circadian cycle.

2228
01:25:12.920 --> 01:25:14.740
Every cell in our body has a 24 hour clock

2229
01:25:14.740 --> 01:25:16.250
that runs at the level of genes,

2230
01:25:16.250 --> 01:25:17.660
and the level of cellular processes

2231
01:25:17.660 --> 01:25:19.100
and every network in our body,

2232
01:25:19.100 --> 01:25:20.010
whether or not it's nervous system,

2233
01:25:20.010 --> 01:25:24.160
spleen, whatever is governed by this 24 hour clock.

2234
01:25:24.160 --> 01:25:29.160
We can fairly crudely but still accurately subdivide

2235
01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:31.710
that 24 hour period into three phases.

2236
01:25:31.710 --> 01:25:33.030
What I would call phase one,

2237
01:25:33.030 --> 01:25:34.270
and I'm just naming it phase one,

2238
01:25:34.270 --> 01:25:36.120
'cause we don't have a better name for it

2239
01:25:36.120 --> 01:25:39.070
is from about zero until about eight or nine hours

2240
01:25:39.070 --> 01:25:40.000
after you wake up.

2241
01:25:40.000 --> 01:25:42.740
Phase two is from about nine hours after you wake up

2242
01:25:42.740 --> 01:25:47.010
until about 16 hours after you've woken up.

2243
01:25:47.010 --> 01:25:51.140
And phase three would be from about 17 hours to 24 hours.

2244
01:25:51.140 --> 01:25:54.500
The 17 hour to 24 hour period that we're calling phase three

2245
01:25:54.500 --> 01:25:55.333
is sleep.

2246
01:25:55.333 --> 01:25:57.740
And we know what we should be doing in that state.

2247
01:25:57.740 --> 01:26:00.300
We should be accessing as much uninterrupted,

2248
01:26:00.300 --> 01:26:01.720
deep sleep as we can,

2249
01:26:01.720 --> 01:26:03.910
and the beautiful work that Matt's done and others have done

2250
01:26:03.910 --> 01:26:06.570
says, okay, lower the temperature in the room,

2251
01:26:06.570 --> 01:26:08.810
elevate your feet a little bit, et cetera, et cetera.

2252
01:26:08.810 --> 01:26:09.643
Keep it dark.

2253
01:26:09.643 --> 01:26:10.476
I mean, there's a lot of things

2254
01:26:10.476 --> 01:26:11.513
that we could talk about there.

2255
01:26:12.790 --> 01:26:14.590
Phase one and phase two,

2256
01:26:14.590 --> 01:26:17.000
we know based on really good neuroscience

2257
01:26:17.870 --> 01:26:21.630
have certain signature patterns of neurotransmitter release

2258
01:26:21.630 --> 01:26:24.737
and autonomic arousal that lend themselves better

2259
01:26:24.737 --> 01:26:26.700
to certain activities,

2260
01:26:26.700 --> 01:26:28.430
both mental and physical and not others.

2261
01:26:28.430 --> 01:26:30.150
So phase one, for instance,

2262
01:26:30.150 --> 01:26:32.880
is always accompanied by an increase in cortisol.

2263
01:26:32.880 --> 01:26:35.640
This is a healthy release of cortisol.

2264
01:26:35.640 --> 01:26:37.040
A stress hormone that wakes us up.

2265
01:26:37.040 --> 01:26:38.910
It's part of the wake up system.

2266
01:26:38.910 --> 01:26:40.610
And the release of epinephrine,

2267
01:26:40.610 --> 01:26:41.920
which is also called adrenaline

2268
01:26:41.920 --> 01:26:43.170
and the molecule of dopamine.

2269
01:26:43.170 --> 01:26:44.630
It turns out epinephrine and dopamine,

2270
01:26:44.630 --> 01:26:47.010
dopamine actually manufactures epinephrine.

2271
01:26:47.010 --> 01:26:48.520
They're biochemically.

2272
01:26:48.520 --> 01:26:52.125
So that phase one of the day in many ways is ideal

2273
01:26:52.125 --> 01:26:55.600
for any kind of linear action oriented items.

2274
01:26:55.600 --> 01:26:57.450
If you had, and people will differ.

2275
01:26:57.450 --> 01:26:59.330
Some people say I do creative work best early in day,

2276
01:26:59.330 --> 01:27:00.750
but most people are gonna do best

2277
01:27:00.750 --> 01:27:04.410
at kind of correct answer type work,

2278
01:27:04.410 --> 01:27:07.140
meaning where there is a correct linear output

2279
01:27:07.140 --> 01:27:09.290
it's going to be accounting type work,

2280
01:27:09.290 --> 01:27:11.070
or it's going to be exercise,

2281
01:27:11.070 --> 01:27:13.630
where you might not know exactly what you're going to do

2282
01:27:13.630 --> 01:27:15.230
but there is a process that you can follow.

2283
01:27:15.230 --> 01:27:17.780
It's going to be punching out a certain amount of words

2284
01:27:17.780 --> 01:27:18.613
on a page,

2285
01:27:18.613 --> 01:27:19.690
'cause you're just trying to get

2286
01:27:19.690 --> 01:27:21.100
the words down on the page for the book,

2287
01:27:21.100 --> 01:27:23.840
or doing math, or doing any kind of linear operation

2288
01:27:23.840 --> 01:27:26.150
during that first phase one.

2289
01:27:26.150 --> 01:27:28.660
Phase two has a signature pattern

2290
01:27:28.660 --> 01:27:31.630
of neurotransmitter release in the brain and body

2291
01:27:31.630 --> 01:27:34.970
that are more closely associated, not exclusively,

2292
01:27:34.970 --> 01:27:36.070
but more closely associated

2293
01:27:36.070 --> 01:27:37.980
with the release of things like serotonin.

2294
01:27:37.980 --> 01:27:40.290
These are neuromodulators that tend to make us feel

2295
01:27:40.290 --> 01:27:42.460
a little bit calmer and they disrupt,

2296
01:27:42.460 --> 01:27:44.200
or I should say they alter

2297
01:27:44.200 --> 01:27:46.030
because it's not a pathological shift,

2298
01:27:46.030 --> 01:27:48.660
but they alter a perception of space and time

2299
01:27:48.660 --> 01:27:51.170
in a way that allows us to make mental associations

2300
01:27:51.170 --> 01:27:52.130
that are a little bit looser.

2301
01:27:52.130 --> 01:27:53.110
They can be non-linear.

2302
01:27:53.110 --> 01:27:55.580
So that's a time that's excellent for creative work

2303
01:27:55.580 --> 01:27:56.910
or for brainstorming.

2304
01:27:56.910 --> 01:27:59.550
And you might notice that when you're a little bit fatigued,

2305
01:27:59.550 --> 01:28:02.510
your mind is a little freer and if you're very fatigued,

2306
01:28:02.510 --> 01:28:04.310
your mind is all over the place.

2307
01:28:04.310 --> 01:28:06.360
So one thing that I think has been missing

2308
01:28:06.360 --> 01:28:08.272
from the brain state work

2309
01:28:08.272 --> 01:28:10.600
has been a kind of cohesive framework.

2310
01:28:10.600 --> 01:28:13.200
And yet everyone, I think every neuroscientist would agree

2311
01:28:13.200 --> 01:28:15.130
that the 24 hour cycle really governs,

2312
01:28:15.130 --> 01:28:17.040
not just digestion and bodily temperature,

2313
01:28:17.040 --> 01:28:19.060
'cause it does temperature is higher in the morning,

2314
01:28:19.060 --> 01:28:20.530
rising, rising, rising to the afternoon,

2315
01:28:20.530 --> 01:28:21.363
then starts coming down,

2316
01:28:21.363 --> 01:28:23.030
and then you go into sleep in phase three

2317
01:28:23.030 --> 01:28:24.870
'cause your body temperature is lowered.

2318
01:28:24.870 --> 01:28:28.220
So those three phases really drive our brain states

2319
01:28:28.220 --> 01:28:29.760
and one precedes the other.

2320
01:28:29.760 --> 01:28:30.593
This is the key thing.

2321
01:28:30.593 --> 01:28:32.730
We can never look at a state in isolation,

2322
01:28:32.730 --> 01:28:34.820
but if you wanted to understand, for instance,

2323
01:28:34.820 --> 01:28:37.160
how to access a state of heightened creativity,

2324
01:28:37.160 --> 01:28:39.320
you might try placing it in phase two of the day.

2325
01:28:39.320 --> 01:28:41.540
So 10 to 16 hours after you've been awake

2326
01:28:41.540 --> 01:28:42.650
and see how that works for you

2327
01:28:42.650 --> 01:28:45.340
and pay attention to your state of mind

2328
01:28:45.340 --> 01:28:47.730
before you at attempt to go into that state,

2329
01:28:47.730 --> 01:28:49.650
and as you exit that state.

2330
01:28:49.650 --> 01:28:51.350
And so I think that if we've learned anything

2331
01:28:51.350 --> 01:28:53.070
from the sleep science

2332
01:28:53.070 --> 01:28:56.510
it's that we can't look at one state of mind in isolation,

2333
01:28:56.510 --> 01:28:59.050
we need to ask what preceded it and what follows it

2334
01:28:59.050 --> 01:29:00.620
in order to understand its structure

2335
01:29:00.620 --> 01:29:02.460
and how to access it better.

2336
01:29:02.460 --> 01:29:03.388
At the same time,

2337
01:29:03.388 --> 01:29:06.660
human beings do their best to,

2338
01:29:06.660 --> 01:29:10.120
fuck with this by sugar, caffeine, alcohol,

2339
01:29:10.120 --> 01:29:12.853
like we're trying to perpetuate a certain state,

2340
01:29:14.230 --> 01:29:17.960
where we feel the best or we're the most productive

2341
01:29:17.960 --> 01:29:22.943
and that disrupts this natural like sort of preset

2342
01:29:24.110 --> 01:29:26.330
regarding the circadian rhythm

2343
01:29:26.330 --> 01:29:29.040
and the kind of hormonal regulatory balance

2344
01:29:29.040 --> 01:29:33.047
that we would experience without all of these inputs

2345
01:29:33.047 --> 01:29:34.760
that are screwing us up,

2346
01:29:34.760 --> 01:29:36.500
and impairing our sleep,

2347
01:29:36.500 --> 01:29:40.317
and ultimately have short-term gains obviously,

2348
01:29:41.910 --> 01:29:45.880
or we wouldn't do it but long term problems.

2349
01:29:45.880 --> 01:29:46.713
Yeah.

2350
01:29:46.713 --> 01:29:50.250
I think that there are basic things that if I may,

2351
01:29:50.250 --> 01:29:53.277
everyone should do in order to anchor their body

2352
01:29:53.277 --> 01:29:57.250
and their mind in the most productive rhythm

2353
01:29:57.250 --> 01:29:58.320
that they possibly can.

2354
01:29:58.320 --> 01:30:01.080
And the reason I feel comfortable saying this is that

2355
01:30:01.080 --> 01:30:03.060
we were not designed to be nocturnal,

2356
01:30:03.060 --> 01:30:04.610
we are capable of nocturnal activity.

2357
01:30:04.610 --> 01:30:07.780
And I wanna just give a nod to shift workers

2358
01:30:07.780 --> 01:30:09.210
and of all kinds that have to work

2359
01:30:09.210 --> 01:30:10.180
in the middle of the night.

2360
01:30:10.180 --> 01:30:12.340
I mean, that's the way the world works nowadays.

2361
01:30:12.340 --> 01:30:14.130
And there are a whole set of things

2362
01:30:14.130 --> 01:30:15.990
that shift workers suffer from;

2363
01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:18.060
digestive issues, mental health issues,

2364
01:30:18.060 --> 01:30:21.610
I mean, they're prone to more cardiac events and suicides.

2365
01:30:21.610 --> 01:30:24.450
I mean, it's obvious that shift work,

2366
01:30:24.450 --> 01:30:26.760
especially swing shift work

2367
01:30:26.760 --> 01:30:27.950
where people are alternating

2368
01:30:27.950 --> 01:30:29.730
their schedule pretty frequently

2369
01:30:29.730 --> 01:30:32.130
is extremely detrimental to brain and body.

2370
01:30:32.130 --> 01:30:33.560
And there are a number of things that people can do

2371
01:30:33.560 --> 01:30:34.393
to offset that.

2372
01:30:34.393 --> 01:30:36.210
I've done an episode of the podcast on that.

2373
01:30:36.210 --> 01:30:37.420
And I do some work with communities

2374
01:30:37.420 --> 01:30:39.110
that are restricted to shift work

2375
01:30:39.110 --> 01:30:40.700
to try and help offset that.

2376
01:30:40.700 --> 01:30:43.110
But for most people it's going to be heading to sleep

2377
01:30:43.110 --> 01:30:45.170
somewhere between 9:00 PM and midnight

2378
01:30:45.170 --> 01:30:49.120
and waking up somewhere between 5:00 AM and 9:00 AM.

2379
01:30:49.120 --> 01:30:49.953
I would think.

2380
01:30:49.953 --> 01:30:51.210
I mean, I'm thinking the college students

2381
01:30:51.210 --> 01:30:52.900
and my college years or sleeping in.

2382
01:30:52.900 --> 01:30:57.900
But there's tremendous value in anchoring one's biology

2383
01:30:58.930 --> 01:31:00.360
to this 24 hour cycle.

2384
01:31:00.360 --> 01:31:02.520
And it's very simple as to how to do that.

2385
01:31:02.520 --> 01:31:05.350
I mean, the most dominant zeitgeber as we say,

2386
01:31:05.350 --> 01:31:07.840
timekeeper that governs all these states,

2387
01:31:07.840 --> 01:31:09.930
including sleep is light.

2388
01:31:09.930 --> 01:31:11.250
There's no question about it.

2389
01:31:11.250 --> 01:31:12.720
And you ask any circadian biologists

2390
01:31:12.720 --> 01:31:16.270
to say there's a kind of a principle of circadian biology

2391
01:31:16.270 --> 01:31:17.827
which is that light is the dominant zeitgeber,

2392
01:31:17.827 --> 01:31:20.000
called zeitgeber 'cause the people who define this

2393
01:31:20.000 --> 01:31:20.833
were German.

2394
01:31:20.833 --> 01:31:21.970
And I'm pronouncing it incorrectly

2395
01:31:21.970 --> 01:31:23.420
because I don't speak German.

2396
01:31:25.350 --> 01:31:29.000
It's clear that if possible one should view sunlight

2397
01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:30.210
or at least get outside,

2398
01:31:30.210 --> 01:31:34.530
even if it's cloudy outside within the first hour of waking.

2399
01:31:34.530 --> 01:31:36.950
And if you wake up before the sun comes out

2400
01:31:36.950 --> 01:31:39.350
to flip on as many artificial lights as possible,

2401
01:31:39.350 --> 01:31:40.710
if your goal is to be awake,

2402
01:31:40.710 --> 01:31:41.780
if your goal is to go back asleep,

2403
01:31:41.780 --> 01:31:43.470
definitely keep the light dim.

2404
01:31:43.470 --> 01:31:46.130
But getting light in your eyes even through cloud cover

2405
01:31:46.130 --> 01:31:47.803
is going to set in motion

2406
01:31:47.803 --> 01:31:50.540
a number of different biological events,

2407
01:31:50.540 --> 01:31:52.500
read out first through certain cells in the eyes;

2408
01:31:52.500 --> 01:31:54.330
these so-called melanopsin and intrinsically

2409
01:31:54.330 --> 01:31:56.380
photosensitive ganglion cells as the fancy name,

2410
01:31:56.380 --> 01:31:59.190
they were discovered by David Berson at Brown University.

2411
01:31:59.190 --> 01:32:00.861
And they send a signal to your hypothalamus

2412
01:32:00.861 --> 01:32:03.700
which then sets in motion a huge number

2413
01:32:03.700 --> 01:32:05.960
of hormonal and neural events in the body.

2414
01:32:05.960 --> 01:32:08.580
They create a cortisol pulse that wakes you up further,

2415
01:32:08.580 --> 01:32:12.360
sets the baseline for your attention throughout the day,

2416
01:32:12.360 --> 01:32:14.890
also sets a timer of about 16 hours,

2417
01:32:14.890 --> 01:32:17.940
not coincidentally on your first melatonin pulse,

2418
01:32:17.940 --> 01:32:18.950
which will happen in the evening.

2419
01:32:18.950 --> 01:32:21.240
So it's timing when your sleep is going to come.

2420
01:32:21.240 --> 01:32:23.980
People who stay indoors in dark environments

2421
01:32:23.980 --> 01:32:25.000
for the early part of the day,

2422
01:32:25.000 --> 01:32:25.950
just looking at their phone,

2423
01:32:25.950 --> 01:32:28.710
you're not getting enough light stimulation.

2424
01:32:28.710 --> 01:32:32.280
The tricky thing is that later in the evening,

2425
01:32:32.280 --> 01:32:36.810
about 17 hours after you've been awake or when you woke up,

2426
01:32:36.810 --> 01:32:39.010
what you'll find is that even a minimum of light

2427
01:32:39.010 --> 01:32:41.520
from screens can shift your circadian clock.

2428
01:32:41.520 --> 01:32:43.290
And the reason is that there's a gradual shift

2429
01:32:43.290 --> 01:32:46.070
in the sensitivity of the retina to external light.

2430
01:32:46.070 --> 01:32:47.480
So you need a lot of light,

2431
01:32:47.480 --> 01:32:49.253
ideally sunlight early in the day,

2432
01:32:50.170 --> 01:32:52.390
and of course, you don't wanna stare directly at any light

2433
01:32:52.390 --> 01:32:53.570
that's so bright that it's painful,

2434
01:32:53.570 --> 01:32:55.240
you don't have to look directly into the sun,

2435
01:32:55.240 --> 01:32:56.080
absolutely blank.

2436
01:32:56.080 --> 01:32:57.730
You can be kind of off center from the sun,

2437
01:32:57.730 --> 01:33:00.360
but try and get out there without sunglasses if you can.

2438
01:33:00.360 --> 01:33:01.800
People always ask,

2439
01:33:01.800 --> 01:33:03.800
can I wear eyeglasses or contacts?

2440
01:33:03.800 --> 01:33:04.633
And I say,

2441
01:33:04.633 --> 01:33:06.100
absolutely because that actually will focus the light

2442
01:33:06.100 --> 01:33:06.933
to your retina.

2443
01:33:06.933 --> 01:33:08.580
And that's what they're designed to do.

2444
01:33:08.580 --> 01:33:10.110
Through a window or a windshield

2445
01:33:10.110 --> 01:33:12.140
simply will not work as well

2446
01:33:12.140 --> 01:33:14.180
because of the filtration of the wavelengths of light

2447
01:33:14.180 --> 01:33:15.013
that you want.

2448
01:33:15.013 --> 01:33:17.933
And then there's a lot of data to support the fact

2449
01:33:17.933 --> 01:33:19.970
that getting as much bright light in your eyes

2450
01:33:19.970 --> 01:33:23.110
throughout the day provided it's not painfully bright

2451
01:33:23.110 --> 01:33:25.170
is excellent for your wakefulness mechanisms,

2452
01:33:25.170 --> 01:33:27.010
and even for the mechanisms of the brain and body

2453
01:33:27.010 --> 01:33:30.100
that control metabolism and feeding, mood and wellbeing.

2454
01:33:30.100 --> 01:33:31.900
This is the work mainly of Samer Hattar

2455
01:33:31.900 --> 01:33:33.490
at the National Institutes of Mental Health.

2456
01:33:33.490 --> 01:33:36.240
He's the Head of the Chronobiology unit

2457
01:33:36.240 --> 01:33:37.860
at the National Institutes of Mental Health.

2458
01:33:37.860 --> 01:33:38.820
So Samer would say,

2459
01:33:38.820 --> 01:33:40.470
get as much bright light in your eyes as you can

2460
01:33:40.470 --> 01:33:41.930
early in the day and throughout the day,

2461
01:33:41.930 --> 01:33:44.290
and then somewhere close to bedtime,

2462
01:33:44.290 --> 01:33:46.620
trough that and live in the darkest environment

2463
01:33:46.620 --> 01:33:48.410
you possibly can manage safely.

2464
01:33:48.410 --> 01:33:49.510
He's not a big fan of being

2465
01:33:49.510 --> 01:33:53.070
in completely light absent environments,

2466
01:33:53.070 --> 01:33:57.430
but that simple behavior of early light viewing most days,

2467
01:33:57.430 --> 01:33:58.556
if not all days sets in motion

2468
01:33:58.556 --> 01:34:01.145
these three phases that lend themselves best

2469
01:34:01.145 --> 01:34:04.760
to focused work, creative work, the ability to stay awake

2470
01:34:04.760 --> 01:34:05.680
most of the day.

2471
01:34:05.680 --> 01:34:07.230
Naps are fine we know,

2472
01:34:07.230 --> 01:34:09.710
provided they don't interfere with nighttime sleep.

2473
01:34:09.710 --> 01:34:11.850
So naps should be shorter than 90 minutes.

2474
01:34:11.850 --> 01:34:15.000
But if a nap of any duration prevents you from falling

2475
01:34:15.000 --> 01:34:18.090
and staying asleep at night, then you don't want to nap.

2476
01:34:18.090 --> 01:34:19.810
So light is the dominant way

2477
01:34:19.810 --> 01:34:23.050
that you set this whole process in motion.

2478
01:34:23.050 --> 01:34:25.930
And we know based on work that I wasn't involved in,

2479
01:34:25.930 --> 01:34:28.860
but that David Spiegel did with the great Robert Sapolsky,

2480
01:34:28.860 --> 01:34:32.900
that if these cortisol pulse doesn't arrive early enough

2481
01:34:32.900 --> 01:34:35.947
in the day and starts arriving in the afternoon,

2482
01:34:35.947 --> 01:34:40.100
that's a physiological signature of depression and anxiety.

2483
01:34:40.100 --> 01:34:42.370
So that cortisol pulse is happening one way or a another

2484
01:34:42.370 --> 01:34:44.820
and you wanna anchor to the early part of the day.

2485
01:34:44.820 --> 01:34:46.390
And so if you're feeling wide awake,

2486
01:34:46.390 --> 01:34:49.260
anxious in the sort of wired and tired late in the day,

2487
01:34:49.260 --> 01:34:51.140
chances are, you're not getting enough sunlight

2488
01:34:51.140 --> 01:34:52.720
early in the day and throughout the day.

2489
01:34:52.720 --> 01:34:53.553
Right.

2490
01:34:53.553 --> 01:34:54.950
So the delayed exposure to sunlight

2491
01:34:54.950 --> 01:34:56.220
in the early part of the day

2492
01:34:56.220 --> 01:34:58.560
can lead to that kind of depressed state.

2493
01:34:58.560 --> 01:34:59.393
Absolutely.

2494
01:34:59.393 --> 01:35:02.660
And it seems like it's an easy fix to ensure

2495
01:35:02.660 --> 01:35:06.210
that you're exposed to sunlight in an adequate way

2496
01:35:06.210 --> 01:35:08.060
in the early part of the day.

2497
01:35:08.060 --> 01:35:12.420
The end of the day part is harder in our modern lives.

2498
01:35:12.420 --> 01:35:13.740
We could do things with our screens

2499
01:35:13.740 --> 01:35:14.900
and make sure they're off.

2500
01:35:14.900 --> 01:35:18.070
Is there a qualitative difference between the light

2501
01:35:18.070 --> 01:35:20.780
that's being emitted from the screens that we're looking at

2502
01:35:20.780 --> 01:35:23.975
versus the overhead lights in our indoor environments?

2503
01:35:23.975 --> 01:35:27.480
We're just in light until it's time to flick the light off

2504
01:35:27.480 --> 01:35:28.640
and close our eyes.

2505
01:35:28.640 --> 01:35:29.740
Yeah, it's a great question.

2506
01:35:29.740 --> 01:35:32.820
One of the things that I think can be very useful,

2507
01:35:32.820 --> 01:35:34.900
again, is anchored in the biology of the system,

2508
01:35:34.900 --> 01:35:37.600
is that so the melanopsin cells reside mostly,

2509
01:35:37.600 --> 01:35:40.200
not exclusively in the lower half of the retina

2510
01:35:40.200 --> 01:35:41.770
and they view the upper visual field.

2511
01:35:41.770 --> 01:35:43.160
That makes sense because these were cells

2512
