And what's interesting is even if I travel

2616
01:39:42.890 --> 01:39:47.890
to a warm climate and it's tropical or whatever,

2617
01:39:48.870 --> 01:39:50.780
it's still that time of year

2618
01:39:50.780 --> 01:39:51.980
where the days are short,

2619
01:39:51.980 --> 01:39:55.410
unless I go below to go to Australia or South America

2620
01:39:55.410 --> 01:39:56.680
or something like that.

2621
01:39:56.680 --> 01:39:59.470
And so it only ameliorates it in part,

2622
01:39:59.470 --> 01:40:01.430
even though I'm in a warm environment,

2623
01:40:01.430 --> 01:40:04.410
I still kind of can feel that

2624
01:40:06.300 --> 01:40:09.330
washed over me until the days start getting longer.

2625
01:40:09.330 --> 01:40:10.163
Yeah.

2626
01:40:10.163 --> 01:40:11.460
Well, there are of couple mechanisms for this.

2627
01:40:11.460 --> 01:40:13.770
First of all, the dopamine system,

2628
01:40:13.770 --> 01:40:14.660
it does many things in the;

2629
01:40:14.660 --> 01:40:15.790
body responsible for movement,

2630
01:40:15.790 --> 01:40:17.410
motivation, craving, et cetera.

2631
01:40:17.410 --> 01:40:19.550
But dopamine is very strongly regulated

2632
01:40:19.550 --> 01:40:21.410
by seasonality in a lot of animals.

2633
01:40:21.410 --> 01:40:24.037
In fact, for the animal lovers out there

2634
01:40:24.037 --> 01:40:27.610
and the wildlife lovers of which I am,

2635
01:40:27.610 --> 01:40:29.258
the shift in pelage color,

2636
01:40:29.258 --> 01:40:33.380
that animals will have white or very pale fur

2637
01:40:33.380 --> 01:40:34.700
in one time of the year

2638
01:40:34.700 --> 01:40:35.860
and then it gets darker

2639
01:40:35.860 --> 01:40:39.740
is actually due to a light regulated shift in dopamine.

2640
01:40:39.740 --> 01:40:42.610
So when it turns out that when days are short

2641
01:40:42.610 --> 01:40:44.780
and nights are long, levels of dopamine are reduced,

2642
01:40:44.780 --> 01:40:47.640
this probably also explains the reduced mood

2643
01:40:47.640 --> 01:40:50.350
that you experience to some extent or another.

2644
01:40:50.350 --> 01:40:53.640
And dopamine is in this tyrosinase pathway,

2645
01:40:53.640 --> 01:40:54.630
people can look this up,

2646
01:40:54.630 --> 01:40:57.960
that is upstream of the melanin pathway

2647
01:40:57.960 --> 01:41:00.631
and as a consequence, pelage becomes white.

2648
01:41:00.631 --> 01:41:02.400
In the spring and summer months

2649
01:41:02.400 --> 01:41:04.530
when animals are getting more sun,

2650
01:41:04.530 --> 01:41:06.340
there's an activation through the eyes,

2651
01:41:06.340 --> 01:41:08.807
again, activates the dopamine system,

2652
01:41:08.807 --> 01:41:11.690
the dopamine system then triggers the melanin system

2653
01:41:11.690 --> 01:41:14.450
to produce darkening of the pelage, right?

2654
01:41:14.450 --> 01:41:16.570
And there's all sorts of amplification

2655
01:41:16.570 --> 01:41:18.020
in seasonally breeding animals

2656
01:41:18.020 --> 01:41:20.506
where animals testes involute, it's a horrible term,

2657
01:41:20.506 --> 01:41:22.890
but they involute in the winter

2658
01:41:22.890 --> 01:41:24.460
and ovaries will involute too.

2659
01:41:24.460 --> 01:41:27.520
In other words, they shrink a species of hamster actually

2660
01:41:27.520 --> 01:41:28.990
where the Siberian hamster,

2661
01:41:28.990 --> 01:41:30.970
where it's testes are about the size of a grain of rice

2662
01:41:30.970 --> 01:41:31.880
during the winter,

2663
01:41:31.880 --> 01:41:35.100
and then they increased by more than a thousand fold

2664
01:41:35.100 --> 01:41:36.300
in the spring or summer.
That's so crazy.

2665
01:41:36.300 --> 01:41:37.660
So there's these massive shifts.

2666
01:41:37.660 --> 01:41:40.225
Now, humans are not restricted to season only breeding.

2667
01:41:40.225 --> 01:41:42.550
I mean, after all I was born in September,

2668
01:41:42.550 --> 01:41:44.395
so that doesn't work

2669
01:41:44.395 --> 01:41:46.340
'cause I'm imagining if all things

2670
01:41:46.340 --> 01:41:48.090
still work the way they they do now

2671
01:41:48.090 --> 01:41:49.546
that I was conceived sometime in December.

2672
01:41:49.546 --> 01:41:50.487
There's a multitude of factors.

2673
01:41:50.487 --> 01:41:51.320
Yeah.

2674
01:41:51.320 --> 01:41:52.153
I don't know the story about conception

2675
01:41:52.153 --> 01:41:53.940
nor do I want to know,

2676
01:41:53.940 --> 01:41:58.170
but nonetheless humans are subject to more subtle mood

2677
01:41:58.170 --> 01:42:01.410
based variations and physiology shifts across the year.

2678
01:42:01.410 --> 01:42:03.210
And the reason is the following:

2679
01:42:03.210 --> 01:42:04.043
Light comes in,

2680
01:42:04.043 --> 01:42:06.670
triggers these intrinsically photosensitive cells,

2681
01:42:06.670 --> 01:42:09.690
then trigger a pathway that eventually converges

2682
01:42:09.690 --> 01:42:11.860
not just on the circadian clock and the hypothalamus,

2683
01:42:11.860 --> 01:42:13.380
but on the pineal gland.

2684
01:42:13.380 --> 01:42:16.360
And the pineal is responsible for secretion of melatonin.

2685
01:42:16.360 --> 01:42:20.170
So when days are long because light inhibits melatonin,

2686
01:42:20.170 --> 01:42:21.390
the pulse of melatonin,

2687
01:42:21.390 --> 01:42:23.980
the duration of your melatonin pulse as we call it

2688
01:42:23.980 --> 01:42:25.460
is very short,

2689
01:42:25.460 --> 01:42:28.840
when days are short and nights are very long

2690
01:42:28.840 --> 01:42:30.900
there's less light to inhibit melatonin,

2691
01:42:30.900 --> 01:42:33.570
and so there's a long melatonin signal.

2692
01:42:33.570 --> 01:42:35.570
The melatonin know it does a number of things,

2693
01:42:35.570 --> 01:42:37.160
it makes you sleepy, et cetera,

2694
01:42:37.160 --> 01:42:38.840
it's gonna make you feel a little bit more subdued,

2695
01:42:38.840 --> 01:42:41.600
but it has a number of downstream effects as well.

2696
01:42:41.600 --> 01:42:43.000
So what I would suggest for people

2697
01:42:43.000 --> 01:42:47.390
that experience moderate seasonal depression

2698
01:42:47.390 --> 01:42:49.150
would be to really emphasize

2699
01:42:49.150 --> 01:42:50.840
trying to get as much bright light in your eyes

2700
01:42:50.840 --> 01:42:53.340
as you can outside, and if appropriate,

2701
01:42:53.340 --> 01:42:54.960
there's a study out of Israel that shows

2702
01:42:54.960 --> 01:42:58.020
that exposing as much of your bodily surface to light

2703
01:42:58.020 --> 01:42:59.480
actually can be useful too.

2704
01:42:59.480 --> 01:43:00.500
And for the following reason,

2705
01:43:00.500 --> 01:43:03.270
we think of skin as just this barrier to outside stuff,

2706
01:43:03.270 --> 01:43:06.020
which it is, but it's actually an endocrine organ.

2707
01:43:06.020 --> 01:43:07.330
It can secrete hormones

2708
01:43:07.330 --> 01:43:10.080
through where it can direct the secretion of hormones.

2709
01:43:10.080 --> 01:43:12.320
There's a pathway involving something called P57,

2710
01:43:12.320 --> 01:43:13.650
and it's a beautiful paper

2711
01:43:13.650 --> 01:43:17.050
that described how people who get two of upper body,

2712
01:43:17.050 --> 01:43:20.490
most of upper body sunlight exposure

2713
01:43:20.490 --> 01:43:24.340
have greatly increased levels of both testosterone

2714
01:43:24.340 --> 01:43:26.020
and estrogen in the appropriate ratios

2715
01:43:26.020 --> 01:43:27.410
in men and women, et cetera,

2716
01:43:27.410 --> 01:43:31.340
and improved mood, and improved libido, improved metabolism.

2717
01:43:31.340 --> 01:43:33.570
The metabolism study is kind of a separate study,

2718
01:43:33.570 --> 01:43:34.460
but nonetheless,

2719
01:43:34.460 --> 01:43:36.650
it's not just about getting sunlight of on your eyes,

2720
01:43:36.650 --> 01:43:38.080
it's also about getting it onto your skin,

2721
01:43:38.080 --> 01:43:40.600
which typically we only do in the spring and summer months.

2722
01:43:40.600 --> 01:43:41.950
So again, if appropriate,

2723
01:43:41.950 --> 01:43:44.530
try and expose as much of yourself to sunlight as possible.

2724
01:43:44.530 --> 01:43:47.160
And I should say, no, this is not a reinforcement

2725
01:43:47.160 --> 01:43:48.900
for this trend that was taking over Instagram

2726
01:43:48.900 --> 01:43:51.950
a few years ago of sunning every orifice on your body.

2727
01:43:51.950 --> 01:43:53.150
That was more, I think about...

2728
01:43:53.150 --> 01:43:54.270
I think we all know what you're talking about.

2729
01:43:54.270 --> 01:43:57.080
That was more of a subculture phenomenon,

2730
01:43:57.080 --> 01:43:58.380
and as far as I know,

2731
01:43:58.380 --> 01:44:00.248
there are no laboratory data to support that.

2732
01:44:00.248 --> 01:44:03.098
That would be a study that would be hard to get approved.

2733
01:44:04.010 --> 01:44:06.279
I wanna switch gears here a little bit

2734
01:44:06.279 --> 01:44:09.760
and talk about performance states.

2735
01:44:09.760 --> 01:44:13.030
I feel like as an athlete,

2736
01:44:13.030 --> 01:44:14.580
I was taught certain things

2737
01:44:14.580 --> 01:44:18.870
and I just naturally developed my own habits

2738
01:44:18.870 --> 01:44:22.740
around getting into an optimal performance state.

2739
01:44:22.740 --> 01:44:25.100
Visualization has always been a tool,

2740
01:44:25.100 --> 01:44:27.810
but also intentional breathing practices,

2741
01:44:27.810 --> 01:44:30.100
without any instruction, it's just you watch people,

2742
01:44:30.100 --> 01:44:32.850
for example, in swimming behind the blocks at the Olympics,

2743
01:44:32.850 --> 01:44:34.780
they're all taking deep breaths,

2744
01:44:34.780 --> 01:44:37.270
it's almost as if their body is telling 'em

2745
01:44:37.270 --> 01:44:40.350
what they need to do to induce a state

2746
01:44:40.350 --> 01:44:43.090
in which they can perform at their absolute peak.

2747
01:44:43.090 --> 01:44:47.050
But I've also been in situations where now I go on stage

2748
01:44:47.050 --> 01:44:49.590
to give a talk and sometimes I'm very relaxed

2749
01:44:49.590 --> 01:44:50.423
and it goes great,

2750
01:44:50.423 --> 01:44:52.830
and other times I'm hyperventilating,

2751
01:44:52.830 --> 01:44:54.680
and I'm sweating, and I'm nervous,

2752
01:44:54.680 --> 01:44:57.730
and I can't catch my breath, and I can't get a word out.

2753
01:44:57.730 --> 01:45:01.870
Like I've exceeded the performance state

2754
01:45:01.870 --> 01:45:05.100
and I'm in a state of induced panic, right?

2755
01:45:05.100 --> 01:45:07.890
So what are some practices, 'cause everybody,

2756
01:45:07.890 --> 01:45:09.810
whether you have to give a presentation at work,

2757
01:45:09.810 --> 01:45:11.160
or make a difficult phone call,

2758
01:45:11.160 --> 01:45:13.140
or have an uncomfortable conversation,

2759
01:45:13.140 --> 01:45:15.880
we all have moments in our life

2760
01:45:15.880 --> 01:45:18.868
where we wanna kind of execute to the best of our abilities.

2761
01:45:18.868 --> 01:45:21.555
And I know that there's many things

2762
01:45:21.555 --> 01:45:24.460
that we can do to kind of ensure

2763
01:45:24.460 --> 01:45:25.960
that we're in the best position

2764
01:45:25.960 --> 01:45:27.550
for the outcome that we seek.

2765
01:45:27.550 --> 01:45:28.800
Yeah.

2766
01:45:28.800 --> 01:45:30.250
Before I answer, I just have to ask,

2767
01:45:30.250 --> 01:45:33.986
is there any correlation with what you described

2768
01:45:33.986 --> 01:45:36.186
with how well you've slept the night before?

2769
01:45:37.330 --> 01:45:38.163
Oh, a 100%.

2770
01:45:38.163 --> 01:45:41.910
Like when I sleep really well, I'm alert, but I'm calm.

2771
01:45:41.910 --> 01:45:46.550
I have a sense of confidence that comes easy,

2772
01:45:46.550 --> 01:45:48.540
words come easy, thoughts come easy,

2773
01:45:48.540 --> 01:45:50.930
my memory is better, my recall, all of that.

2774
01:45:50.930 --> 01:45:52.250
If I don't sleep well,

2775
01:45:52.250 --> 01:45:55.240
and often I don't particularly when I have to give a speech

2776
01:45:55.240 --> 01:45:56.220
in front of a lot of people

2777
01:45:56.220 --> 01:45:57.907
because that induces a lot of anxiety in me

2778
01:45:57.907 --> 01:45:59.670
and that I don't rest well.

2779
01:45:59.670 --> 01:46:02.590
And then I wake up and I'm already off my game,

2780
01:46:02.590 --> 01:46:05.300
and then I'm in some sort of damage control.

2781
01:46:05.300 --> 01:46:08.170
And often I find myself in a vicious circle

2782
01:46:08.170 --> 01:46:09.230
that just makes it worse

2783
01:46:09.230 --> 01:46:11.090
and then there I am on stage

2784
01:46:11.090 --> 01:46:14.720
and just delivering a C-minus performance.

2785
01:46:14.720 --> 01:46:15.582
Although I doubt it.

2786
01:46:15.582 --> 01:46:17.229
(both chattering)

2787
01:46:17.229 --> 01:46:18.490
You're your own toughest critic.

2788
01:46:18.490 --> 01:46:19.966
Definitely.

2789
01:46:19.966 --> 01:46:24.190
I have a lot to learn when it comes to that art form.

2790
01:46:24.190 --> 01:46:25.220
You're an excellent speaker.

2791
01:46:25.220 --> 01:46:27.800
I hear the Stanford student in you

2792
01:46:27.800 --> 01:46:29.660
and very self critical.

2793
01:46:29.660 --> 01:46:32.362
But look, self criticism is also the gateway

2794
01:46:32.362 --> 01:46:34.713
to high performance over time, right?

2795
01:46:36.250 --> 01:46:39.230
Okay, so in terms of thinking about accessing states

2796
01:46:39.230 --> 01:46:42.083
and self-directing states for performance,

2797
01:46:43.160 --> 01:46:44.490
we should probably return to this notion

2798
01:46:44.490 --> 01:46:45.670
of the autonomic seesaw,

2799
01:46:45.670 --> 01:46:50.670
because we are all slaves to this process of alert but calm,

2800
01:46:50.740 --> 01:46:53.370
or stressed, or sleepy, et cetera.

2801
01:46:53.370 --> 01:46:55.420
The reason I asked about sleep is that

2802
01:46:55.420 --> 01:46:57.080
the way to think about the autonomic nervous system

2803
01:46:57.080 --> 01:46:59.287
is that it's not truly autonomic.

2804
01:46:59.287 --> 01:47:01.180
And autonomic means automatic.

2805
01:47:01.180 --> 01:47:03.320
We actually can exert some control over it.

2806
01:47:03.320 --> 01:47:06.110
We can have certain kinds of conversations and not others.

2807
01:47:06.110 --> 01:47:08.440
We can breathe faster or more slowly.

2808
01:47:08.440 --> 01:47:10.460
As we talked about before you can use your visual system

2809
01:47:10.460 --> 01:47:11.293
in a number of ways.

2810
01:47:11.293 --> 01:47:13.630
So we can exert control over this thing

2811
01:47:13.630 --> 01:47:15.230
that we're calling the autonomic nervous system

2812
01:47:15.230 --> 01:47:16.550
or the seesaw.

2813
01:47:16.550 --> 01:47:18.470
Perhaps a better way to visualize it is that

2814
01:47:18.470 --> 01:47:22.240
you're sort of like a, imagine yourself as a stick figure,

2815
01:47:22.240 --> 01:47:24.910
that's you on the autonomic seesaw

2816
01:47:24.910 --> 01:47:28.390
and under well rested, well prepared conditions,

2817
01:47:28.390 --> 01:47:30.490
you can move by back and forth across that seesaw,

2818
01:47:30.490 --> 01:47:33.163
you can kind of surf that seesaw all day long.

2819
01:47:34.030 --> 01:47:37.000
And the hinge on that seesaw so to speak

2820
01:47:37.000 --> 01:47:39.730
is of the appropriate tightness and looseness

2821
01:47:39.730 --> 01:47:41.510
so that makes it very easy.

2822
01:47:41.510 --> 01:47:42.980
When we're sleep deprived,

2823
01:47:42.980 --> 01:47:44.886
there's a tendency for us to find

2824
01:47:44.886 --> 01:47:47.130
that getting from one end of the seesaw to the other

2825
01:47:47.130 --> 01:47:48.470
is a little bit more strenuous.

2826
01:47:48.470 --> 01:47:49.510
And I've talked to Matt Walker

2827
01:47:49.510 --> 01:47:51.240
about some of the underlying circuits

2828
01:47:51.240 --> 01:47:53.565
that might reflect the hinge might actually be,

2829
01:47:53.565 --> 01:47:55.150
and that's something that we might collaborate on

2830
01:47:55.150 --> 01:47:56.210
in the future.

2831
01:47:56.210 --> 01:47:58.640
But it's very clear that when we're well rested,

2832
01:47:58.640 --> 01:48:00.670
we can surf this seesaw pretty well.

2833
01:48:00.670 --> 01:48:02.990
We feel stressed and we can kind of take a deep breath,

2834
01:48:02.990 --> 01:48:04.210
calm ourselves, tell ourselves,

2835
01:48:04.210 --> 01:48:07.570
okay, that person's nuts or that person is,

2836
01:48:07.570 --> 01:48:08.510
I'll deal with that later.

2837
01:48:08.510 --> 01:48:12.980
But when we're sleep deprived we compromise our ability

2838
01:48:12.980 --> 01:48:14.220
to do this.

2839
01:48:14.220 --> 01:48:15.540
Okay.

2840
01:48:15.540 --> 01:48:17.520
Regardless of whether or not you're well rested or not,

2841
01:48:17.520 --> 01:48:19.960
it's clear that there's a small handful of tools

2842
01:48:19.960 --> 01:48:23.570
that can allow one to direct their movement

2843
01:48:23.570 --> 01:48:25.740
along the seesaw more efficiently.

2844
01:48:25.740 --> 01:48:27.370
The first one is one that we talked about before

2845
01:48:27.370 --> 01:48:29.270
which is the physiological sigh.

2846
01:48:29.270 --> 01:48:32.240
And I'm not here to promote....

2847
01:48:32.240 --> 01:48:33.958
First of all, I didn't discover it.

2848
01:48:33.958 --> 01:48:36.020
I don't own it.

2849
01:48:36.020 --> 01:48:38.500
It was Jack Feldman that really understood

2850
01:48:38.500 --> 01:48:39.560
the neural machinery,

2851
01:48:39.560 --> 01:48:41.070
but physiological sighs have been known about

2852
01:48:41.070 --> 01:48:42.550
since the 1930s,

2853
01:48:42.550 --> 01:48:46.210
as a way to reset the oxygen, CO2 balance in the system.

2854
01:48:46.210 --> 01:48:50.780
If ones feeling stressed or I should just say more alert

2855
01:48:50.780 --> 01:48:52.910
than one would like to be,

2856
01:48:52.910 --> 01:48:56.160
under any conditions physiological sighs

2857
01:48:56.160 --> 01:48:57.260
are at least to my knowledge

2858
01:48:57.260 --> 01:49:00.730
the fastest way to bring down that level of alertness.

2859
01:49:00.730 --> 01:49:03.080
And again, it's a full inhale through the nose

2860
01:49:03.080 --> 01:49:04.430
as deeply as you possibly can,

2861
01:49:04.430 --> 01:49:06.830
and then sneak in a little bit more air at the top,

2862
01:49:06.830 --> 01:49:09.430
that's gonna inflate the maximum number of alveoli

2863
01:49:09.430 --> 01:49:11.050
and then a long exhale

2864
01:49:12.660 --> 01:49:14.600
until basically you have no more air to exhale.

2865
01:49:14.600 --> 01:49:16.400
And how many times do you do that?

2866
01:49:16.400 --> 01:49:18.610
We find that just once is often enough,

2867
01:49:18.610 --> 01:49:20.760
but maybe three times maximum.

2868
01:49:20.760 --> 01:49:21.593
Now that's very different

2869
01:49:21.593 --> 01:49:23.623
than the five minute physiological sighing practice

2870
01:49:23.623 --> 01:49:24.710
that we were talking about before.

2871
01:49:24.710 --> 01:49:27.140
This is what I would call a real time tool.

2872
01:49:27.140 --> 01:49:29.250
And this is something that my lab has been really interested

2873
01:49:29.250 --> 01:49:31.100
in developing because so many of the things

2874
01:49:31.100 --> 01:49:34.140
that make us able to form well involved,

2875
01:49:34.140 --> 01:49:36.782
taking us out of the arena that we're in, it's meditation,

2876
01:49:36.782 --> 01:49:39.810
it's a massage, it's a great night's sleep,

2877
01:49:39.810 --> 01:49:41.503
it's quality social interaction and nutrition,

2878
01:49:41.503 --> 01:49:44.530
and of course, we should still all do all of those things

2879
01:49:44.530 --> 01:49:45.800
as often as we can,

2880
01:49:45.800 --> 01:49:47.166
but there's this double edged sword about,

2881
01:49:47.166 --> 01:49:48.060
or I should say,

2882
01:49:48.060 --> 01:49:50.550
there's this very diabolical aspect to stress

2883
01:49:50.550 --> 01:49:51.950
which is that when we are stressed,

2884
01:49:51.950 --> 01:49:54.016
oftentimes we can't remove ourself from the stress

2885
01:49:54.016 --> 01:49:56.260
or the thing that's stressing us.

2886
01:49:56.260 --> 01:49:59.640
And oftentimes we are compromised in our ability

2887
01:49:59.640 --> 01:50:02.940
to access the states of mind and good sleep

2888
01:50:02.940 --> 01:50:03.773
and all those sorts of things.

2889
01:50:03.773 --> 01:50:06.160
You even alluded to that in your description.

2890
01:50:06.160 --> 01:50:08.985
So physiological sighs are an excellent real time tool

2891
01:50:08.985 --> 01:50:12.050
for dealing with things in real time.

2892
01:50:12.050 --> 01:50:13.960
The other thing that's clearly useful

2893
01:50:13.960 --> 01:50:16.630
is for people to get comfortable

2894
01:50:16.630 --> 01:50:19.380
at high levels of autonomic arousal.

2895
01:50:19.380 --> 01:50:21.340
And in many ways

2896
01:50:21.340 --> 01:50:24.770
this could be thought of as raising one's stress threshold.

2897
01:50:24.770 --> 01:50:29.580
And here I have to give a nod wim hof type breathing,

2898
01:50:29.580 --> 01:50:32.450
also which is very similar to tumor type breathing,

2899
01:50:32.450 --> 01:50:33.330
which is very similar

2900
01:50:33.330 --> 01:50:35.910
to what we call cyclic hyperventilation in the lab.

2901
01:50:35.910 --> 01:50:37.193
But basically having a practice

2902
01:50:37.193 --> 01:50:39.340
that you do away from the event,

2903
01:50:39.340 --> 01:50:41.820
it doesn't necessarily have to be done every day,

2904
01:50:41.820 --> 01:50:44.530
but maybe three times a week or so

2905
01:50:44.530 --> 01:50:48.290
of deliberately putting your autonomic nervous system,

2906
01:50:48.290 --> 01:50:51.200
excuse me, into a state of very high autonomic arousal.

2907
01:50:51.200 --> 01:50:54.870
So this would involve doing 20 or 30 really deep breaths

2908
01:50:54.870 --> 01:50:58.907
in through the nose and then exhaling very intensely.

2909
01:50:58.907 --> 01:51:03.530
Normally our inhales are active and our exhales are passive.

2910
01:51:03.530 --> 01:51:06.170
In reptilian species it's the opposite,

2911
01:51:06.170 --> 01:51:07.270
it's kind of interesting.

2912
01:51:07.270 --> 01:51:09.170
Their exhales tend to be active

2913
01:51:09.170 --> 01:51:10.540
and their inhales tend to be passive,

2914
01:51:10.540 --> 01:51:12.850
but for humans, we tend to inhale

2915
01:51:12.850 --> 01:51:15.040
and then just let the air kind of drift out of us.

2916
01:51:15.040 --> 01:51:18.410
When you both inhale intensely and exhale intensely

2917
01:51:18.410 --> 01:51:21.530
20 or 30 times, you're going to quote unquote "heat up."

2918
01:51:21.530 --> 01:51:23.500
That's the release of adrenaline from your adrenals.

2919
01:51:23.500 --> 01:51:25.240
There's no question about that.

2920
01:51:25.240 --> 01:51:27.410
You could also get into a cold shower,

2921
01:51:27.410 --> 01:51:29.950
you could also do an ice bath, you could do submersion,

2922
01:51:29.950 --> 01:51:31.590
you could do any number of things

2923
01:51:31.590 --> 01:51:34.340
provided that it makes you uncomfortable.

2924
01:51:34.340 --> 01:51:36.280
What you're doing is you're tipping that seesaw

2925
01:51:36.280 --> 01:51:37.550
and you're doing it deliberately.

2926
01:51:37.550 --> 01:51:40.450
And then immediately after that 20 or 30 breaths,

2927
01:51:40.450 --> 01:51:42.030
you exhale all your air.

2928
01:51:42.030 --> 01:51:44.720
What I'm describing is classic tumor wim hof type breathing

2929
01:51:44.720 --> 01:51:45.860
to many people,

2930
01:51:45.860 --> 01:51:47.090
and you hold your breath and try

2931
01:51:47.090 --> 01:51:48.870
and maintain a state of mental calm

2932
01:51:48.870 --> 01:51:51.030
despite being flooded with adrenaline.

2933
01:51:51.030 --> 01:51:52.313
And then you repeat, and then you repeat again,

2934
01:51:52.313 --> 01:51:55.470
and maybe you do that three or four rounds.

2935
01:51:55.470 --> 01:51:58.590
Some people include big inhales with breath holds.

2936
01:51:58.590 --> 01:52:00.221
I should point out that none of this should be done

2937
01:52:00.221 --> 01:52:01.600
anywhere near water,

2938
01:52:01.600 --> 01:52:02.930
not even standing next to a puddle

2939
01:52:02.930 --> 01:52:06.800
because people have blacked out and drown in ice baths

2940
01:52:06.800 --> 01:52:08.570
and in oceans, and people have died

2941
01:52:08.570 --> 01:52:09.710
doing this type of thing,

2942
01:52:09.710 --> 01:52:12.260
because of the ability that you can't breathe underwater

2943
01:52:12.260 --> 01:52:14.280
and the shallow water blackout phenomenon.

2944
01:52:14.280 --> 01:52:16.810
So I wanna emphasize that people who have a high degree

2945
01:52:16.810 --> 01:52:20.050
of anxiety or who suffer from panic attacks

2946
01:52:20.050 --> 01:52:21.390
are not going to want to do this,

2947
01:52:21.390 --> 01:52:24.140
unless they're doing this with clinical support right there.

2948
01:52:24.140 --> 01:52:27.180
But for most people and for sake of high performance,

2949
01:52:27.180 --> 01:52:31.230
getting very comfortable in high levels of adrenaline

2950
01:52:31.230 --> 01:52:34.530
pumping through your system can be immensely useful,

2951
01:52:34.530 --> 01:52:36.970
such that when you get hit by something from the outside,

2952
01:52:36.970 --> 01:52:39.760
like the sudden appearance of an elevated heart rate

2953
01:52:39.760 --> 01:52:41.550
for reasons you don't understand,

2954
01:52:41.550 --> 01:52:45.127
you can sort of third person from that in recognizing,

2955
01:52:45.127 --> 01:52:46.730
"I've been in this state before."

2956
01:52:46.730 --> 01:52:48.060
It's sort of like when you learn how to drive,

2957
01:52:48.060 --> 01:52:49.310
you do it around the parking lot

2958
01:52:49.310 --> 01:52:50.550
and you do it in the local roads,

2959
01:52:50.550 --> 01:52:52.980
and then the first time you drive in really dense fog

2960
01:52:52.980 --> 01:52:54.950
on a mountain road, and you're scared for your life,

2961
01:52:54.950 --> 01:52:56.860
it's one reflector at a time.

2962
01:52:56.860 --> 01:52:59.340
You make it through that, the next time it's two reflectors

2963
01:52:59.340 --> 01:53:01.365
you're like, "Okay, don't like this could get rear ended

2964
01:53:01.365 --> 01:53:04.660
anytime could go off a cliff, but I've been here before."

2965
01:53:04.660 --> 01:53:07.086
Well, lot's useful practice.

2966
01:53:07.086 --> 01:53:08.504
I didn't mean to interrupt.

2967
01:53:08.504 --> 01:53:12.457
A lot of that has to do with the root of the anxiety

2968
01:53:12.457 --> 01:53:15.050
and often that's traced

2969
01:53:15.050 --> 01:53:17.940
to a relationship with control, right?

2970
01:53:17.940 --> 01:53:19.300
You wanna control the outcome.

2971
01:53:19.300 --> 01:53:21.750
If you're an athlete and you have a game, or a performance,

2972
01:53:21.750 --> 01:53:25.430
or whatever it is, it's that idea of,

2973
01:53:25.430 --> 01:53:27.030
I want this to go a certain way,

2974
01:53:27.030 --> 01:53:30.050
I only have control over some of these variables.

2975
01:53:30.050 --> 01:53:31.530
How am I gonna be perceived?

2976
01:53:31.530 --> 01:53:35.780
What happens if X, Y, or Z happens in an unexpected way?

2977
01:53:35.780 --> 01:53:38.520
Which is why I found visualization to be a powerful tool

2978
01:53:38.520 --> 01:53:41.020
and I've generally used that or deployed it

2979
01:53:41.020 --> 01:53:42.440
to visualize success,

2980
01:53:42.440 --> 01:53:45.630
like walking myself mentally through every step

2981
01:53:45.630 --> 01:53:46.780
of what's gonna happen

2982
01:53:46.780 --> 01:53:50.850
and kind of plotting how it's gonna turn out

2983
01:53:50.850 --> 01:53:51.860
in a favorable way.

2984
01:53:51.860 --> 01:53:54.830
But I know that you have some thoughts around doing that

2985
01:53:54.830 --> 01:53:56.480
in a failure context as well.

2986
01:53:56.480 --> 01:53:58.850
So you're getting a comprehensive view of all the things

2987
01:53:58.850 --> 01:53:59.683
that could happen,

2988
01:53:59.683 --> 01:54:02.780
which could then kind of calm yourself down

2989
01:54:02.780 --> 01:54:06.160
because you've considered it all.

2990
01:54:06.160 --> 01:54:06.993
Yeah.

2991
01:54:06.993 --> 01:54:10.710
So there's some interesting data, actually modeling data,

2992
01:54:10.710 --> 01:54:13.540
mean computational models of learning that's say,

2993
01:54:13.540 --> 01:54:17.030
what should the percentage of successful trials

2994
01:54:17.030 --> 01:54:18.160
versus failed trials be

2995
01:54:18.160 --> 01:54:19.335
when you're trying to learn something?

2996
01:54:19.335 --> 01:54:21.230
So this is a little bit different than high performance

2997
01:54:21.230 --> 01:54:24.860
in the context of competition or the onstage performance

2998
01:54:24.860 --> 01:54:27.697
where we're really talking about training at this point.

2999
01:54:27.697 --> 01:54:30.840
And there are a couple of interesting things about this.

3000
01:54:30.840 --> 01:54:32.100
First of all, the number

3001
01:54:32.100 --> 01:54:34.920
that falls out of this really nice paper is that

3002
01:54:36.240 --> 01:54:38.350
the best level of difficulty for something

3003
01:54:38.350 --> 01:54:42.800
should be approximately successful trials 85% of the time,

3004
01:54:42.800 --> 01:54:45.610
15% of trials should be failed trials.

3005
01:54:45.610 --> 01:54:47.700
And obviously this will vary by context,

3006
01:54:47.700 --> 01:54:48.860
if you're learning piano

3007
01:54:48.860 --> 01:54:51.260
versus if you're shooting on a range,

3008
01:54:51.260 --> 01:54:55.210
or different for cognitive tasks from math, et cetera.

3009
01:54:55.210 --> 01:54:58.600
But this 85/15 rule seems to work well

3010
01:54:58.600 --> 01:55:01.210
for the understanding of how the underlying neural networks

3011
01:55:01.210 --> 01:55:02.750
for learning work.

3012
01:55:02.750 --> 01:55:04.430
So that's hopefully we'll give people

3013
01:55:04.430 --> 01:55:06.950
a little bit of guidance in terms of structuring

3014
01:55:06.950 --> 01:55:08.500
their training or of their learning

3015
01:55:08.500 --> 01:55:10.550
in a way that it's appropriate levels of difficulty,

3016
01:55:10.550 --> 01:55:12.720
neither too difficult, nor too easy.

3017
01:55:12.720 --> 01:55:14.800
The other thing that's very clear is that

3018
01:55:15.900 --> 01:55:17.770
the process of neuroplasticity

3019
01:55:17.770 --> 01:55:20.500
as I mentioned, has two steps, major steps.

3020
01:55:20.500 --> 01:55:22.730
One is focus, and then one is deep rest.

3021
01:55:22.730 --> 01:55:25.040
Most of the rewiring and learning in your nervous system

3022
01:55:25.040 --> 01:55:26.820
is going to occur during deep sleep.

3023
01:55:26.820 --> 01:55:28.890
And so obviously try and get sufficient

3024
01:55:28.890 --> 01:55:31.630
amount of quality sleep, slow wave and REM sleep

3025
01:55:31.630 --> 01:55:33.290
as much as possible.

3026
01:55:33.290 --> 01:55:35.250
But there are two really nice papers

3027
01:55:35.250 --> 01:55:36.970
published in the journal Cell Reports,

3028
01:55:36.970 --> 01:55:39.040
the Cell Press journal, excellent journal,

3029
01:55:39.040 --> 01:55:43.520
which show that even brief 20 minute periods of resting

3030
01:55:43.520 --> 01:55:45.640
or napping after a learning bout

3031
01:55:45.640 --> 01:55:48.090
can greatly accelerate the neuroplasticity process.

3032
01:55:48.090 --> 01:55:50.460
So whether or not you're trying to learn a new motor skill,

3033
01:55:50.460 --> 01:55:54.130
whether or not you have just done an intense visualization,

3034
01:55:54.130 --> 01:55:55.190
which can be relaxing

3035
01:55:55.190 --> 01:55:58.570
but it's really a form of training and repetition,

3036
01:55:58.570 --> 01:56:00.490
whether or not you're learning math, or piano,

3037
01:56:00.490 --> 01:56:03.790
or whatever it is that in some period of time

3038
01:56:03.790 --> 01:56:06.320
within the immediate four hours after the learning bout

3039
01:56:06.320 --> 01:56:07.420
or the visualization,

3040
01:56:07.420 --> 01:56:10.880
to get 20 minutes of maybe a shallow nap,

3041
01:56:10.880 --> 01:56:12.910
you could do what we call non-sleep deep breath,

3042
01:56:12.910 --> 01:56:13.870
some people call it yoga nidra.

3043
01:56:13.870 --> 01:56:15.880
There are other forms of non-sleep deep breath,

3044
01:56:15.880 --> 01:56:19.060
essentially letting the brain go idle, closing your eyes.

3045
01:56:19.060 --> 01:56:21.430
I should say this is work done in humans,

3046
01:56:21.430 --> 01:56:24.060
it was done sensory, motor learning and complex sequences

3047
01:56:24.060 --> 01:56:25.830
of a kind of assign assess game,

3048
01:56:25.830 --> 01:56:28.280
which is hitting some people won't remember what that is,

3049
01:56:28.280 --> 01:56:30.166
but remembering the sequences of light

3050
01:56:30.166 --> 01:56:31.860
and it can be very easy

3051
01:56:31.860 --> 01:56:34.100
when there's only three or four things in that sequence

3052
01:56:34.100 --> 01:56:35.390
because you get up to 20 or 30,

3053
01:56:35.390 --> 01:56:37.000
that's subserous work.

3054
01:56:37.000 --> 01:56:40.180
The other thing that really favors neuroplasticity

3055
01:56:40.180 --> 01:56:41.560
or what are called gap effects.

3056
01:56:41.560 --> 01:56:45.550
And these date back more than a couple decades

3057
01:56:45.550 --> 01:56:48.150
and have been demonstrated in a huge variety of context

3058
01:56:48.150 --> 01:56:48.983
of learning.

3059
01:56:48.983 --> 01:56:51.740
But anytime you're learning something,

3060
01:56:51.740 --> 01:56:55.450
it pays to have random intervals in which you stop

3061
01:56:55.450 --> 01:56:56.750
and do nothing.

3062
01:56:56.750 --> 01:57:00.740
So let's use learning a sequence of the keys on the piano

3063
01:57:00.740 --> 01:57:02.120
just for sake of example.

3064
01:57:02.120 --> 01:57:03.300
You're practicing a scale.

3065
01:57:03.300 --> 01:57:04.980
You're practicing a piece music.

3066
01:57:04.980 --> 01:57:05.813
Every once in a while,

3067
01:57:05.813 --> 01:57:07.510
if you have a timer that goes off randomly,

3068
01:57:07.510 --> 01:57:09.410
or the instructor or teacher tells you stop,

3069
01:57:09.410 --> 01:57:11.190
and you attempt to do nothing,

3070
01:57:11.190 --> 01:57:12.890
you could close your eyes or keep them open

3071
01:57:12.890 --> 01:57:14.450
but you stop the motor pattern

3072
01:57:14.450 --> 01:57:16.020
or you stop trying to do the math problem,

3073
01:57:16.020 --> 01:57:18.090
or you stop trying to do the physical sequence

3074
01:57:18.090 --> 01:57:19.310
that you were doing.

3075
01:57:19.310 --> 01:57:21.030
Turns out that when we sleep,

3076
01:57:21.030 --> 01:57:24.560
there's a rapid replay of these sequences

3077
01:57:24.560 --> 01:57:27.580
of events during the day, but at much higher speed.

3078
01:57:27.580 --> 01:57:28.700
So for per unit time,

3079
01:57:28.700 --> 01:57:31.636
you're getting about 10 to 60 times the replay or repetition

3080
01:57:31.636 --> 01:57:33.650
of what you're doing during the day.

3081
01:57:33.650 --> 01:57:35.090
During these brief gaps,

3082
01:57:35.090 --> 01:57:38.520
these 10 second gaps that are introduced while awake,

3083
01:57:38.520 --> 01:57:40.400
during your learning,

3084
01:57:40.400 --> 01:57:43.900
you get a 10 to 20 times repeat

3085
01:57:43.900 --> 01:57:45.650
of whatever sequence you were trying to learn.

3086
01:57:45.650 --> 01:57:47.774
So this is a way of getting more repetitions in,

3087
01:57:47.774 --> 01:57:49.750
even though you're doing less.

3088
01:57:49.750 --> 01:57:52.950
And so these gap effects are fascinating.

3089
01:57:52.950 --> 01:57:54.770
They're being demonstrated now for more and more

3090
01:57:54.770 --> 01:57:55.870
types of learning.

3091
01:57:55.870 --> 01:57:58.020
And fortunately the more recent papers

3092
01:57:58.020 --> 01:57:59.470
really point to the neural mechanisms,

3093
01:57:59.470 --> 01:58:01.220
it's the hippocampus and the neocortex

3094
01:58:01.220 --> 01:58:04.260
areas involved in storage and retrieval of memories

3095
01:58:04.260 --> 01:58:07.650
that are rapidly replaying these sequences.

3096
01:58:07.650 --> 01:58:09.900
We talked about how one state defines the next.

3097
01:58:09.900 --> 01:58:11.840
So when you're repeating something over and over and over,

3098
01:58:11.840 --> 01:58:13.080
you stop the nervous system

3099
01:58:13.080 --> 01:58:14.480
doesn't just switch to what you're doing,

3100
01:58:14.480 --> 01:58:16.240
it keeps generating that sequence.

3101
01:58:16.240 --> 01:58:18.320
And when you suddenly limit your motor behavior,

3102
01:58:18.320 --> 01:58:19.870
your cognitive behavior,

3103
01:58:19.870 --> 01:58:21.700
so you're maybe reciting lines to remember

3104
01:58:21.700 --> 01:58:22.533
and all of a sudden you just stop

3105
01:58:22.533 --> 01:58:24.920
and you attempt to stop doing that,

3106
01:58:24.920 --> 01:58:26.200
your mind keeps going

3107
01:58:26.200 --> 01:58:27.940
and that accelerates the number of repetitions

3108
01:58:27.940 --> 01:58:30.070
and then you return to the practice.

3109
01:58:30.070 --> 01:58:32.510
Why has that not been incorporated

3110
01:58:32.510 --> 01:58:34.003
into our educational system?

3111
01:58:34.003 --> 01:58:35.400
I mean, that's crazy.

3112
01:58:35.400 --> 01:58:38.840
That seems like such a turbo blast

3113
01:58:38.840 --> 01:58:41.715
in our ability to synthesize information

3114
01:58:41.715 --> 01:58:43.720
that it should be part and parcel

3115
01:58:43.720 --> 01:58:48.030
of how we are approaching our curriculums.

3116
01:58:48.030 --> 01:58:48.863
Well, first of all,

3117
01:58:48.863 --> 01:58:50.150
I have two responses to that.

3118
01:58:50.150 --> 01:58:50.983
First of all,

3119
01:58:50.983 --> 01:58:52.770
I've made some attempt to put that out there.

3120
01:58:53.830 --> 01:58:55.700
There's a free lecture that I did

3121
01:58:55.700 --> 01:58:57.093
thanks to the support of Logitech.

3122
01:58:57.093 --> 01:58:58.380
There's a free online lecture

3123
01:58:58.380 --> 01:58:59.860
that was specifically for teachers

3124
01:58:59.860 --> 01:59:03.060
and some steps to gaining more rapid neuroplasticity.

3125
01:59:03.060 --> 01:59:04.580
Because I think teachers are hungry

3126
01:59:04.580 --> 01:59:06.670
for better ways to teach their students.

3127
01:59:06.670 --> 01:59:08.939
I think I can speculate

3128
01:59:08.939 --> 01:59:11.020
having been in the field of neuroscience

3129
01:59:11.020 --> 01:59:12.330
for a long while now

3130
01:59:12.330 --> 01:59:15.470
that there was so much excitement about neuroplasticity

3131
01:59:15.470 --> 01:59:18.870
and BDNF, or neuroplasticity and nerve growth factor.

3132
01:59:18.870 --> 01:59:22.820
All these frankly, hard to access mechanisms

3133
01:59:22.820 --> 01:59:23.756
that people were thinking,

3134
01:59:23.756 --> 01:59:25.160
what can I eat?

3135
01:59:25.160 --> 01:59:27.170
What can I do in order to enhance my learning?

3136
01:59:27.170 --> 01:59:29.250
What's gonna trigger BDNF release?

3137
01:59:29.250 --> 01:59:31.430
When in fact the process of neuroplasticity

3138
01:59:31.430 --> 01:59:33.730
is built into our childhood and how we developed

3139
01:59:33.730 --> 01:59:35.130
for better or for worse, right?

3140
01:59:35.130 --> 01:59:36.900
Hence the discussion about trauma.

3141
01:59:36.900 --> 01:59:40.730
But there are a couple elements to neuroplasticity

3142
01:59:40.730 --> 01:59:43.280
that we now know require focus and then rest.

3143
01:59:43.280 --> 01:59:46.060
So the number one support for learning

3144
01:59:46.060 --> 01:59:48.600
is gonna be deep sleep, regular deep sleep.

3145
01:59:48.600 --> 01:59:51.510
So if kids wanna learn better, adults wanna learn better

3146
01:59:51.510 --> 01:59:52.930
that's the thing to focus on first.

3147
01:59:52.930 --> 01:59:54.227
That's really the anchor to everything.

3148
01:59:54.227 --> 01:59:55.180
And we know this.

3149
01:59:55.180 --> 01:59:58.800
And for people that feel anxiety,

3150
01:59:58.800 --> 02:00:00.860
hearing this realize that you are resilient,

3151
02:00:00.860 --> 02:00:02.540
you can have a poor night's, but I would say,

3152
02:00:02.540 --> 02:00:05.200
try and get great sleep about 80% of the time.

3153
02:00:05.200 --> 02:00:06.627
But that sleep is really the thing to focus on

3154
02:00:06.627 --> 02:00:09.950
until it's in place to get everything else to work well.

3155
02:00:09.950 --> 02:00:11.820
But these other things like gap learning effects,

3156
02:00:11.820 --> 02:00:13.480
they've been in the literature,

3157
02:00:13.480 --> 02:00:16.260
but they've never actually been translated into protocols

3158
02:00:16.260 --> 02:00:17.110
for the real world.

3159
02:00:17.110 --> 02:00:19.230
And so the neuroscience field

3160
02:00:19.230 --> 02:00:21.475
is sort of guilty of placing things in language and context

3161
02:00:21.475 --> 02:00:23.540
that's been really hard to access,

3162
02:00:23.540 --> 02:00:26.870
and kind of there have been people who have attempted

3163
02:00:26.870 --> 02:00:29.280
to unvault this stuff.

3164
02:00:29.280 --> 02:00:31.530
People like Mike Mesnick from UCSF,

3165
02:00:31.530 --> 02:00:33.780
John Brady from Harvard Medical School.

3166
02:00:33.780 --> 02:00:35.451
But I think in the '80s and '90s

3167
02:00:35.451 --> 02:00:38.050
and early 2000s, the world wasn't really ready for it.

3168
02:00:38.050 --> 02:00:41.380
And here's the case where social media wasn't there,

3169
02:00:41.380 --> 02:00:43.200
there weren't a lot of online learning platform

3170
02:00:43.200 --> 02:00:44.860
for people to hear about this stuff.

3171
02:00:44.860 --> 02:00:45.960
And I wanna be very clear.

3172
02:00:45.960 --> 02:00:48.050
I have nothing to do with this specific research.

3173
02:00:48.050 --> 02:00:51.040
I am the academic descendant of some people

3174
02:00:51.040 --> 02:00:52.900
who did very well in their discovery

3175
02:00:52.900 --> 02:00:54.760
of critical periods of neuroplasticity,

3176
02:00:54.760 --> 02:00:56.240
but this isn't my work,

3177
02:00:56.240 --> 02:01:00.283
but I am absolutely overwhelmingly enthusiastic

3178
02:01:02.420 --> 02:01:04.917
about this work, 'cause I like we can all benefit from it.

3179
02:01:04.917 --> 02:01:06.770
And this is absolutely free.

3180
02:01:06.770 --> 02:01:08.130
There's no product to purchase.

3181
02:01:08.130 --> 02:01:11.100
There's no thing to do except to introduce these short gaps.

3182
02:01:11.100 --> 02:01:15.410
And then after learning to take a 20 minute nap if you can,

3183
02:01:15.410 --> 02:01:18.130
or just try and shut off the amount of sensory input

3184
02:01:18.130 --> 02:01:18.963
coming in.

3185
02:01:18.963 --> 02:01:20.320
And again, that doesn't have to happen immediately

3186
02:01:20.320 --> 02:01:21.250
after the learning,

3187
02:01:21.250 --> 02:01:23.100
but at some period in the next four hours

3188
02:01:23.100 --> 02:01:24.140
after the learning,

3189
02:01:24.140 --> 02:01:28.710
because we know that recall, persistence of the memory

3190
02:01:28.710 --> 02:01:30.680
of what you learned and the ability to execute

3191
02:01:30.680 --> 02:01:32.850
these motor sequences just goes way up.

3192
02:01:32.850 --> 02:01:33.860
Yeah.

3193
02:01:33.860 --> 02:01:37.030
It's so crazy how we've historically gotten it backwards.

3194
02:01:37.030 --> 02:01:39.280
Like whether you're a med student, or a college student,

3195
02:01:39.280 --> 02:01:41.290
or law school, or whatever it is.

3196
02:01:41.290 --> 02:01:44.260
It's all about cramming, staying up all night,

3197
02:01:44.260 --> 02:01:46.920
exhausting yourself, showing up for the test,

3198
02:01:46.920 --> 02:01:49.810
doing the dump and then crashing for a couple days,

3199
02:01:49.810 --> 02:01:52.280
which is completely antithetical

3200
02:01:52.280 --> 02:01:54.500
to maximizing your performance.

3201
02:01:54.500 --> 02:01:55.890
Yeah.

3202
02:01:55.890 --> 02:01:58.030
Unfortunately Stanford understands the biology

3203
02:01:58.030 --> 02:01:59.780
and so they don't do this at Stanford Medicine

3204
02:01:59.780 --> 02:02:01.270
or that in medical school,

3205
02:02:01.270 --> 02:02:03.002
many places students are sleep deprived

3206
02:02:03.002 --> 02:02:04.404
and the residents on call.

3207
02:02:04.404 --> 02:02:07.210
I mean, we all know that I'm gonna get in trouble

3208
02:02:07.210 --> 02:02:09.217
for saying this with the medical community,

3209
02:02:09.217 --> 02:02:10.080
but I'll say it.

3210
02:02:10.080 --> 02:02:11.840
If you end up in the hospital in the middle of the night,

3211
02:02:11.840 --> 02:02:15.150
you should ask how long the physician has been there.

3212
02:02:15.150 --> 02:02:17.500
I mean, fatal mistakes have been made.

3213
02:02:17.500 --> 02:02:18.349
Right. So yeah.

3214
02:02:18.349 --> 02:02:20.879
(both chattering)

3215
02:02:20.879 --> 02:02:24.110
The people that we turn over complete control

3216
02:02:24.110 --> 02:02:25.320
of our lives over to,

3217
02:02:25.320 --> 02:02:28.193
yeah, air traffic controllers, pilots, surgeons,

3218
02:02:29.633 --> 02:02:32.360
people that are on these crazy shifts.

3219
02:02:32.360 --> 02:02:33.520
Yeah, exactly.

3220
02:02:33.520 --> 02:02:34.353
It makes no sense.

3221
02:02:34.353 --> 02:02:35.186
It makes no sense.

3222
02:02:35.186 --> 02:02:38.580
And the consequences are many and they're severe.

3223
02:02:38.580 --> 02:02:39.810
I mean, they really can be severe.

3224
02:02:39.810 --> 02:02:41.210
I don't know what it's like in law school,

3225
02:02:41.210 --> 02:02:42.900
but I wanna make sure that any lawyer I hire

3226
02:02:42.900 --> 02:02:44.870
has actually been rested for the learning of the material.

3227
02:02:44.870 --> 02:02:47.079
Who knows what the lawyers are doing?

3228
02:02:47.079 --> 02:02:48.529
I certainly, don't.

3229
02:02:48.529 --> 02:02:51.170
Not a lot of sleep in law school.

3230
02:02:51.170 --> 02:02:52.680
All right, well, we gotta round this out,

3231
02:02:52.680 --> 02:02:54.320
but maybe let's close it down

3232
02:02:54.320 --> 02:02:58.937
with a recap or a supplementation of some of the practices

3233
02:02:58.937 --> 02:03:01.505
that people who are listening or watching

3234
02:03:01.505 --> 02:03:03.336
can begin to incorporate into their lives.

3235
02:03:03.336 --> 02:03:07.170
We talked about some of the breathwork practices,

3236
02:03:07.170 --> 02:03:10.432
exposure to light, sleep, we've talked about.

3237
02:03:10.432 --> 02:03:12.398
We don't need to go super into nutrition,

3238
02:03:12.398 --> 02:03:14.170
although that's super important,

3239
02:03:14.170 --> 02:03:16.320
but maybe just a few things

3240
02:03:17.400 --> 02:03:20.500
that people can start thinking about and practicing.

3241
02:03:20.500 --> 02:03:22.357
Yeah, I think the most important things

3242
02:03:22.357 --> 02:03:23.890
are the foundational practices,

3243
02:03:23.890 --> 02:03:25.110
which are get your sleep right.

3244
02:03:25.110 --> 02:03:26.300
Best way to get your sleep right

3245
02:03:26.300 --> 02:03:28.980
is get your morning sunlight exposure,

3246
02:03:28.980 --> 02:03:31.390
ideally within an hour of waking up.

3247
02:03:31.390 --> 02:03:33.390
Again, if you wake up before the sun comes out,

3248
02:03:33.390 --> 02:03:36.680
flip on as many lights as you possibly can,

3249
02:03:36.680 --> 02:03:38.443
if you want to be awake, right?

3250
02:03:40.121 --> 02:03:42.210
That's one basic way

3251
02:03:42.210 --> 02:03:44.510
to just set the whole thing in motion properly.

3252
02:03:44.510 --> 02:03:46.240
I think everyone should have a real time tool

3253
02:03:46.240 --> 02:03:48.290
for dealing with stress, the physiological sighs,

3254
02:03:48.290 --> 02:03:49.750
the best one that I'm aware of.

3255
02:03:49.750 --> 02:03:52.250
And it's grounded in close to a hundred years

3256
02:03:52.250 --> 02:03:54.320
in physiology and excellent work

3257
02:03:54.320 --> 02:03:56.900
in a variety of laboratories.

3258
02:03:56.900 --> 02:03:58.560
I think that people should also

3259
02:03:58.560 --> 02:04:01.000
have a way of increasing their stress threshold.

3260
02:04:01.000 --> 02:04:03.940
And there's a lot made nowadays of ice baths

3261
02:04:03.940 --> 02:04:06.200
and cold showers for dopamine release,

3262
02:04:06.200 --> 02:04:08.080
and there is evidence for that, believe it or not.

3263
02:04:08.080 --> 02:04:10.760
Extended dopamine release from cold showers,

3264
02:04:10.760 --> 02:04:12.923
or ice baths, or just cold immersion.

3265
02:04:14.200 --> 02:04:15.530
That can be very useful.

3266
02:04:15.530 --> 02:04:16.840
I also think it's just very useful

3267
02:04:16.840 --> 02:04:18.880
to have some sort of breathing practice

3268
02:04:18.880 --> 02:04:20.470
or cold water exposure practice

3269
02:04:20.470 --> 02:04:22.870
that you do maybe three to seven times a week,

3270
02:04:22.870 --> 02:04:24.400
depending on how aggressive you are

3271
02:04:24.400 --> 02:04:25.860
to raise your stress threshold

3272
02:04:25.860 --> 02:04:28.830
so that you can manage under conditions of high adrenaline.

3273
02:04:28.830 --> 02:04:30.740
And your minimum effective dose

3274
02:04:30.740 --> 02:04:32.930
for that is pretty nominal.

3275
02:04:32.930 --> 02:04:33.763
Yeah.

3276
02:04:33.763 --> 02:04:35.970
So there was a beautiful study published,

3277
02:04:35.970 --> 02:04:37.567
the first author is Dr. Susanna Sobad,

3278
02:04:37.567 --> 02:04:38.930
this was a study done

3279
02:04:38.930 --> 02:04:41.190
at Scandinavia published in Cell Reports Medicine.

3280
02:04:41.190 --> 02:04:42.630
I've spent a lot of time with this study.

3281
02:04:42.630 --> 02:04:44.920
It was mainly focused on the metabolic effects

3282
02:04:44.920 --> 02:04:46.230
of cold exposure.

3283
02:04:46.230 --> 02:04:47.680
Showing that if you want to be comfortable

3284
02:04:47.680 --> 02:04:48.540
in cold environments,

3285
02:04:48.540 --> 02:04:49.373
the best thing you can do

3286
02:04:49.373 --> 02:04:51.030
is get into a very cold environment

3287
02:04:51.030 --> 02:04:54.450
for about 11 minutes total per week.

3288
02:04:54.450 --> 02:04:55.784
They also saw metabolic increases.

3289
02:04:55.784 --> 02:04:58.780
You also see increases in resilience,

3290
02:04:58.780 --> 02:05:00.480
which is basically translate to an ability

3291
02:05:00.480 --> 02:05:03.420
to stay mentally calm when you have a lot of adrenaline.

3292
02:05:03.420 --> 02:05:04.710
You can do this with cold showers.

3293
02:05:04.710 --> 02:05:06.730
You can do this with immersion up to the neck.

3294
02:05:06.730 --> 02:05:08.690
A lot of people say, which is better?

3295
02:05:08.690 --> 02:05:11.040
Immersion is better, mostly because it's been studied more.

3296
02:05:11.040 --> 02:05:12.810
It's hard to do studies on showers

3297
02:05:12.810 --> 02:05:15.100
because people don't always stand in the same depth.

3298
02:05:15.100 --> 02:05:17.390
The shower has immersion is one way, et cetera.

3299
02:05:17.390 --> 02:05:18.850
If you don't have access to either of those,

3300
02:05:18.850 --> 02:05:21.650
yes, you could go outside in cold conditions,

3301
02:05:21.650 --> 02:05:24.130
but the heat transfer in water is much greater.

3302
02:05:24.130 --> 02:05:28.920
So 11 minutes total translates to two or three minutes

3303
02:05:28.920 --> 02:05:30.320
as many times a week as needed

3304
02:05:30.320 --> 02:05:32.660
to hit about that 11 minute threshold,

3305
02:05:32.660 --> 02:05:35.790
is really going to improve your brown a post tissue stores.

3306
02:05:35.790 --> 02:05:37.980
These are tissue stores that generate heats

3307
02:05:37.980 --> 02:05:39.160
kinda like a furnace in your body,

3308
02:05:39.160 --> 02:05:40.260
increase metabolism,

3309
02:05:40.260 --> 02:05:41.753
increase this thing we're calling resilience

3310
02:05:41.753 --> 02:05:43.140
or stress tolerance.

3311
02:05:43.140 --> 02:05:44.723
So 11 minutes per week.

3312
02:05:44.723 --> 02:05:47.270
A lot of data also about sauna, et cetera.

3313
02:05:47.270 --> 02:05:48.510
But I'll just say,

3314
02:05:48.510 --> 02:05:50.927
if you are somebody doing cold exposure, people say,

3315
02:05:50.927 --> 02:05:53.040
"Can I end with a warm shower or hot shower?"

3316
02:05:53.040 --> 02:05:55.630
That's what I do, 'cause I like it.

3317
02:05:55.630 --> 02:05:56.463
You're not supposed to do that.

3318
02:05:56.463 --> 02:05:58.320
If you want to increase your metabolism,

3319
02:05:58.320 --> 02:06:01.660
you will end with cold so that you have to continue

3320
02:06:01.660 --> 02:06:03.950
to use your bodily functions,

3321
02:06:03.950 --> 02:06:06.510
your metabolism more in order to reheat.

3322
02:06:06.510 --> 02:06:08.200
Shivering is actually a good thing.

3323
02:06:08.200 --> 02:06:09.750
There's evidence that shivering from the muscle

3324
02:06:09.750 --> 02:06:11.510
can release something called suckeny,

3325
02:06:11.510 --> 02:06:15.040
which triggers the more brown fat accumulation

3326
02:06:15.040 --> 02:06:16.720
which is not the blubbery fat

3327
02:06:16.720 --> 02:06:18.130
that most people wanna get rid of,

3328
02:06:18.130 --> 02:06:20.200
but is the fat that generates this heat in the body.

3329
02:06:20.200 --> 02:06:21.660
So those are good practices to have.

3330
02:06:21.660 --> 02:06:24.340
So 11 minutes of cold exposure total per week

3331
02:06:24.340 --> 02:06:25.930
divided into several sessions.

3332
02:06:25.930 --> 02:06:27.960
But if it ends up being six minutes and five minutes,

3333
02:06:27.960 --> 02:06:28.793
that's fine too.

3334
02:06:28.793 --> 02:06:31.170
I imagine it's never really been looked at

3335
02:06:31.170 --> 02:06:33.840
and I can only imagine having talked to Susan Sobad

3336
02:06:33.840 --> 02:06:36.160
that that would be good as well.

3337
02:06:36.160 --> 02:06:37.440
So have a real time tool,

3338
02:06:37.440 --> 02:06:41.170
have a tool to increase resilience or stress tolerance.

3339
02:06:41.170 --> 02:06:44.450
Then also try and limit your light exposure in the evening.

3340
02:06:44.450 --> 02:06:46.680
Try and dim those lights, bring them low in the environment,

3341
02:06:46.680 --> 02:06:49.770
try and make the room cool, so you can sleep well.

3342
02:06:49.770 --> 02:06:51.110
Pile on the blankets if you want.

3343
02:06:51.110 --> 02:06:52.230
That doesn't mean being cold,

3344
02:06:52.230 --> 02:06:53.117
it means having your environment be cold

3345
02:06:53.117 --> 02:06:55.160
so you can toss off the blankets as needed

3346
02:06:55.160 --> 02:06:56.950
and get deep sleep.

3347
02:06:56.950 --> 02:06:58.790
The other things I think could be very useful

3348
02:06:58.790 --> 02:07:03.130
is to think about this three view of states,

3349
02:07:03.130 --> 02:07:04.120
both in sleep and awake,

3350
02:07:04.120 --> 02:07:07.340
that from zero to eight or nine hours after waking,

3351
02:07:07.340 --> 02:07:09.890
that's going to be high energy, high output,

3352
02:07:09.890 --> 02:07:12.810
high adrenaline on just naturally high cortisol.

3353
02:07:12.810 --> 02:07:15.267
So linear type of operations that people always say,

3354
02:07:15.267 --> 02:07:16.610
"What's your morning routine?

3355
02:07:16.610 --> 02:07:18.610
Do you do the most difficult thing early in the day?"

3356
02:07:18.610 --> 02:07:21.850
Not necessarily, sometimes if you do the most boring,

3357
02:07:21.850 --> 02:07:22.683
terrible thing in day,

3358
02:07:22.683 --> 02:07:25.190
which for me would be like an Excel spreadsheet.

3359
02:07:25.190 --> 02:07:26.023
For me is just,

3360
02:07:26.023 --> 02:07:28.200
I'd rather get hives than do an Excel spreadsheet,

3361
02:07:28.200 --> 02:07:30.100
but the most difficult thing might be the creative work

3362
02:07:30.100 --> 02:07:32.110
which would fall, or brainstorming work,

3363
02:07:32.110 --> 02:07:34.720
or what we would call non-linear type thing,

3364
02:07:34.720 --> 02:07:37.110
taking existing elements but arranging them in new ways,

3365
02:07:37.110 --> 02:07:40.190
are probably best done for most people

3366
02:07:40.190 --> 02:07:42.300
in the second phase of the day.

3367
02:07:42.300 --> 02:07:45.680
And then of course from 17 to 24 hours after waking,

3368
02:07:45.680 --> 02:07:47.090
you wanna be asleep.

3369
02:07:47.090 --> 02:07:48.700
Naps and deep rest are terrific.

3370
02:07:48.700 --> 02:07:50.900
Limit them to 90 minutes or less,

3371
02:07:50.900 --> 02:07:53.720
and don't do them at all if they inhibit your ability

3372
02:07:53.720 --> 02:07:54.640
to sleep at night.

3373
02:07:54.640 --> 02:07:56.620
And then in terms of additional resources,

3374
02:07:56.620 --> 02:08:00.000
or tools I should say that the gap effects introduced

3375
02:08:00.000 --> 02:08:01.810
during learning at random.

3376
02:08:01.810 --> 02:08:05.150
So 10 second gaps introduced at random,

3377
02:08:05.150 --> 02:08:06.820
what the proper ratio is.

3378
02:08:06.820 --> 02:08:09.960
So let's say you're doing an hour long training bout,

3379
02:08:09.960 --> 02:08:11.910
you might introduce a kind of a 10 to one.

3380
02:08:11.910 --> 02:08:15.070
So for every 10 minutes, excuse me,

3381
02:08:15.070 --> 02:08:16.260
for every 10 minutes of training,

3382
02:08:16.260 --> 02:08:18.180
you might introduce one of these gaps.

3383
02:08:18.180 --> 02:08:19.350
You could do them more frequently.

3384
02:08:19.350 --> 02:08:20.570
It really hasn't been explored

3385
02:08:20.570 --> 02:08:22.210
how often to introduce these gaps,

3386
02:08:22.210 --> 02:08:24.270
but they should arrive more or less at random.

3387
02:08:24.270 --> 02:08:27.510
Is that a situation like, for example,

3388
02:08:27.510 --> 02:08:29.210
you're reading a textbook, you're a student.

3389
02:08:29.210 --> 02:08:32.340
So you read a certain section for 10 minutes

3390
